  2024,Vertex Algebras Course 5-6,"https://people.maths.ox.ac.uk/joyce/VertexAlgebras2021/VA5+6.pdf",ac.uk,math.QA,math.QA hep-th mathematics physics vertex-algebras lecture-notes,null,null,null,"Vertex Algebras Course 5-6. Lattice vertex algebras and the Monster vertex algebra $V^\\natural$."
  2025,Vertex Algebras Course 7-8,"https://people.maths.ox.ac.uk/joyce/VertexAlgebras2021/VA7+8.pdf",ac.uk,math.QA,math.QA hep-th math.AG math.RT mathematics physics vertex-algebras enumerative-geometry lie-theory lecture-notes,null,null,null,Vertex Algebras Course 7-8. Wall-crossing structures and vertex Lie algebras in Donaldson–Thomas
  2026,Eran Assaf,"https://math.dartmouth.edu/~lesen/notes/General_Shimura_Varieties_talk.pdf",dartmouth.edu,math.AG,math.AG math.NT math.CT mathematics algebraic-geometry shimura-varieties category-theory motives survey,null,null,null,"Eran Assaf, General Shimura Varieties. Introduction to the category of abelian motives. Classification of Shimura varieties based on abelian type."
  2027,Ben Breen,"https://math.dartmouth.edu/~lesen/notes/talk_slides_Aug17.pdf",dartmouth.edu,math.AG,math.AG math.NT mathematics algebraic-geometry shimura-varieties survey,null,null,null,"Ben Breen, Shimura Varieties. Algebras with involutions. Hermitian forms. Introduction to Shimura datum."
  2028,Ben Breen,"https://math.dartmouth.edu/~lesen/notes/ShimuraVarietiesCMBen.pdf",dartmouth.edu,math.AG,math.AG math.NT mathematics algebraic-geometry shimura-varieties elliptic-curves survey,null,null,null,"Ben Breen, Complex Multiplication in Shimura Varieties. Introduction to the main theorem of Shimura varieties."
  2029,Avi Kulkarni,"https://math.dartmouth.edu/~lesen/notes/talk_slides_May29.pdf",dartmouth.edu,math.AG,math.AG math.NT mathematics algebraic-geometry shimura-varieties elliptic-curves survey,null,null,null,"Avi Kulkarni, PEL Shimura Varieties. Introduction to PEL Shimura Varieties. Introduction to PEL Shimura datum. Endomorphisms of elliptic curves."
  2030,Avi Kulkarni,"https://math.dartmouth.edu/~lesen/notes/talk_slides_Aug17.pdf",dartmouth.edu,math.NT,math.NT math.RA math.AG math.GR mathematics abstract-algebra elliptic-curves galois-theory,null,null,null,"Avi Kulkarni, Uniqueness of Canonical Models. Shimura reciprocity and relation to complex multiplication. Transcendental Galois theory."
  2031,Jeff Cheeger,"http://numr.wdfiles.com/local--files/differential-cohomology/cheeger-simons.pdf",wdfiles.com,math.AT,math.AT math.AG hep-th math.DG mathematics physics differential-cohomology sheaf-theory,null,null,null,"Jeff Cheeger, James Simons, Differential characters and geometric invariants. A classical non-sheaf theoretic approach to differential cohomology."
  2032,Urs Schreiber,"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1310.7930.pdf",arxiv.org,math.AG,math.AG math.AT hep-th math.CT mathematics physics differential-cohomology stacks topos-theory arxiv,null,null,"1310.7930","Urs Schreiber, Differential cohomology in a cohesive infinity-topos. Higher stack model of differential cohomology (similar to Brylinski's gerbes, but generalized to higher dimensions)."
  2033,Ulrich Bunke,"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1208.3961",arxiv.org,math.AT,math.AT math.DG mathematics differential-cohomology algebraic-topology characteristic-classes homotopy-theory lecture-notes survey slides arxiv,null,null,"1208.3961","Ulrich Bunke, Differential cohomology. These course note first provide an introduction to secondary characteristic classes and differential cohomology. They continue with a presentation of a stable homotopy theoretic approach to the theory of differential extensions of generalized cohomology theories including products and Umkehr maps."
  2034,Hisham Sati,"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1605.03444.pdf",arxiv.org,math.AT,math.AT math.CT hep-th math.DG mathematics physics differential-cohomology homological-algebra arxiv,null,null,"1605.03444","Hisham Sati, Spectral sequences in smooth generalized cohomology. Brief summary of Bunke's model for differential cohomology theories."
  2035,Juvitop Seminar for differential cohomology,"https://math.mit.edu/juvitop/pastseminars/2019_Fall.html",mit.edu,math.AT,math.AT hep-th math.DG mathematics physics differential-cohomology,null,null,null,"Juvitop Seminar for differential cohomology. In Fall 2019, Juvitop was about Differential Cohomology. This seminar was organized by Araminta Amabel and Peter Haine."
  2036,Yi Yu Zhang,"https://math.mit.edu/juvitop/pastseminars/notes_2019_Fall/Park.pdf",mit.edu,math.AT,math.AT math.DG math.AG math.FA mathematics characteristic-classes differential-geometry intersection-theory nonstandard-analysis survey slides,null,null,null,"Yi Yu Zhang, Introduction to equivariant cohomology and the equivariant first Chern class. The goal of this paper to provide a relatively accessible and integrated introduction to the algebraic topology of spaces with Lie group actions, in both the smooth and the holomorphic category. We present detailed treatment of the basic constructions in equivariant de Rham theory and Dolbeault theory. We also discuss equivariant connections and curvature on vector bundles equipped with infinitesimal lifts, as well as the equivariant first Chern class of line bundles with inﬁnitesimal lifts. A novel feature of this presentation is the deﬁnition of infinitesimal lift via differential operators on the vector bundles."
  2037,Wilsonian renormalisation Perimeter Institute slides,"https://pdf.pirsa.org/files/08040019.pdf",pirsa.org,hep-th,hep-th math-ph physics quantum-field-theory fixed-point-theorems statistical-mechanics slides,null,null,null,"Wilsonian renormalisation Perimeter Institute slides, blocking dynamics and linearization onto IR fixed points giving universal critical exponents. Ferromagnet example. Proof that nonperturbative renormalisable theory in 4D is trivial."
  2038,Arthur-César Le Bras,"https://www.ihes.fr/~abbes/SGA/Slides_Lebras.pdf",ihes.fr,math.AG,math.AG math.NT mathematics p-adic-hodge-theory abelian-varieties prismatic-cohomology perfectoid-spaces,null,null,null,"Arthur-César Le Bras, prismatic Dieudonné theory classifies $p$-divisible groups via filtered prismatic Dieudonné crystals. Key computation: specializing to perfectoid rings recovers the Scholze–Weinstein classification via minuscule Breuil–Kisin–Fargues modules (where the filtration is redundant); specializing to characteristic $p$ recovers Berthelot–Breen–Messing crystalline Dieudonné theory and de Jong's/Lau's classification; and evaluating on $(\\mathfrak{S}, (E))$ for $R = \\mathcal{O}_K$ recovers Breuil–Kisin classification of $p$-divisible groups."
  2039,Jeffrey Lambie-Hanson (joint with Chris Lambie-Hanson),"https://www.mv.helsinki.fi/home/jvaanane/arctic/2025/Slides/lambie-hanson_arctic25.pdf",helsinki.fi,math.AG,math.AG math.AT math.CO math.CT mathematics algebraic-topology condensed-mathematics combinatorics mathematical-logic talk,null,null,null,"Jeffrey Lambie-Hanson (joint with Chris Lambie-Hanson), infinitary combinatorics in condensed mathematics and strong homology. Key computation: for $X^{n,\\lambda}$ the one-point compactification of a disjoint sum of $\\lambda$ copies of the open $n$-ball, $\\bar H_p(\\bigvee_\\omega X^{n,\\lambda})=\\bigoplus_\\omega \\bar H_p(X^{n,\\lambda})$ iff $\\lim^{n-p} A^{\\omega}_\\lambda=0$, where $A^\\kappa_\\lambda=\\langle\\prod_{I(f)}\\mathbb Z\\rangle$ is the inverse system attached to $f:\\kappa\\to[\\lambda]^{<\\omega}$, generalizing Mardešić–Prasolov's criterion; forcing with Hechler or $\\beth_\\omega$-many Laver reals kills all $\\lim^n A[H]$ (yielding $2^{\\aleph_0}>\\aleph_\\omega$ via Casarosa–Lambie-Hanson), making strong homology additive on locally compact separable metric spaces and embedding separable pro-abelian groups fully faithfully in $\\mathrm{Cond}(\\mathrm{Ab})$; in ZFC the uncountable earring space and $\\prod_\\omega\\prod_{\\omega_1}\\mathbb{Z}$ give counterexamples, so nonseparable condensed behavior is provably bad."
  2040,Guozhen Wang,"https://math.mit.edu/topology/notes/wang-slides.pdf",mit.edu,math.CT,math.CT math.AG math.AT math.NT mathematics homological-algebra descent-theory topological-hochschild-homology abelian-varieties,null,null,null,"Guozhen Wang, topological cyclic homology of local fields via a descent spectral sequence built from a prismatic/Dieudonné-type $\\delta$-ring structure. Key computation: for a local field $K$ with ring of integers $O_K$ and uniformizer $\\varpi$, computes relative $THH(O_K/S\\langle z\\rangle)$ for $S\\langle z\\rangle=\\mathbb{Z}_p[z]$, $z\\mapsto\\varpi$; the filtration by the fiber of $\\mathrm{can}-\\varphi$ gives $E_1^{i,j}\\Rightarrow TC_{i+j}(O_K)$, and the $E_2$-term of the descent spectral sequence for $TP(O_K)$ is computed as $\\mathrm{Ext}$ of the Hopf algebroid $(TP_0(O_K/S[z_0,z_1]), TP_0(O_K/S[z_0,z_0,z_1]))$, with $TP_0(O_K/S[z_0,z_1])$ the Nygaard-completion of a $\\delta$-ring generated by $h=\\varphi(E)^{-1}\\sigma$-type elements."
  2041,Albert Jinghui Yang,"https://kclo3-naoh.github.io/files/TC_of_local_fields.pdf",github.io,math.AT,math.AT math.CT math.KT math.RA mathematics topological-hochschild-homology homological-algebra descent-theory algebraic-topology github,null,null,null,"Albert Jinghui Yang, topological cyclic homology of local fields via secondary spectral sequences on the Nygaard filtration. Key computation: the descent spectral sequence for $TC(O_K)$ is obtained by filtering by the fiber of $\\mathrm{can}-\\varphi$ levelwise, $E_1^{i,j}\\Rightarrow TC_{i+j}(O_K\\otimes\\mathbb{Z}_p^t\\text{-terms})$; the Nygaard filtration $N^{\\geq j}$ on the $E_1$-terms of the descent spectral sequence for $TC^-$ and $TP$ produces secondary spectral sequences, e.g. $E_2^{1}(TC^-)=H^i(\\mathrm{gr}^{2k}_{TC^-(O_K/S\\otimes[n])})\\Rightarrow E_2^2(TC^-)$, connecting Nygaard-complete $\\delta$-ring data to explicit $TC$-groups."
  2042,Chrysanthemum (Chang),"https://chry-santhemum.github.io/website/PrismIntro.pdf",github.io,math.NT,math.NT math.AG mathematics prismatic-cohomology p-adic-numbers commutative-algebra differential-geometry survey github,null,null,null,"Chrysanthemum (Chang), introduction to prismatic cohomology identifying it with derived de Rham cohomology. Key computation: for a quasi-syntomic ring $R$, after $p$-completion there is a canonical quasi-isomorphism $q\\Omega^*_{R/\\mathbb{Z}_p}\\simeq \\Delta_R$ over the base prism $(\\mathbb{Z}_p,p)$, resolving a conjecture; the Hodge–Tate comparison gives $\\mathrm{gr}^i\\bar\\Delta_R \\cong \\bar\\Omega^i_R[i]$, and the Nygaard filtration on $TC^-$ recovers the motivic filtration."
  2043,Huan–Young,"https://aritopo.github.io/2022-12/Huan.pdf",github.io,math.KT,math.KT math.AT mathematics k-theory github,null,null,null,"Huan–Young, twisted Real quasi-elliptic cohomology realized as a twist of Tate K-theory. Key computation: twisted Real quasi-elliptic cohomology $QEll_H^*(X)$ comes with explicit restriction $K_G^*(X)\\to QEll_H^*(X)$, induction $QEll_H^*(X)\\to K_H^*(X)$, and Künneth $QEll_G^*(X)\\hat\\otimes_{\\mathbb{Z}[q^{\\pm}]} QEll_H^*(Y)\\to QEll_{G\\times H}^*(X\\times Y)$ maps; a change-of-group isomorphism $QEll_G^*(Y\\times_H G)\\cong QEll_H^*(Y)$ and a transfer map $K_{\\Sigma_N}^*(pt)\\to K^*(pt)/I_{tr}$ with explicit transfer ideal $I_{tr}^{\\mathrm{Tate}}$ complete the picture."
  2044,Bin Zhao,"https://aritopo.github.io/2022-12/Zhao.pdf",github.io,math.NT,math.NT cs.DS math.RA math.NA mathematics computer-science modular-forms algorithms linear-algebra github,null,null,null,"Bin Zhao, slopes of modular forms and the ghost conjecture. Key computation: for normalized $f\\in S_k(\\Gamma_1(N))$ with $p\\mid N$, the slope of $f$ is $v_p(a_p)$ for the $U_p$-eigenvalue $a_p$; a ghost map built from the ghost components of a Witt-vector-like ring on the space of modular forms gives an algorithm computing the Newton slopes of $U_p$, and the ghost conjecture (Buzzard) states this ghost map computes slopes exactly, verified in explicit slope tables for small $p,N$."
  2045,Zhang,"https://aritopo.github.io/2022-12/Zhang.pdf",github.io,math.AT,math.AT math.NT math.KT mathematics algebraic-number-theory homotopy-theory k-theory github,null,null,null,"Zhang, a Quillen–Lichtenbaum conjecture for Dirichlet $L$-functions. Key computation: reformulates Dirichlet's unit theorem $\\mathcal{O}_F^\\times\\cong\\mu(F)\\times\\mathbb{Z}^{\\oplus r_1+r_2-1}$ (so $\\dim_\\mathbb{Q}\\mathcal{O}_F^\\times\\otimes\\mathbb{Q}=\\mathrm{ord}_{s=0}\\zeta_F(s)$) and the class number formula $\\zeta_F^*(0)=-\\frac{|cl(F)|}{|\\mu(F)|}R(F)$ via $K_0(\\mathcal{O}_F)\\cong\\mathbb{Z}\\oplus cl(F)$ and $K_1(\\mathcal{O}_F)\\cong\\mathcal{O}_F^\\times$, then states/proves a Quillen–Lichtenbaum-type statement identifying rational equivariant algebraic K-groups of number fields with values of Dirichlet $L$-functions at nonpositive integers."
  2046,Xintong Gu,"https://aritopo.github.io/2022-12/Gu.pdf",github.io,math.AG,math.AG math.AT math.NT math.CT mathematics motives algebraic-topology k-theory stacks github,null,null,null,"Xintong Gu, ordinary and motivic cohomology of $BPGL_n(\\mathbb{C})$. Key computation: motivic cohomology satisfies $\\tilde{H}M^{s,s}(\\mathrm{spec}(k),A)\\cong K_M^s(k)\\otimes A$ (Milnor K-theory) and $\\mathbb{A}^1$-homotopy invariance $H_M^{*,*}(X\\times\\mathbb{A}^1)\\cong H_M^{*,*}(X)$; computes the motivic cohomology of the classifying stack $BPGL_n$ via the sites $\\mathrm{Torsor}_{Nis}(-)\\to H_M^{*,*}(-,B_{Nis}G)$ and $\\mathrm{Torsor}_{\\acute{e}t}(-)\\to H_M^{*,*}(-,B_{\\acute{e}t}G)$, classifying $G$-torsors and giving explicit $H^{*,*}(BPGL_n)$-calculations."
  2047,Si-Ying Lee,"https://Est-wald.github.io/notes-of-talks/courses/LSY-prismatic.pdf",github.io,math.NT,math.NT math.CT math.AG math.RA mathematics homological-algebra p-adic-hodge-theory prismatic-cohomology abelian-varieties github,null,null,null,"Si-Ying Lee, prismatic Dieudonné theory generalizing Breuil–Kisin's classification of $p$-divisible groups. Key computation: reduces good reduction of $p$-divisible groups $G=A_{\\mathbb{Z}}[p^\\infty]$ to $T_pG$ being crystalline (the proved Fontaine conjecture), via Breuil–Kisin's fully faithful functor $\\mathrm{Rep}_{\\mathcal{O}_K^{ur}}(\\mathrm{Gal}_k)\\to\\{\\text{Breuil–Kisin modules}\\}$ and $BT/\\mathcal{O}_K\\simeq\\{\\text{subcat. of BK-modules}\\}$, generalized by Bhatt–Scholze's prismatic F-crystals; for a $p$-divisible group $G/X$, $\\mathrm{Ext}^2_{X/S}(\\underline{G},0)=0$ and $\\mathrm{Ext}^1_{X/S}(\\underline{G},0)\\simeq\\mathrm{Im}(\\mathrm{Ext}^1(\\underline{G}_{n+m},0)\\to\\mathrm{Ext}^1(\\underline{G}_n,0))$ is locally free of rank $2kh/\\mathcal{O}_T$, computed via the snake lemma on $0\\to\\underline{G}_n\\to\\underline{G}\\xrightarrow{p^n}\\underline{G}\\to0$."
  2048,Avi Zeff,"https://math.berkeley.edu/~avizeff/seminars/prismatic-overview.pdf",berkeley.edu,math.AG,math.AG math.NT mathematics p-adic-hodge-theory prismatic-cohomology etale-cohomology hodge-theory survey,null,null,null,"Avi Zeff, overview of prismatic cohomology and its comparison theorems. Key computation: for a perfect prism $(A,(d))$ with $R=A/d$ perfectoid, the étale comparison identifies nearby cycles $R\\mu_*\\mathbb{Z}/p^n \\simeq (\\Delta_{X/A}[1/d]/p^n)^{\\varphi=1}$; the Hodge–Tate comparison shows $\\Delta_{X/A}$ is a perfect complex with $\\mathrm{gr}^i \\simeq \\Omega^i_{X/(A/I)}[-i]$ via reduction to characteristic $p$ and the Cartier isomorphism; and the Nygaard filtration $\\mathrm{Fil}_i^N A := \\{x\\in A : \\varphi(x)\\in d^i A\\}$ lifts the Hodge filtration via $d^{\\varphi(i)}$-twisted maps."
  2049,Bhargav Bhatt,"https://public.websites.umich.edu/~bhattb/teaching/prismatic-columbia/lecture7-derived-prismatic.pdf",umich.edu,math.CT,math.CT math.AG mathematics category-theory descent-theory homological-algebra prismatic-cohomology,null,null,null,"Bhargav Bhatt, derived prismatic cohomology extended to arbitrary $p$-complete $A/I$-algebras via non-abelian derived functors. Key computation: for a regular semiperfect $k$-algebra $S=R/I$ with $R$ perfect over $k$ and $I$ generated by a regular sequence, $L_{S/R} \\simeq I/I^2[1]$ and $\\wedge^i L_{S/R} \\simeq \\Gamma_i^R(I/I^2)[i]$; the conjugate filtration forces $dR_{S/k}^\\infty$ to be concentrated in degree $0$ and equal $D_I^\\infty(R)$ — e.g. $S=k[x^{1/p^\\infty}]/(x)$ gives $dR_{S/k}^\\infty \\simeq \\bigoplus_{i\\in\\mathbb{N}[1/p]} k\\cdot x^i/\\lfloor i\\rfloor!$ — and descending along the Čech nerve of $R\\to R_{perf}$ computes $dR_{R/k}$ for smooth $R$."
  2050,Bhargav Bhatt,"https://public.websites.umich.edu/~bhattb/teaching/prismatic-columbia/lecture5-prismatic-site.pdf",umich.edu,math.NT,math.NT math.AG math.CT math.DG mathematics p-adic-hodge-theory prismatic-cohomology differential-geometry p-adic-numbers,null,null,null,"Bhargav Bhatt, the prismatic site $(R/A)_\\Delta$ and the Hodge–Tate comparison theorem. Key computation: for the torus $R=(\\mathcal{O}_K[X^{\\pm1}])^\\wedge$ over the base prism $(A,I)=(\\mathbb{Z}_p\\langle q-1\\rangle,([p]_q))$ with $[i]_q=(q^i-1)/(q-1)$, $\\bar\\Delta_{R/A}$ is computed by the two-term $q$-de Rham complex $A\\langle X^{\\pm1}\\rangle \\xrightarrow{[i]_q\\frac{dX}{X}} A\\langle X^{\\pm1}\\rangle$; since $[i]_q$ is invertible mod $[p]_q$ for $p\\nmid i$ and $[kp]_q=0$, reduction mod $[p]_q$ yields the graded pieces $H^0=\\bigoplus_i (A/I)X^{kp}$, $H^1=\\bigoplus_i (A/I)X^{kp}\\frac{dX}{X}$, explicitly verifying the Hodge–Tate isomorphism $\\Omega^*_{R/(A/I)}\\cong H^*(\\bar\\Delta_{R/A})$ and perfectness of $\\bar\\Delta_{R/A}$."
  2051,Rizzo,"https://blogs.cuit.columbia.edu/jr4460/files/2024/12/IntroCondensedMathChile.pdf",columbia.edu,math.CT,math.CT math.AG math.FA math.CO mathematics condensed-mathematics category-theory extremal-combinatorics,null,null,null,"Rizzo, condensed mathematics and analytic geometry classifying classical topological categories by separatedness conditions on condensed sets. Key computation: Theorem 1.1.15 shows the category $\\mathrm{Top}^\\omega$ of compactly generated topological spaces is equivalent to quasi-separated condensed sets; light profinite sets are exactly quotients of the Cantor set $\\{0,1\\}^{\\mathbb{N}}$, and extremally disconnected profinite sets give internally projective $\\mathbb{Z}[S]$, proving $\\mathrm{Cond}(\\mathrm{Ab})$ is abelian with enough projectives."
  2052,Xinyu Zhou,"https://math.bu.edu/people/xyz6/prism_lec1.pdf",bu.edu,math.AG,math.AG math.NT mathematics prismatic-cohomology category-theory differential-geometry etale-cohomology,null,null,null,"Xinyu Zhou, prismatic cohomology as cohomology of the structure sheaf on the relative prismatic site, comparing to classical $p$-adic cohomology theories without smoothness/properness. Key computation: for a bounded prism $(A,I)$ and smooth $p$-adic formal $X/\\mathrm{Spf}\\,A/I$, the crystalline case $I=(p)$ gives $R\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{crys}}(X/A)\\simeq R\\Gamma_\\Delta(X/A)\\otimes^L_{A,\\varphi_A}A$; the de Rham comparison gives $H^i(R\\Gamma_\\Delta(X/A)\\otimes^L_A A/I)\\simeq\\Omega^i_{R/(A/I)}\\{-i\\}$ with Nygaard twist $\\{-i\\}=-(I/I^2)^{\\otimes i}$; the étale comparison for perfect $A$ gives $R\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{\\acute et}}(X_\\eta,\\mathbb{Z}/p^n)\\simeq R\\Gamma_\\Delta(X/A)\\otimes^L_A(A/p)[1/I]$; and the functor $R\\mapsto(A_{\\mathrm{inf}}(R),\\ker\\theta)$ gives an equivalence perfect prisms $\\simeq$ perfectoid rings."
  2053,Peter Scholze,"https://www.math.uni-bonn.de/people/scholze/PerfectoidSpaces.pdf",uni-bonn.de,math.AG,math.AG math.NT mathematics perfectoid-spaces algebraic-geometry category-theory p-adic-hodge-theory,null,null,null,"Peter Scholze, perfectoid spaces as a framework for almost purity and weight-monodromy via tilting between mixed and equal characteristic. Key computation: classification of points of the adic space $\\mathrm{Spa}(R,R^+)$ over $k=\\langle T\\rangle$: the functor $r:X\\mapsto X^{ad}$ induces an equivalence $\\{$qs rigid-analytic varieties$/k\\}\\cong\\{$qs adic spaces locally of finite type$/k\\}$, and the five types of points are classified — closed rank-1 points, branching points of type 2 with the Gauß point, type-3 points from a disc $D(x,r)$, dead ends from nested discs $D_1\\supset D_2\\supset\\cdots$ with empty intersection (existing iff $k$ is not spherically complete), and rank-2 points with value group $\\Gamma=\\mathbb{R}_{>0}\\times\\gamma^{\\mathbb{Z}}$."
  2054,Peter Scholze,"https://www.math.uni-bonn.de/people/scholze/ICM.pdf",uni-bonn.de,math.AG,math.AG math.NT math.AC mathematics p-adic-hodge-theory perfectoid-spaces commutative-algebra algebraic-geometry,null,null,null,"Peter Scholze, perfectoid spaces and their applications: tilting/untilting and the Fargues–Fontaine curve. Key computation: Theorem 3.3 — for a perfectoid field $K$ with tilt $K^\\flat$, $L\\mapsto L^\\flat$ gives an equivalence between finite extensions of $K$ and of $K^\\flat$, hence an isomorphism $G_K\\cong G_{K^\\flat}$; Theorem 4.1 (Fargues–Fontaine) — for a perfectoid field $L$ of characteristic $p$ there is a regular noetherian scheme $X_L$ of Krull dimension 1 over $\\mathbb{Q}_p$ whose closed points are in bijection with equivalence classes of untilts $(K,\\iota:L\\to K^\\flat)$ up to $\\iota\\circ\\Phi^n$, of degree $[K^\\flat:L]$."
  2055,Bhargav Bhatt,"https://www.math.ias.edu/~bhatt/teaching/prismatic-columbia/lecture1-overview.pdf",ias.edu,math.NT,math.NT math.AG mathematics p-adic-numbers commutative-algebra differential-geometry etale-cohomology lecture-notes survey,null,null,null,"Bhargav Bhatt, overview lecture on prismatic cohomology unifying integral $p$-adic cohomology theories. Key computation: Theorem 3.3 attaches to $R$ a $(p,u)$-adically complete complex $\\Delta_{R/A}$ with Frobenius $\\varphi_{R/A}:\\varphi^*\\Delta_{R/A}\\to\\Delta_{R/A}$ satisfying $\\varphi^*\\Delta_{R/A}\\otimes^L_A \\mathbb{F}_p \\simeq \\Omega^*_{R_{\\mathbb{F}_p}/\\mathbb{F}_p}$ (crystalline comparison), $\\Delta_{R/A}\\otimes^L_A \\mathbb{F}_p((u))$ with $\\varphi=1$ fixed points $\\simeq R\\Gamma_{\\text{ét}}(\\mathrm{Spec}\\,R_C,\\mathbb{F}_p)$ (étale comparison), and $H^i(\\Delta_{R/A}\\otimes^L_A \\mathbb{Z}_p)\\simeq\\Omega^i_{R/\\mathbb{Z}_p}$ (de Rham comparison); for $R$ the completion of $\\mathbb{Z}[x,x^{-1}]$, $\\Omega^*_{R/\\mathbb{Z}_p}=\\bigoplus_{i\\in\\mathbb{Z}}\\mathbb{Z}_p\\cdot x^i(dx/x)\\to \\mathbb{Z}_p\\cdot x^i$ computes the torus example."
  2056,Peter Scholze,"https://www.math.uni-bonn.de/people/scholze/prisms.pdf",uni-bonn.de,math.AG,math.AG math.NT mathematics prismatic-cohomology differential-geometry p-adic-hodge-theory p-adic-numbers,null,null,null,"Peter Scholze, prisms and prismatic cohomology. Key computation: Theorem 1.8 — for a bounded prism $(A,I)$ and smooth $p$-adic formal $X/\\mathrm{Spf}(A/I)$, $\\mathrm{can}\\otimes\\varphi: \\varphi^*R\\Gamma_\\Delta(X/A)\\otimes^L_A A[1/I]\\simeq R\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{crys}}(X/A)$, and the Hodge–Tate comparison gives $\\Omega^i_{R/(A/I)}\\{-i\\}\\simeq H^i(R\\Gamma_\\Delta(X/A)\\otimes^L_A A/I)$ with Tate twist $\\{-i\\}=(I/I^2)^{\\otimes i}$; over the $q$-prism $(\\mathbb{Z}_p\\langle q-1\\rangle,([p]_q))$ with $[p]_q=(q^p-1)/(q-1)$, prismatic cohomology recovers the $q$-de Rham complexes, and the Nygaard filtration is $F^iA=\\{x\\in A:\\varphi(x)\\in d^iA\\}$."
  2057,Jacob Lurie,"https://www.math.ias.edu/~lurie/ffcurve/Lecture6-8-Perfectoid.pdf",ias.edu,math.AG,math.AG math.NT mathematics p-adic-hodge-theory perfectoid-spaces,null,null,null,"Jacob Lurie, foundations of perfectoid spaces and the tilting correspondence for integral perfectoid rings. Key computation: Theorem 1.7 — for integral perfectoid $A$, $B\\mapsto B^\\flat$ is an equivalence $\\{$integral perfectoid $A$-algebras$\\}\\cong\\{$integral perfectoid $A^\\flat$-algebras$\\}$ with inverse $C\\mapsto C^\\#=W(C)\\otimes_{W(A^\\flat),\\theta}A$, compatible with almost isomorphisms; Proposition 1.6 constructs $\\theta:W(A^\\flat)\\to A$ with kernel generated by $\\xi=p+[\\pi^\\flat]^px$, and the divisibility computation $\\pi^{p^l}a^p\\in\\pi^pA\\Rightarrow\\pi^la\\in\\pi A$ shows $\\xi$ is a non-zero-divisor."
  2058,B,"https://bheuer.github.io/PerfectoidNotes.pdf",github.io,math.NT,math.NT math.AG mathematics p-adic-hodge-theory p-adic-numbers perfectoid-spaces category-theory github,null,null,null,"B. Heuer, perfectoid spaces via inverse perfection and tilting. Key computation: Theorem 1.1.13 (Fontaine–Wintenberger) — the natural isomorphism $\\mathrm{Gal}(\\mathbb{Q}_p\\langle p^{1/p^\\infty}\\rangle \\mid \\mathbb{Q}_p\\langle p^{1/p}\\rangle)\\cong \\mathrm{Gal}(\\mathbb{F}_p((t^{1/p^\\infty}))\\mid \\mathbb{F}_p((t^{1/p})))$, obtained by matching finite ramified extensions such as $X^n-X-p$ over $\\mathbb{Q}_p\\langle p^{1/p^\\infty}\\rangle$ to $X^n-X-t$ over $\\mathbb{F}_p((t^{1/p^\\infty}))$; Definition 1.1.26 — the tilting functor $A\\mapsto A^\\flat=\\varprojlim_{x\\mapsto x^p}(A/\\pi)[(\\pi^\\flat)^{-1}]$ classifies perfectoid algebras up to untilting."
  2059,Kiran Kedlaya,"https://kskedlaya.org/slides/ams-sf-2014.pdf",kskedlaya.org,math.NT,math.NT math.AG mathematics p-adic-hodge-theory perfectoid-spaces commutative-algebra galois-theory slides,null,null,null,"Kiran Kedlaya, a brief history of perfectoid spaces. Key computation: the Fontaine–Wintenberger field-of-norms isomorphism between the absolute Galois groups of $\\mathbb{Q}_p(\\zeta_{p^\\infty})$ and $\\mathbb{F}_p((\\pi))$ is, in retrospect, the first tilting equivalence between perfectoid spaces; the slides trace the period ring construction $B^+(L)$ as the completion of $W(O_L)[1/p]$ and its role in parametrizing untilts."
  2060,Jared Weinstein,"https://swc-math.github.io/aws/2017/2017WeinsteinNotes.pdf",github.io,math.AG,math.AG math.NT mathematics p-adic-hodge-theory perfectoid-spaces github,null,null,null,"Jared Weinstein, adic spaces for perfectoid geometry. Key computation: Theorem 2.3.2 — the map $I\\mapsto(W(K^\\circ)/I)[1/p]$ is a bijection between degree-1 primitive ideals of $W(K^\\circ)$ and isomorphism classes of untilts of the perfectoid field $K$ ($I=(p)$ giving the trivial untilt); Theorem 2.3.5 (FF11) — for algebraically closed $C$ the closed maximal ideals of $B_C$ parametrize untilts, and $X_K=Y_K/\\varphi^{\\mathbb{Z}}$ with $Y_K=\\mathrm{Spa}(W(K^\\circ))\\setminus\\{|p[\\omega]|=0\\}$ is the Fargues–Fontaine curve."
  2061,Dominic Joyce,"https://people.maths.ox.ac.uk/joyce/DAG2022/lecture2.pdf",ac.uk,math.AG,math.AG mathematics algebraic-geometry derived-algebraic-geometry schemes,null,null,null,"Dominic Joyce, derived algebraic geometry background lectures. Key computation: derived Bézout — for projective curves $C,D\\subset\\mathbb{CP}^2$ of degrees $m,n$, the derived intersection $C\\cap D$ is a proper quasi-smooth derived scheme with $\\mathrm{vdim}=0$ and virtual count $[X]^{\\mathrm{virt}}=mn$ even when $\\dim X=1$; the cdga model $A^*=\\mathbb{C}[x_1..x_m;y_1..y_n]$ with $dy_i=p_i$ gives $H^0=\\mathbb{C}[x]/(p_i)$, $H^{-1}=$ dependencies, so $x^2=y^2=0$ and $x^2=y^2=x^2+y^2=0$ are distinct derived schemes."
  2062,John Rognes,"https://www.sas.rochester.edu/mth/sites/doug-ravenel/otherpapers/uio-chromatic.pdf",rochester.edu,math.AT,math.AT math.CT mathematics chromatic-homotopy-theory category-theory algebraic-topology homotopy-theory lecture-notes,null,null,null,"John Rognes, chromatic homotopy theory course notes. Key computation: the thick subcategory theorem (Hopkins–Smith) states every thick subcategory $T\\subset \\mathrm{Ho}(\\mathrm{Sp}^\\omega_p)$ of finite $p$-local spectra equals $\\mathrm{Ho}(\\mathrm{Sp}^\\omega_{\\ge n})$ for some $0\\le n\\le\\infty$; Mitchell's $F(n)$ has $H^*(F(n);\\mathbb{F}_p)$ free over $A(n-1)$ with $K(m)_*(F(n))=0$ for $m<n$ and $K(n)_*(F(n))\\neq0$, so $\\mathrm{type}(F(n))=n$, and Ravenel's result gives $\\dim K(n)_*(F)\\le\\dim K(n+1)_*(F)$, i.e. $K(n+1)_*(F)=0\\Rightarrow K(n)_*(F)=0$."
  2063,Akhil Mathew,"https://math.uchicago.edu/~amathew/notes.pdf",uchicago.edu,math.AG,math.AG math.NT mathematics p-adic-hodge-theory perfectoid-spaces commutative-algebra lecture-notes,null,null,null,"Akhil Mathew, perfectoid spaces course notes. Key computation: Theorem 157 (Scholze) — over the perfectoid valuation ring $V=K^\\circ$ of a perfectoid field, any almost finitely generated $V$-module $M$ satisfies $M\\approx V/I_{\\gamma_1}\\oplus V/I_{\\gamma_2}\\oplus\\cdots$ for a unique nondecreasing sequence $\\gamma_i\\in\\mathbb{R}_{\\ge0}$ with $\\gamma_i\\to1$, where $I_\\gamma=\\{x\\in V:|x|\\le\\gamma\\}$ (or $<\\gamma$), and every almost finitely generated projective module is $M\\approx V^n$ for a unique $n\\ge0$."
  2064,Johan Commelin,"https://math.commelin.net/files/condmath.pdf",commelin.net,math.AG,math.AG math.CT math.FA mathematics condensed-mathematics category-theory commutative-algebra homological-algebra,null,null,null,"Johan Commelin, condensed mathematics seminar: condensed abelian groups form an abelian category satisfying AB4*/AB6 with a fully faithful embedding $D^b(\\mathrm{LCA})\\to D(\\mathrm{Cond}(\\mathrm{Ab}))$ preserving Pontryagin duality. Key computation: for two completions $\\mathbb{Q}_v,\\mathbb{Q}_v'$ of $\\mathbb{Q}$, derived solidification computes $\\mathbb{Q}_v\\otimes^L\\mathbb{Q}_v'$; the structure theorem $0\\to\\mathbb{R}^n\\oplus C\\to A\\to D\\to0$ for $A\\in\\mathrm{LCA}$ yields $R\\mathrm{Hom}(A,B)$ via the compact-open topology compatible with internal Hom, so condensed cohomology of a compact Hausdorff $S$ with $\\mathbb{Z}$-coefficients equals sheaf cohomology while higher real coefficients vanish."
  2065,Neil Strickland,"https://strickland1.org/talks/survey_talk.pdf",strickland1.org,math.AT,math.AT mathematics chromatic-homotopy-theory algebraic-topology arithmetic-geometry homotopy-theory,null,null,null,"Neil Strickland, recent progress in chromatic homotopy theory: reports the disproof of Ravenel's Telescope Conjecture (Burklund–Hahn–Levy–Schlank 2023). Key computation: the telescope conjecture fails for height $n>1$ while being true for $n=0,1$; equivalently $K(p,\\le n)_*(X)=0$ does not force $X$ to be a filtered colimit of finite spectra $X_\\alpha$ with $K(p,\\le n)_*(X_\\alpha)=0$."
  2066,Marius Nielsen,"https://mariusnielsen.github.io/FormalGroups.pdf",github.io,math.AT,math.AT math.NT math.CT mathematics arithmetic-geometry homotopy-theory stable-homotopy-theory algebraic-topology github,null,null,null,"Marius Nielsen, chromatic homotopy theory from the perspective of formal groups. Key computation: Quillen's theorem $L\\cong MU_*$ identifies $FGL=\\mathrm{Spec}(L)$ with $MU$, giving $\\mathrm{Comod}_{MU_*MU}\\cong\\mathrm{QCoh}(M_{fg})$ and $E_2^{s,2t}=H^s(M_{fg},\\omega^t)$ in the Adams spectral sequence; the thick subcategory theorem states $C\\subset \\mathrm{Sp}^\\omega_{(p)}$ is thick iff $C$ consists of spectra of height $\\le n$ for some $n$, via the height filtration $M_{fg}^{\\ge n}\\subset M_{fg}$ with $[p]$-multiplication factoring through $\\mathrm{Frob}^n$."
  2067,C,"https://sites.math.duke.edu/~kgw/8803_Stable/L1_Introduction.pdf",duke.edu,math.AT,math.AT math.CT mathematics algebraic-topology homological-algebra homotopy-theory stable-homotopy-theory survey,null,null,null,"C. Westerland, introduction to stable homotopy theory. Key computation: the EHP fibration $S^n\\to\\Omega S^{n+1}\\to\\Omega S^{2n+1}$ with the James construction gives the split $S^{2n-1}\\to\\Omega\\Sigma S^n$, so the EHP spectral sequence $E^1_k=\\pi_k(S^{2n+1})\\Rightarrow\\pi_*(S^{n+1})$ computes the 2-primary stable stems; concretely the $e$-invariant element $\\pi_{4n-1}(S)\\otimes\\mathbb{Z}\\cong\\mathbb{Z}_{e(n)}$ is classified by the degree of the induced map on $S^{4n-1}$."
  2068,A,"https://math.uchicago.edu/~amathew/256y.pdf",uchicago.edu,math.AT,math.AT mathematics algebraic-topology homotopy-theory stable-homotopy-theory lecture-notes,null,null,null,"A. Mathew, stable homotopy theory course notes. Key computation: classification of the number $r(n)$ of linearly independent vector fields on $S^{n-1}$ by the Radon–Hurwitz number $\\rho(n)-1$: writing $n=(2a+1)\\cdot2^{4b+c}$ with $c=0,1,2,3$, then $\\rho(n)=2^c+8b$, and $\\beta(n)=\\rho(n)-1$ independent nowhere-vanishing fields exist, equality holding up to the $J$-image; concretely $\\widetilde{KO}(\\mathbb{RP}^n)=\\mathbb{Z}/2^c\\oplus\\mathbb{Z}/2^{\\lceil n/2\\rceil}$ computed from $KO(\\mathbb{CP}^n)=\\mathbb{Z}[u]/(u^{n+1})$."
  2069,Caleb Ji,"https://www.math.columbia.edu/~calebji/stable-notes.pdf",columbia.edu,math.AT,math.AT mathematics algebraic-topology stable-homotopy-theory category-theory,null,null,null,"Caleb Ji, spectra and the stable homotopy category. Key computation: the homotopy groups of a spectrum $X$ are $\\pi_k(X)=\\mathrm{colim}_n\\pi_{k+n}(X_n)$, and the smash product $X\\wedge Y$ with latching structure $(X\\wedge Y)_n=(X\\wedge Y)_n^{\\mathrm{lat}}\\cup\\cdots$ shows $S^0\\wedge S^0=S^0$ gives $\\pi_*(S)$ as the initial stable homotopy ring."
  2070,Dominic Joyce,"https://people.maths.ox.ac.uk/joyce/DAG2022/DAG11+12.pdf",ac.uk,math.AG,math.AG math.LO mathematics algebraic-geometry derived-algebraic-geometry field-with-one-element mathematical-logic,null,null,null,"Dominic Joyce, derived algebraic geometry foundations and higher stacks. Key computation: for the derived zero locus $X=s^{-1}(0)$ of a section $s$ of a vector bundle $E\\to V$, $L_X\\simeq[E^*|_X\\xrightarrow{Ds}T^*V|_X]$, perfect in degrees $[-1,0]$; explicitly $A^*=K[z_1..z_n,y_1..y_m,x_1..x_l]$ with $\\deg z=0,\\deg y=-1,\\deg x=-2$, $dy_j=f_j(z)$, $dx_i=\\sum_j g_{ij}(z)y_j$, $d^2=0$ forcing $\\sum_j g_{ij}(z)f_j(z)=0$, so $H^0(A^*)=K[z]/(f_1=0,..,f_m=0)$ classifies the classical truncation $X=t_0(X)\\subset\\mathbb{A}^n$, with $L_X\\simeq[\\mathcal{O}_X^m\\xrightarrow{\\partial f_j/\\partial z_k}\\mathcal{O}_X^n]$ in degrees $[-1,0]$."
  2071,Raphaël Ponge,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/73/57/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.OA,math.OA math-ph math.CA math.DG mathematics physics noncommutative-geometry real-analysis spectral-geometry,null,null,null,"Raphaël Ponge, semiclassical analysis on spectral triples extending the semiclassical Weyl law and Connes' integration formula to $p$-summable spectral triples without Hölder-regularity. Key computation: the semiclassical Weyl law $\\lim_{h\\to0^+}h^n N^-(h^2\\Delta_g+V)=\\int_M V_-(x)^{n/2}\\,d\\nu_g(x)$ (and $\\lim h^n N^-(h^{2q}\\Delta_g^q+V)=\\int V_-^{n/2q}$); the main theorem — if for all $a\\in A$, $a>0$, $\\lim_{j\\to\\infty}j^{-2/p}\\lambda_j(aD^2a)=\\tau(a^{-p/2})$, then $\\lim_{j}j^{-2/p}\\lambda_j(|D|^{-q/2}a|D|^{-q/2})=\\tau(a^{q/p})$ for all $q>0$, and for $V^*=V\\in A$, $\\lim_{h\\to0^+}h^p N^-(h^2D^2+V)=\\mathrm{Res}_{z=p}\\,\\mathrm{Tr}(V_-)^{z/2}|D|^{-z}$, recovering Connes' integration formula $\\int f=\\mathrm{Res}_{z=p}\\mathrm{Tr}(f|D|^{-z})$."
  2072,chromatic homotopy seminar notes,"https://shaybm.com/chromatic-seminar/notes/chromatic-homotopy-nilpotence-thick-subcategories.pdf",shaybm.com,math.AT,math.AT mathematics chromatic-homotopy-theory algebraic-topology category-theory combinatorics lecture-notes,null,null,null,"chromatic homotopy seminar notes, the nilpotence theorem and the thick subcategory theorem (Devinatz–Hopkins–Smith). Key computation: Theorem 9 (Hopkins–Smith) classifies thick subcategories of finite $p$-local spectra as exactly the $\\mathcal{C}_{\\ge n}=\\{X:K(m)_*(X)=0\\text{ for }m<n\\}$; the Nilpotence theorem states $MU$ detects nilpotence ($x\\in\\pi_*(R)$ nilpotent iff $MU_*x$ nilpotent iff $MU_*x=0$ in $MU_*R$), so a self-map $f$ vanishing on $K(n)_0R$ forces $f$ nilpotent, and the enumeration proceeds by showing $C_{n+1}\\subsetneq C_n$ is proper via Morava $K$-acyclicity."
  2073,Constantin Teleman,"https://chromotopy.org/teaching/Fall2012/seminar/constantin-seminar.pdf",chromotopy.org,math.AT,math.AT math.NT math.CT mathematics chromatic-homotopy-theory p-adic-numbers homological-algebra algebraic-topology lecture-notes,null,null,null,"Constantin Teleman, chromatic spectral sequence and stable homotopy of the spheres seminar notes: develops the $MU$-Adams (chromatic) spectral sequence $E_2=H^*(M_{fg},\\omega^*)$ and computes $\\pi_*L_{K(1)}S$. Key computation: for $S_1=\\mathbb{G}_m(\\mathbb{Z}_p^\\wedge)$, $(E_1)_*=\\mathbb{Z}_p^\\wedge[u^{\\pm1}]$; splitting $S_1=C_{p-1}\\times\\mathbb{Z}_p^\\wedge$ ($p$ odd), $H^*(C_{p-1};\\mathbb{Z}_p^\\wedge[u^{\\pm1}])=\\mathbb{Z}_p^\\wedge[u^{\\pm(p-1)}]$, and $H^i(\\mathbb{Z}_p^\\wedge;A)=\\mathbb{Z}_p^\\wedge/(kp)$ for $i=2k(p-1)-1$, $k\\ne0$, else $0$; the truncated chromatic spectral sequence gives the $E(q)$-local sphere agreeing with true stable stems within a finite, monotonically increasing radius (chromatic convergence), with stabilizer group $S_q\\subset O_q=\\mathbb{F}_{p^q}\\langle S\\rangle/(S^q=p,\\,Sw=w^\\varphi S)$ and $L_{K(n)}S\\simeq E_n^{hS_n}$."
  2074,"facets of spectral algebraic geometry seminar notes (KTH/SU): spectra as $S$-modules","https://dskern.github.io/Talks/SAG_Overview.pdf",github.io,math.AT,math.AT math.AG mathematics algebraic-geometry formal-groups stable-homotopy-theory moduli-spaces stacks topological-hochschild-homology lecture-notes github,null,null,null,"facets of spectral algebraic geometry seminar notes (KTH/SU): spectra as $S$-modules, $THH(A)=A\\otimes^*_S A$, $TC^t$ the $S^1$-equivariant refinement, and $MP$ (the 2-periodization of $MU$) as an affine chart for the moduli stack of oriented spectral formal groups. Key computation: $\\pi_*MU\\cong L$, the Lazard ring, so formal groups over $R$ are classified by $L\\to R$, and $E^*(X)=MU^*(X)\\otimes_{MU^*(*)}E^*(*)$ defines a cohomology theory iff $(p,v_1^{(p)},...,v_n^{(p)})$ is regular in $E^*(*)$ for all $p,n$ (Landweber), equivalently $\\mathrm{Spec}\\,R\\to M_{fg}$ flat, classifying Landweber-exact theories as flat families over the moduli stack of formal groups."
  2075,Bhargav Bhatt,"https://public.websites.umich.edu/~bhattb/teaching/mat679w17/lectures.pdf",umich.edu,math.NT,math.NT math.AG mathematics p-adic-hodge-theory perfectoid-spaces category-theory etale-cohomology,null,null,null,"Bhargav Bhatt, perfectoid spaces via inversions/tilting, developing the tilting correspondence and almost purity in dimension 0 and in characteristic $p$. Key computation: Theorem 3.2.8 — for a perfectoid field $K$, the tilt $L\\mapsto L^\\flat$ induces an equivalence of finite-étale categories $\\hat{K}_{\\text{ét}}\\simeq \\hat{K}^\\flat_{\\text{ét}}$, i.e. finite extensions of $K$ are classified by finite extensions of $K^\\flat$; concretely $\\mathbb{Z}_p^\\flat\\cong\\mathbb{F}_p((t^{1/p^\\infty}))$, with the chain $\\mathrm{Perf}_K\\simeq\\mathrm{Perf}_{K^{\\circ a}}\\simeq\\mathrm{Perf}_{K^{\\circ a}/\\pi}$ implemented by $R\\mapsto R^{\\circ a}$, $A\\mapsto A^*/[1/t]$, $A\\mapsto A/\\pi$."
  2076,Krauze,"https://www.math.ku.dk/english/calendar/events/zeta-values/slides--abstracts/talks_Krauze.pdf",ku.dk,math.NT,math.NT math.AG mathematics p-adic-numbers prismatic-cohomology algorithms,null,null,null,"Krauze, prismatic envelopes and an explicit algorithm computing $\\mathbb{Z}_p(i)(O_K/\\varpi^n)$. Key computation: for $A=W(k)[[z]]$, $R=O_K/\\varpi^n=A/(E(z),z^n)$, the prismatic envelope $\\Delta_{R/A}=(A\\{a\\}/(da-r)_\\delta)^\\wedge_{p,d}$ is free over $R$ on monomials $\\prod(\\delta^u a)^{e_u}$ with $e_u<p$, and $\\mathbb{Z}_p(i)(R)$ is the fiber of $\\mathrm{can}-\\varphi$ on the double complex over $\\Delta_{R/\\mathbb{Z}_p[[z_0,z_1]]}$; using the staircase bases $z^kd^c\\prod f_u^{e_u}$ ($c+p^ue_u\\ge i$) and a comparison lemma for differing differentials, Theorem 5.1 gives $|H^1(\\mathbb{Z}_p(i)(O_K/\\varpi^n))|/|H^2(\\mathbb{Z}_p(i)(O_K/\\varpi^n))|=q^{(n-1)i}$, hence $|K_{2i-1}(O_K/\\varpi^n)|/|K_{2i-2}(O_K/\\varpi^n)|=q^{(n-1)i}(q^i-1)$, and Theorem 5.2 gives even vanishing $K_{2i-2}(O_K/\\varpi^n)=0$ for $i\\gg0$."
  2077,Dominic Joyce,"https://people.maths.ox.ac.uk/joyce/DAG2022/DAG13%2B14.pdf",ac.uk,math.AG,math.AG mathematics enumerative-geometry algebraic-geometry curves deformation-theory,null,null,null,"Dominic Joyce, obstruction theories, virtual classes, and invariants: constructs virtual fundamental classes for quasi-smooth derived schemes. Key computation: for $X=s^{-1}(0)$ the derived zero locus of a section $s:V\\to E$ with $V$ smooth and $E$ a vector bundle, $L_X\\simeq[E^*|_X\\xrightarrow{Ds}T^*V|_X]$, $\\mathrm{vdim}(X)=\\dim(V)-\\mathrm{rank}(E)$, and $[X]^{\\mathrm{virt}}=[V]\\cap c_{\\mathrm{rank}(E)}(E)$; for plane curves $C,D\\subset\\mathbb{CP}^2$ of degrees $m,n$ this gives the virtual intersection number $mn$ even when $C\\cap D$ is not transverse."
  2078,Jiantong Liu,"https://jiantongliu.github.io/files/Categorifying_Spectra.pdf",github.io,math.CT,math.CT math.AC math.AT mathematics category-theory commutative-algebra stable-homotopy-theory github,null,null,null,"Jiantong Liu, categorifying spectra: explains the Ramzi–Sosnilo–Winges construction showing every spectrum occurs as nonconnective $K$-theory of a small idempotent-complete stable $\\infty$-category. Key computation: the Dundas–McCarthy linearization is $M\\simeq\\mathrm{colim}_n\\Omega^n\\widetilde{K}(\\mathrm{Sp}^\\omega;\\Sigma^nM)$; identifying this colimit as $K(C_M)$ yields $K(C_M)\\simeq M$ functorially, and the resulting examples show the nonconnective theorem-of-the-heart map $K(E^\\heartsuit)\\to K(E)$ can fail without the Noetherian hypothesis."
  2079,algebraic topology lecture notes,"https://math.mit.edu/~hrm/papers/notes-905.pdf",mit.edu,math.AT,math.AT math.CT math.RA mathematics algebraic-topology homological-algebra lecture-notes,null,null,null,"algebraic topology lecture notes, an explicit Tor calculation extracting torsion. Key computation: for $R=\\mathbb{Z}$ and $M=\\mathbb{Z}/n$, the free resolution $0\\to\\mathbb{Z}\\xrightarrow{n}\\mathbb{Z}\\to\\mathbb{Z}/n\\to0$ gives $\\mathbb{Z}/n\\otimes N\\cong N/nN$ and $\\mathrm{Tor}_1^{\\mathbb{Z}}(\\mathbb{Z}/n,N)\\cong\\ker(n:N\\to N)$, so $\\mathrm{Tor}_1$ extracts exactly the $n$-torsion of $N$."
  2080,Stefan Schwede,"https://www.math.uni-bonn.de/~schwede/universal_symmetries-9ecm.pdf",uni-bonn.de,math.AT,math.AT math.RT mathematics algebraic-topology equivariant-topology homotopy-theory algebraic-groups,null,null,null,"Stefan Schwede, universal symmetries: global equivariant homotopy theory packages compatible equivariant homotopy theories for all compact Lie groups into global spectra and global Mackey functors. Key computation: $\\pi_0^G(\\mathbb{S})$ is the Burnside ring $A(G)$, freely generated by transfers $\\mathrm{tr}_H^G(1)$ over conjugacy classes of closed $H\\le G$ with finite Weyl group; the unit $\\mathbb{S}\\to KU$ sends a finite $G$-set to its permutation representation, giving the homomorphism $A(G)\\to R(G)$."
  2081,Jiwu Jang,"https://math.mit.edu/research/highschool/primes/materials/2024/October/12-4%20Jang.pdf",mit.edu,math.RT,math.RT math.AG math.NT math.RA mathematics quiver-representations algebraic-geometry l-functions,null,null,null,"Jiwu Jang, vertex functions of type D Nakajima quiver varieties: proves a product expression for type-$D$ vertex functions in terms of $q$-binomial factors indexed by roots. Key computation: for the $D_4$ quiver with dimension vector $v=(1,2,1,1)$ and framing $w=(0,0,0,1)$, the vertex function factors into $q$-binomial-series terms associated to specific $D_4$ roots; in the $D_1$ case $V(z)=\\sum_{k\\ge0}(\\hbar)_k/(q)_k\\cdot z^k$, the ordinary $q$-binomial series."
  2082,Vesselin Dimitrov (joint with Frank Calegari and Yunqing Tang),"https://sf-web-assets-prod.s3.amazonaws.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/05104057/Dimitrov-Vesselin-Dimitrov.pdf",amazonaws.com,math.NT,math.NT mathematics l-functions differential-geometry talk,null,null,null,"Vesselin Dimitrov (joint with Frank Calegari and Yunqing Tang), arithmetic holonomy bounds and special values of $L$-functions: proves $\\mathbb{Q}$-linear independence results for $1,\\zeta(2)$, and $L(2,\\chi_{-3})$ using Apéry-style $G$-functions and modular periods. Key computation: with the Picard–Fuchs operator $L=x(1-x)(1-9x)d^2/dx^2+(1-20x+27x^2)d/dx+(9x-3)$, the chosen functions satisfy $L(A)=0$, $L(AB)=1$, and $L(AC)=1/(1-x)$; the Apéry limits identify $L(2,\\chi_{-3})/2$ and $\\zeta(2)/4$ as $w_6$-periods, producing simultaneous rational approximants."
  2083,Gabriel Dospinescu,"https://public.websites.umich.edu/~kaletha/slides/Dospinescu.pdf",umich.edu,math.AG,math.AG math.NT mathematics p-adic-hodge-theory perfectoid-spaces survey,null,null,null,"Gabriel Dospinescu, overview of the Fargues–Fontaine curve presenting the classification of vector bundles and its diamond-theoretic construction. Key computation: for $S=\\mathrm{Spa}(C)$ geometric, every rank-$n$ vector bundle on $X_C$ is uniquely classified by a nonincreasing slope sequence $\\lambda_1\\ge\\cdots\\ge\\lambda_n\\in\\mathbb{Q}$ through $\\mathcal{E}\\simeq\\bigoplus_i\\mathcal{O}(\\lambda_i)$, equivalently by $B(\\mathrm{GL}_n)$; this follows from $X_S=Y_S/\\varphi^{\\mathbb{Z}}$ and, for the pullback $\\mathcal{F}$ to the Stein space $Y_S$, the two-term computation $R\\Gamma(X_S,\\mathcal{E})\\simeq[\\mathcal{F}(Y_S)\\xrightarrow{1-\\varphi}\\mathcal{F}(Y_S)]$, so $H^i(X_S,\\mathcal{E})=0$ for $i>1$."
  2084,Alain Connes (joint with Caterina Consani),"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/44/59/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.OA,math.OA math.AG math-ph math.QA mathematics physics noncommutative-geometry tropical-geometry field-with-one-element intersection-theory slides talk,null,null,null,"Alain Connes (joint with Caterina Consani), from noncommutative geometry to tropical geometry: develops the arithmetic site and a tropical Riemann–Roch theory for the scaling quotient $C_p=\\mathbb{R}_+^*/p^{\\mathbb{Z}}$. Key computation: for $p>2$, principal divisors lie in the kernel of degree and the pair $(\\deg,\\chi)$ gives $\\mathrm{Div}(C_p)/\\mathcal{P}\\xrightarrow{\\sim}\\mathbb{R}\\times\\mathbb{Z}/(p-1)\\mathbb{Z}$; with $H^0(D)_\\rho=\\{f\\in H^0(D):\\lVert f\\rVert_p\\le\\rho\\}$ and $\\mathrm{Dim}_{\\mathbb{R}}H^0(D)=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}p^{-n}\\dim_{\\mathrm{top}}H^0(D)^{p^n}$, the slides compute $\\mathrm{Dim}_{\\mathbb{R}}H^0(D)-\\mathrm{Dim}_{\\mathbb{R}}H^0(-D)=\\deg D$."
  2085,Bertrand Toën,"https://www.math.univ-toulouse.fr/~btoen/ICM-2014-slides.pdf",univ-toulouse.fr,math.AG,math.AG quant-ph math.CT math.RT mathematics physics quantum-mechanics algebraic-geometry derived-algebraic-geometry lie-theory,null,null,null,"Bertrand Toën, derived algebraic geometry and deformation quantization: explains the canonical $(2-d)$-quantization of the derived stack $\\mathrm{Bun}_G(X)$ for a compact oriented $d$-manifold (or Calabi–Yau variety). Key computation: at a $G$-bundle $E$, $T_{\\mathrm{Bun}_G(X),E}\\simeq C^*(X,\\mathrm{ad}(E))[1]$; cup product followed by the invariant pairing on $\\mathfrak{g}$ and integration over $X$ produces a nondegenerate closed 2-form of degree $2-d$, hence a $(2-d)$-shifted Poisson structure, while higher formality identifies deformations of $D_{\\mathrm{qcoh}}(Z)$ as an $E_n$-monoidal dg-category with the dg Lie algebra $R\\Gamma(Z,\\mathrm{Sym}_{\\mathcal{O}_Z}(T_Z[-1-n]))[n+1]$, yielding the quantization."
  2086,Dominic Joyce,"https://people.maths.ox.ac.uk/joyce/DAG2022/DAG7+8.pdf",ac.uk,math.CT,math.CT math.AG mathematics category-theory derived-categories algebraic-geometry harmonic-analysis,null,null,null,"Dominic Joyce, triangulated categories and derived categories: culminates in the Fourier–Mukai classification of exact functors between derived categories of smooth projective schemes. Key computation: a kernel $\\mathcal{E}^\\bullet\\in D^b\\mathrm{coh}(X\\times Y)$ defines $\\Phi_{\\mathcal{E}}(\\mathcal{G}^\\bullet)=R(\\pi_Y)_*(L\\pi_X^*\\mathcal{G}^\\bullet\\otimes^L\\mathcal{E}^\\bullet)$; Mukai's formulas give its adjoints using $D_{X\\times Y}(\\mathcal{E}^\\bullet)\\otimes^L\\pi_Y^*K_Y[\\dim Y]$ and $D_{X\\times Y}(\\mathcal{E}^\\bullet)\\otimes^L\\pi_X^*K_X[\\dim X]$, and Orlov's theorem identifies every exact functor $D^b\\mathrm{coh}(X)\\to D^b\\mathrm{coh}(Y)$ with left and right adjoints with a unique such kernel up to isomorphism."
  2087,Jonathan Wang (joint with Tony Feng),"https://www.cirm-math.fr/RepOrga/2154/Slides/WangJonathan_nopause.pdf",cirm-math.fr,math.AG,math.AG math.NT math.CO math.RT mathematics automorphic-forms graph-theory derived-categories geometric-langlands talk,null,null,null,"Jonathan Wang (joint with Tony Feng), geometric periods: categorifies the $\\mathrm{GL}_2$ period formula and proves matching filtrations on its spectral and automorphic objects under geometric Langlands. Key computation: for $\\check{X}=\\mathrm{std}=\\mathbb{A}^2$ and $X=\\mathbb{G}_m\\backslash\\mathrm{GL}_2$, the two $\\check{G}$-orbits $\\{0\\}$ and $\\mathbb{A}^2\\setminus\\{0\\}=\\check{G}/\\check{\\mathrm{Mir}}_2$ yield the distinguished triangle $\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{std}}\\to\\Pi^{\\mathrm{spec}}_*(\\omega_{\\mathrm{LocSys}_{\\check{X}/\\check{G}}})\\to\\mathrm{Eis}^{\\mathrm{spec}}_{\\check{\\mathrm{Mir}}_2}(\\omega_{\\mathrm{LocSys}_{\\mathbb{G}_m}})\\xrightarrow{[1]}$, whose first term has !-stalk $\\mathrm{Sym}\\,H^\\bullet_{\\mathrm{dR}}(C,E)$; unfolding produces the matching automorphic triangle with $T_{\\mathrm{Sym}(\\mathrm{std})}(\\mathrm{Whit})$, $\\Pi_!(\\mathbb{C}_{\\mathrm{Bun}_X})$, and $\\mathrm{Eis}_{\\mathrm{Mir}_2,!}(\\mathbb{C}_{\\mathrm{Bun}_{\\mathbb{G}_m}})$."
  2088,Sanath K,"https://sanathdevalapurkar.github.io/files/princeton-fall-23-talk.pdf",github.io,math.AT,math.AT math.NT math.RT math.AG mathematics langlands-program algebraic-topology equivariant-topology formal-groups github,null,null,null,"Sanath K. Devalapurkar, equivariant homotopy theory and geometric Langlands: proves generalized derived Satake equivalences with periodic and elliptic cohomology coefficients. Key computation: for simply connected, simply laced semisimple $G$, $\\mathrm{Shv}_G^c(\\Omega G;KU)\\otimes\\mathbb{Q}\\simeq\\mathrm{QCoh}(\\check{G}_{\\mathrm{sc}}/\\check{G})_{\\text{2-per}}$, replacing the rational equivalence $\\mathrm{Shv}_G^c(\\Omega G;\\mathbb{Q})\\simeq\\mathrm{QCoh}(\\check{\\mathfrak{g}}^*/\\check{G})$; for an elliptic theory $A$ with curve $E$, this becomes $\\mathrm{Shv}_{G/Z(G)}^c(\\Omega G;A)\\otimes\\mathbb{Q}\\simeq\\mathrm{QCoh}(\\mathrm{Bun}^{\\mathrm{ss},0}_{\\check{G}}(E))\\otimes_{\\pi_0A}\\pi_*(A_{\\mathbb{Q}})$, identifying the spectral moduli object from the coefficient theory's formal group."
  2089,Shachar Carmeli,"https://www.sas.rochester.edu/mth/sites/doug-ravenel/otherpapers/carmelli-slides.pdf",rochester.edu,math.AT,math.AT math.NT math.AG math.KT mathematics chromatic-homotopy-theory algebraic-k-theory arithmetic-geometry commutative-algebra,null,null,null,"Shachar Carmeli, cyclotomic redshift: presents chromatic cyclotomic extensions and their height-raising behavior under algebraic $K$-theory. Key computation: the splitting $S_{T(n)}[B^nC_{p^k}]\\simeq S_{T(n)}[B^nC_{p^{k-1}}]\\times S_{T(n)}[\\omega_{p^k}^{(n)}]$ defines a $(\\mathbb{Z}/p^k)^\\times$-Galois extension, and for every commutative $T(n)$-local ring spectrum $R$ the redshift equivalence $L_{T(n+1)}K(R[\\omega_{p^k}^{(n)}])\\simeq L_{T(n+1)}K(R)[\\omega_{p^k}^{(n+1)}]$ raises its chromatic height; passing to the tower gives cyclotomic hyperdescent $L_{K(n+1)}K(R[\\omega_{p^\\infty}^{(n)}])^{h\\mathbb{Z}}\\simeq L_{K(n+1)}K(R)$."
  2090,Franz Luef,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/programs/scientific/12-13/quantumgroups/slides/Luef.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.AG,math.AG math.OA math-ph math.GR mathematics physics algebraic-groups noncommutative-geometry,null,null,null,"Franz Luef, noncommutative geometry and time-frequency analysis: identifies well-localized Gabor frames with projective modules and explicit projections over noncommutative tori. Key computation: the Feichtinger module $M_s^1(\\mathbb{R}^d)$ is an equivalence bimodule between $A_s^1(\\alpha\\mathbb{Z}\\times\\beta\\mathbb{Z},c)$ and $A_s^1(\\alpha^{-1}\\mathbb{Z}\\times\\beta^{-1}\\mathbb{Z},c)$ via the associativity identity ${}_\\Lambda\\langle f,g\\rangle h=f\\langle g,h\\rangle_{\\Lambda^\\circ}$; a Gabor atom satisfies $p_g={}_\\Lambda\\langle g,g\\rangle$, with $p_g^2=p_g=p_g^*$ exactly when $g\\langle g,g\\rangle_{\\Lambda^\\circ}=g$, and the canonical tight atom $h_0=S_{g,g}^{-1/2}g$ yields such a projection, existing for rectangular $\\Lambda=\\alpha\\mathbb{Z}\\times\\beta\\mathbb{Z}$ precisely when $\\alpha\\beta<1$."
  2091,Linus Hamann,"https://web.math.princeton.edu/~lhamann/Compatibility_Presentation.pdf",princeton.edu,math.NT,math.NT math.RT cs.NI mathematics computer-science langlands-program networking automorphic-forms p-adic-groups,null,null,null,"Linus Hamann, compatibility of the Fargues–Scholze and Gan–Takeda local Langlands parameters for $\\mathrm{GSp}_4$ and its inner form in the stated unramified $p>2$ cases. Key computation: for a stable supercuspidal parameter $\\phi$ with packets $\\Pi_\\phi(G)=\\{\\pi\\}$ and $\\Pi_\\phi(J)=\\{\\rho\\}$, local-shtuka cohomology is $R\\Gamma_c(G,b,\\mu)[\\pi]\\simeq\\rho\\otimes(\\mathrm{std}\\circ\\phi)^\\vee\\otimes|\\cdot|^{-3/2}[-3]$ and $R\\Gamma_c(G,b,\\mu)[\\rho]\\simeq\\pi\\otimes(\\mathrm{std}\\circ\\phi)\\otimes|\\cdot|^{-3/2}[-3]$; for endoscopic $\\mathrm{std}\\circ\\phi\\simeq\\phi_1\\oplus\\phi_2$, the two packet members pair with the corresponding $\\phi_i$ summands."
  2092,Guozhen Wang (joint with Ruochuan Liu),"https://pouiyter.github.io/CIslides.pdf",github.io,math.AT,math.AT math.CT math.KT math.OA mathematics topological-hochschild-homology descent-theory homological-algebra noncommutative-geometry slides talk github,null,null,null,"Guozhen Wang (joint with Ruochuan Liu), topological cyclic homology of local fields: computes the descent spectral sequences for $THH$, $TP$, and $TC$ of a local field's integers. Key computation: for $K/\\mathbb{Q}_p$ with ramification index $e$ and $d=[K(\\zeta_p):K]$, the slides obtain a Bott class $\\beta\\in E_2^{0,2d}(TC(\\mathcal{O}_K);\\mathbb{F}_p)$ and $E_2^{0,*}\\cong\\mathbb{F}_p[\\beta]$, $E_2^{1,*}\\cong\\mathbb{F}_p[\\beta]\\{\\lambda,\\gamma\\}\\oplus k[\\beta]\\{\\alpha_i^{(j)}\\mid1\\le i\\le e,1\\le j\\le d\\}$, $E_2^{2,*}\\cong\\mathbb{F}_p[\\beta]\\{\\lambda\\gamma\\}$, and $E_2^{i,*}=0$ for $i\\ge3$, forcing the descent spectral sequence to collapse. https://raphaelponge.org/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/berkeley_ncg4.pdf, Raphaël Ponge, the Connes–Chern character of a finitely summable spectral triple pairs with projective-module $K$-theory to recover the Fredholm index, and for the noncommutative torus is represented by its fundamental cyclic $2$-cocycle. Key computation: for $E=eA^q$ and $k\\geq p/2$, $\\operatorname{ind}D_{\\nabla^E}=\\frac12\\operatorname{Str}(D^{-1}[D,e])^{2k+1}=\\langle\\operatorname{Ch}(D),\\operatorname{Ch}(E)\\rangle$; for $A_\\theta$, $HP_0(A_\\theta)=\\langle\\tau_0,\\tau_2\\rangle$ with $\\tau_2(a^0,a^1,a^2)=\\tau_0(a^0(\\delta_1(a^1)\\delta_2(a^2)-\\delta_2(a^1)\\delta_1(a^2)))$, giving the index pairing explicitly."
  2093,Raphaël Ponge (joint with Hang Wang),"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/programs/scientific/13-14/COSY2014/slides/Ponge.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.OA,math.OA math-ph math.AG math.QA mathematics physics noncommutative-geometry deformation-theory operator-algebras talk,null,null,null,"Raphaël Ponge (joint with Hang Wang), conformal deformation produces a twisted spectral triple whose Connes–Chern character is conformally invariant and is computed by a fixed-point local index cocycle. Key computation: $\\widehat g=k^{-2}g$ gives $(C^\\infty(M),L^2_{\\widehat g}(M,S),D_{\\widehat g})\\simeq(C^\\infty(M),L^2_g(M,S),\\sqrt{k}D_g\\sqrt{k})$ and, for the crossed product $C^\\infty(M)\\rtimes G$, $\\sigma_g(fu_\\phi)=k_\\phi^{-1}fu_\\phi$; for a $G$-invariant $g$, $\\operatorname{Ch}(D_g)_{\\sigma_g}$ is represented by $\\varphi_{2m}(f^0u_{\\phi_0},\\ldots,f^{2m}u_{\\phi_{2m}})=\\frac{(-i)^{n/2}}{(2m)!}\\sum_a(2\\pi)^{-a/2}\\int_{M_a^\\phi}\\widehat A(R^{TM_a^\\phi})\\wedge\\nu_\\phi(R^{N^\\phi})\\wedge f^0d\\widehat f^1\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge d\\widehat f^{2m}$."
  2094,Nitin Nitsure,"https://qgm.au.dk/fileadmin/www.qgm.au.dk/Events/2014/Slides_Nitin.pdf",au.dk,math.AG,math.AG math.CT math.RA mathematics homological-algebra algebraic-geometry moduli-spaces nonstandard-analysis,null,null,null,"Nitin Nitsure, cotangent-complex Ext groups classify square-zero deformation problems for schemes, morphisms, and coherent sheaves and supply Artin-style tangent–obstruction data for algebraic moduli stacks. Key computation: for a flat $f:Y\\to X$ and a square-zero extension $X\\hookrightarrow X'$ with ideal $I$, the transitivity triangle $f^*L_{X/X'}\\to L_{Y/X'}\\to L_{Y/X}\\xrightarrow{[1]}$ yields $0\\to\\operatorname{Ext}^1(L_{Y/X},f^*I)\\to\\operatorname{Exal}(Y/X',f^*I)\\to\\operatorname{End}(f^*I)\\xrightarrow{\\partial}\\operatorname{Ext}^2(L_{Y/X},f^*I)$; the obstruction is $\\partial(\\operatorname{id}_{f^*I})$, lifts form a torsor under $\\operatorname{Ext}^1$, and infinitesimal automorphisms are $\\operatorname{Ext}^0$."
  2095,Avi Zeff,"https://math.berkeley.edu/~avizeff/seminars/other/prismatic.pdf",berkeley.edu,math.AG,math.AG math.NT mathematics p-adic-hodge-theory prismatic-cohomology perfectoid-spaces topos-theory,null,null,null,"Avi Zeff, prisms deperfect perfectoid geometry while prismatic cohomology recovers crystalline cohomology and equips quasiregular semiperfectoid rings with an explicit Nygaard filtration. Key computation: $R\\mapsto(A_{\\inf}(R),\\ker\\theta)$ and $(A,I)\\mapsto A/I$ give inverse equivalences between perfectoid rings and perfect prisms; for a crystalline prism $(A,I)$, $R\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{crys}}(R/A)\\simeq\\varphi^*\\Delta_{R/A}=\\Delta_{R/A}\\widehat\\otimes^L_{A,\\varphi_A}A$, while for quasiregular semiperfectoid $R$ the site has initial prism $(\\Delta_R^{\\mathrm{init}},(d))$, so $\\Delta_R=\\Delta_R^{\\mathrm{init}}$ is concentrated in degree $0$ and $N^{\\geq i}\\Delta_R=\\varphi^{-1}(d^i\\Delta_R)$."
  2096,Raphaël Ponge,"https://raphaelponge.org/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/berkeley_ncg1.pdf",raphaelponge.org,math.AP,math.AP math.DG math.OA math-ph mathematics physics differential-geometry microlocal-analysis noncommutative-geometry,null,null,null,"Raphaël Ponge, Connes' trace theorem identifies the Dixmier-trace noncommutative integral of an order-$-n$ pseudodifferential operator with its Wodzicki residue, recovering Riemannian volume spectrally. Key computation: Weyl's law gives $\\lambda_k(\\Delta_g)\\sim(k/(c_n\\operatorname{Vol}_gM))^{2/n}$, hence $\\Delta_g^{-n/2}$ is measurable and $\\operatorname{Tr}_\\omega(\\Delta_g^{-n/2})=c_n\\operatorname{Vol}_g(M)$; more generally, for $P\\in\\Psi^{-n}(M)$, $\\operatorname{Tr}_\\omega(P)=\\frac1n\\operatorname{Res}(P)$, so $\\operatorname{Tr}_\\omega(f\\Delta_g^{-n/2})=c_n\\int_M f(x)\\sqrt{g(x)}\\,d^nx$."
  2097,Raphaël Ponge,"https://raphaelponge.org/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/berkeley_ncg2.pdf",raphaelponge.org,math.OA,math.OA math.RA math-ph math.DG mathematics physics linear-algebra noncommutative-geometry spectral-geometry,null,null,null,"Raphaël Ponge, the irrational noncommutative torus admits an explicit Dirac spectral triple, while conformal weights produce twisted spectral triples and a conformally invariant zeta value. Key computation: $A_\\theta=\\{\\sum_{m,n}a_{m,n}U^mV^n:(a_{m,n})\\in\\mathcal{S}(\\mathbb{Z}^2)\\}$ with $VU=e^{-2\\pi i\\theta}UV$, $\\partial=\\delta_1+i\\delta_2$, and $D=\\begin{psmallmatrix}0&\\partial^*\\\\ \\partial&0\\end{psmallmatrix}$ is isospectral to $\\partial+\\partial^*$ on $\\mathbb{T}^2$; a Weyl factor $k>0$ gives $\\sigma(a)=k^{-1}ak$ and the twisted triple $(A_\\theta^{\\mathrm{op}},H_\\varphi,D_\\varphi)_\\sigma$, for which $\\zeta(\\Delta_\\varphi;0)$ is independent of $\\varphi(a)=\\tau_0(ak^{-2})$."
  2098,Raphaël Ponge,"https://raphaelponge.org/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/berkeley_ncg3.pdf",raphaelponge.org,math.AT,math.AT math.DG mathematics algebraic-topology differential-geometry topological-hochschild-homology,null,null,null,"Raphaël Ponge, the $(b,B)$ mixed complex identifies periodic cyclic homology of smooth functions with de Rham cohomology and gives an explicit cyclic-homology Chern character for projective modules. Key computation: the HKR map $\\alpha(f^0\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes f^m)=\\frac1{m!}f^0df^1\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge df^m$ intertwines $(b,B)$ with $(0,d)$, yielding $HP_0(C^\\infty(M))\\simeq H^{\\mathrm{ev}}(M)$ and $HP_1(C^\\infty(M))\\simeq H^{\\mathrm{odd}}(M)$; for $E\\simeq eA^q$, $\\operatorname{Ch}_{2k}(e)=(-1)^k\\frac{(2k)!}{k!}\\operatorname{Tr}((e-\\frac12)\\otimes e^{\\otimes 2k})$ satisfies $(b+B)\\operatorname{Ch}(e)=0$ and depends only on $E$."
  2099,Thomas Nikolaus,"https://www.uni-muenster.de/IVV5WS/WebHop/user/nikolaus/Files/ICM_Slides.pdf",uni-muenster.de,math.AT,math.AT math.KT math.AG math.NT mathematics topological-hochschild-homology prismatic-cohomology algebraic-topology computability,null,null,null,"Thomas Nikolaus, Frobenius maps in higher algebra turn topological Hochschild homology into a computable cyclotomic object and identify prismatic graded pieces of relative $\\mathrm{THH}$ and $\\mathrm{TC}$. Key computation: the Tate diagonal and multiplication give $\\varphi_p:R\\to(R^{\\otimes_S p})^{tC_p}\\to R^{tC_p}$ and $\\varphi_p:\\mathrm{THH}(R)\\to\\mathrm{THH}(R)^{tC_p}$; for connective $R$, $\\mathrm{TC}(R)\\simeq\\operatorname{map}_{\\mathrm{CycSp}}(\\mathbbm{1},\\mathrm{THH}(R))$, while for a prism $(A,I)$ and $R=A/I$ ultimate Bökstedt periodicity computes $\\mathrm{THH}_*(R/S_A)\\cong I^n/I^{n+1}$ in degree $2n$ and $0$ in odd degrees, and the complete filtration on $\\mathrm{TC}(R/S_A)$ has graded pieces $\\mathbb{Z}_p(i)(R/A)[2i]$."
  2100,Seokbeom Yoon (joint with Stavros Garoufalidis),"https://aritopo.github.io/2022-12/Yoon.pdf",github.io,math.RA,math.RA math.NA mathematics linear-algebra talk github,null,null,null,"Seokbeom Yoon (joint with Stavros Garoufalidis), twisted Neumann–Zagier matrices compute twisted loop invariants of finite cyclic covers and force higher-loop asymptotic multiplicativity. Key computation: from $A(t)=G(t)-G'(t)$ and $B(t)=G''(t)-G(t)$ one has $A(t)B(1/t)^T=B(t)A(1/t)^T$; the twisted one-loop invariant is $\\Phi_{1,M}(t)=\\frac12\\det(A(t)\\operatorname{diag}(z_1'',\\ldots,z_N'')+B(t)\\operatorname{diag}(z_1^{-1},\\ldots,z_N^{-1}))$, and for the $n$-cyclic cover $\\Phi_{1,M^{(n)}}=\\prod_{\\omega^n=1}\\Phi_{1,M}(\\omega)$; for $M=S^3\\setminus4_1$, $\\Phi_{2,M^{(n)}}=\\frac{55\\sqrt3}{1512}n-\\frac{8\\sqrt3\\,n}{63(1-\\lambda^n)}+\\frac{8\\sqrt3\\,n}{63(1-\\lambda^n)^2}\\sim\\frac{55\\sqrt3}{1512}n$, where $\\lambda=(7+3\\sqrt5)/2$ is a root of $\\Phi_{1,M}(t)$."
  2101,William Balderrama,"https://bpb-us-e1.wpmucdn.com/s.wayne.edu/dist/2/145/files/2022/02/WBalderrama20220224.pdf",wpmucdn.com,math.AT,math.AT math.NT mathematics chromatic-homotopy-theory arithmetic-geometry descent-theory formal-groups,null,null,null,"William Balderrama, Borel-completed Morava theory supplies an equivariant $K(n)$-local sphere whose localization agrees with the explicit cofree construction, with a descent route to $RO(G)$-graded computations. Key computation: for a finite $p$-group the Segal conjecture gives $S_G^{\\wedge_p}\\simeq b_G(S)$ and $b_G(S_{K(n)})=b_G(E_n)^{h\\mathbb{G}_n}$, with $H_c^*(K;\\pi_?b_G(E_n))\\Longrightarrow\\pi_?b_G(E_n^{hK})$ and $\\pi_\\alpha b_G(E_n)=E_n^0\\operatorname{Th}(\\alpha)$; taking $K(n)_G=b_G(K(n))$ yields $L_{b_G(K(n))}S_G\\simeq b_G(S_{K(n)})$, while at height one $KU_G/(p)\\simeq b_G(KU/(p))$ and the explicit $C_2$ calculation has $KO_{C_2}/(\\eta_{C_2})\\simeq KR$."
  2102,N,"https://pages.uoregon.edu/ncp/Courses/KThyReading_W24_Web/IntroLectureSlides/Talk_slides.pdf",uoregon.edu,math.KT,math.KT math.AT mathematics k-theory functional-analysis operator-algebras,null,null,null,"N. Christopher Phillips, algebraic, topological, and operator-algebraic $K$-theory share the projective-module construction, which becomes a periodic cohomology theory for Banach algebras. Key computation: Swan's equivalence $\\operatorname{Vect}_{\\mathbb{C}}(X)\\simeq\\operatorname{Proj}_{\\mathrm{fg}}(C(X))$ identifies $K^0(X)=K_0(C(X))=G(V(X))$; concretely $V(A)$ is the monoid of stable Murray--von Neumann classes of idempotents in $M_\\infty(A)$, and for $A=\\mathcal{L}(\\ell^2)$ one has $V(A)\\cong\\{0,1,2,\\ldots,\\infty\\}$ with $n+\\infty=\\infty$, hence $K_0(A)=0$; Bott periodicity $K_0(SA)\\cong K_1(A)$ and $K_1(SA)\\cong K_0(A)$ then converts an extension $0\\to J\\to A\\to A/J\\to0$ into the six-term exact sequence in $K$-theory."
  2103,Raphaël Ponge,"https://raphaelponge.org/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/tsinghua2.pdf",raphaelponge.org,math.OA,math.OA math.RA math.KT math-ph mathematics physics noncommutative-geometry abstract-algebra commutative-algebra index-theory,null,null,null,"Raphaël Ponge, the Atiyah--Singer formula becomes the $K$-theoretic index pairing of the Dirac spectral triple, extending verbatim to finitely generated projective modules over a noncommutative algebra. Key computation: for a compact even-dimensional spin manifold and a bundle $E$, the twisted Dirac operator satisfies $\\operatorname{ind}D_E=(2\\pi i)^{-n/2}\\int_M\\widehat A(R^M)\\wedge\\operatorname{Ch}(F^E)$; Serre--Swan writes $E=eC^\\infty(M)^q$ and identifies $D_E$ with $e(D\\otimes1)e$, so the spectral triple $(A,H,D)$ sends $eA^q$ to the Fredholm operator $D_E=e(D\\otimes1)e$ and defines $\\operatorname{ind}_D:K_0(A)\\to\\mathbb{Z}$, recovering $\\operatorname{ind}_D[E]=(2\\pi i)^{-n/2}\\langle\\widehat A(R^M)^\\vee,E\\rangle$ when $A=C^\\infty(M)$."
  2104,Eva Viehmann (joint with Kieu Hieu Nguyen),"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/45/34/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.AG,math.AG math.AP math.NT math.SG mathematics geometric-invariant-theory p-adic-hodge-theory pde talk,null,null,null,"Eva Viehmann (joint with Kieu Hieu Nguyen), modifications of the trivial $G$-bundle on the Fargues--Fontaine curve admit a canonical Harder--Narasimhan reduction, stratifying the $B_{\\mathrm{dR}}^+$-Grassmannian and relating weak admissibility to Newton strata. Key computation: Beauville--Laszlo gluing gives $\\mathrm{BL}:\\mathrm{Gr}_G\\to\\mathrm{Bun}_G$, and for $x\\in\\mathrm{Gr}_G(C)$ the unique parabolic $P\\subset G$ has a $P$-dominant slope vector $v_P$ with semistable Levi modification; this defines the lower semicontinuous map $\\mathrm{HN}:|\\mathrm{Gr}_{G,\\mu}|\\to B(G,\\mu)$, satisfying $\\mathrm{HN}(x)\\le[(b')^{-1}]$ on the Newton stratum where $\\mathcal{E}_{1,x}\\simeq\\mathcal{E}_{b'}$, while for classical $x\\in\\mathrm{Gr}_{G,\\mu}(K)$ equality $\\mathrm{HN}(x)=[(b')^{-1}]=\\mathrm{HN}_{\\mathrm{DOR}}(\\mathrm{BB}_\\mu(x))$ identifies the classical semistable and basic loci."
  2105,Xu Shen (joint with Miaofen Chen and Laurent Fargues),"https://hkumath.hku.hk/~imr/event/SRC/files/slides_Xu_Shen.pdf",hku.hk,math.NT,math.NT math.AG mathematics p-adic-hodge-theory talk,null,null,null,"Xu Shen (joint with Miaofen Chen and Laurent Fargues), the Fargues--Rapoport conjecture classifies exactly when the admissible and weakly admissible $p$-adic period domains coincide. Key computation: a point $x\\in\\mathcal{F}(G,\\mu)(C)$ modifies the Fargues bundle $\\mathcal{E}_b$ at $\\infty$ to $\\mathcal{E}_{b,x}$, giving $\\mathcal{F}(G,\\mu,b)^a(C)=\\{x\\mid\\mathcal{E}_{b,x}\\simeq\\mathcal{E}_1\\}$; for basic $[b_0]\\in B(G,\\mu)$, one proves $\\mathcal{F}(G,\\mu,b_0)^a=\\mathcal{F}(G,\\mu,b_0)^{wa}$ iff $(G,\\{\\mu\\})$ is fully Hodge--Newton decomposable, meaning each nonbasic $[b']\\in B(G,\\mu)$ has a Newton--Hodge contact point that is a break of the Newton polygon and reduces the datum to a proper Levi, with examples including $(\\mathrm{GL}_h,(1,0,\\ldots,0))$ and $(\\mathrm{GSp}_4,(1,1,0,0))$."
  2106,Kiran S,"https://kskedlaya.org/slides/hopkins2011.pdf",kskedlaya.org,math.NT,math.NT math.AG math.DG mathematics etale-cohomology differential-geometry p-adic-numbers geometric-invariant-theory,null,null,null,"Kiran S. Kedlaya, the Fargues--Fontaine curve converts $p$-adic Galois representations and relative étale local systems into semistable degree-zero vector bundles from which étale and de Rham realizations can be recovered. Key computation: with $P=\\bigoplus_{n\\ge0}B^{\\varphi=p^n}$, $X_{FF}=\\operatorname{Proj}(P)$ is regular, connected, noetherian, and one-dimensional, and $\\operatorname{Rep}_{\\mathbb{Q}_p}^{\\mathrm{cont}}(G_{\\mathbb{Q}_p})\\simeq\\operatorname{Bun}^{\\Gamma,\\mathrm{ss},\\mu=0}(X_{FF})$; for a perfect Banach $\\mathbb{F}_p$-algebra $R$, the relative construction $P_R=\\bigoplus_{n\\ge0}B_R^{\\varphi=p^n}$ yields natural equivalences between étale $\\mathbb{Q}_p$-local systems on $\\mathcal{M}(R)$, those on $\\mathcal{M}(\\widetilde R)$, and fibrewise semistable degree-zero bundles on $\\operatorname{Proj}(P_R)$, while the global $\\Gamma$-fixed sections and the completed local fraction field at the de Rham point recover étale and de Rham cohomology respectively."
  2107,J,"https://webhomes.maths.ed.ac.uk/~jpridham/Kslide.pdf",ac.uk,math.AT,math.AT math.AG math.AC math.CA mathematics commutative-algebra complex-geometry differential-cohomology functional-analysis,null,null,null,"J. P. Pridham, smooth pro-Banach completion of algebraic $K$-theory is computed by real Deligne cohomology, refining the gap between discrete algebraic and topological $K$-theory. Key computation: differentiable cohomology gives $\\operatorname{Hom}(K^{\\mathrm{Ban}}(A),V)\\simeq P\\,R\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{diff}}(B\\mathrm{GL}(A),V)$; for $A=\\mathcal{O}_X(U)$ on a polydisc, $U_n(\\mathbb{C})\\backslash\\mathrm{GL}_n(A)$ is contractible, so this becomes relative continuous Lie algebra cohomology, whose primitive chains reduce by Loday--Quillen and Fréchet HKR to $\\widehat C_\\bullet(A)\\simeq\\bigoplus_{p>0}(\\Omega_A^{2p-\\bullet}/F^{p+1})$; adjoining the $\\mathfrak u$-contribution $(2\\pi i)^p\\mathbb{R}$ and sheafifying yields $K^{\\mathrm{Ban}}(X)\\simeq\\bigoplus_{p\\ge0}R\\Gamma_D(X,\\mathbb{R}(p))[2p]$ for compact complex $X$."
  2108,Rick Jardine,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/26/82/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.AT,math.AT mathematics algebraic-topology category-theory homotopy-theory,null,null,null,"Rick Jardine, persistent homotopy admits a controlled homotopy theory in which Rips stability is preserved quantitatively by homotopy-theoretic gluing. Key computation: a finite metric sample $X$ defines the system $V_*(X):s\\mapsto B P_s(X)$, and $d_H(X,Y)<r$ produces a homotopy interleaving $V_s(X)\\to V_s(Y)\\to V_{s+2r}(X)$, hence a $2r$-equivalence on every $\\pi_n$; if a sectionwise cofibration $A\\to B$ is an $r$-equivalence, every pushout $C\\to B\\cup_A C$ is a $2r$-equivalence, so for $X\\subset Y$ and $X\\subset W$ the comparison $V_*(W)\\cup_{V_*(X)}V_*(Y)\\to V_*(W\\cup Y)$ is a $6r$-equivalence, giving a quantitative excision theorem for the Vietoris--Rips functor."
  2109,Michael Harris (joint with Wee Teck Gan and Will Sawin),"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/40/55/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.NT,math.NT math.RT mathematics l-functions p-adic-groups function-fields random-matrix-theory talk,null,null,null,"Michael Harris (joint with Wee Teck Gan and Will Sawin), purity and globalization force ramification of Genestier--Lafforgue parameters of supercuspidal representations over local function fields. Key computation: for $F=k((t))$, a pure supercuspidal $\\pi$ compactly induced from $U=G(\\mathcal{O}_F)$ is globalized over $K=k(t)$ by the Poincaré series $P_\\phi(g)=\\sum_{\\gamma\\in G(K)}\\phi(\\gamma g)$ with $\\phi_z=\\phi_\\pi$ for $z\\in\\mathbb{G}_m(k)$, $\\phi_x=1_{G(\\mathcal{O}_x)}$ off $|\\mathbb{P}^1(k)|$, $\\phi_\\infty=1_{I_{\\infty,+}}$, and $\\phi_0=\\chi_k$, whose support gives $P_\\phi(1)=1$; if $L(\\pi)$ were unramified, purity would extend $L(\\Pi)_\\sigma$ to a punctually pure tame local system on $\\mathbb{G}_m$, hence a sum of finite-order tame characters, contradicting variation of $\\chi_k$, so $L(\\pi)$ is ramified and, for induction from a sufficiently small compact, wildly ramified."
  2110,Kęstutis Česnavičius,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/45/43/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.AG,math.AG math.NT mathematics commutative-algebra p-adic-hodge-theory perfectoid-spaces prismatic-cohomology schemes,null,null,null,"Kęstutis Česnavičius, the perfectoid approach proves purity for the Brauer group and for flat cohomology with finite flat coefficients. Key computation: for a perfectoid ring $A$ and a closed $Z\\subset\\operatorname{Spec}(A[1/p])$ of codimension at least $2$, prismatic Dieudonné theory sends a finite locally free commutative $p$-power-order group $G$ to $M(G)=\\operatorname{Ext}^1(G,\\mathcal{O}_{\\Prism})$ equipped with semilinear $F,V$ satisfying $FV=p$; $p$-complete arc hyperdescent reduces the resulting purity comparison to rank-one valuation rings, yielding $R\\Gamma(A,G)\\simeq R\\Gamma(\\operatorname{Spec}(A[1/p])\\setminus Z,G)$ and, for a regular local ring $(R,\\mathfrak m)$, $H^i_{\\mathfrak m}(R,G)=0$ for $i<\\dim R$."
  2111,Yves André,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/45/35/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.AG,math.AG math.NT mathematics schemes p-adic-hodge-theory perfectoid-spaces etale-cohomology p-adic-numbers,null,null,null,"Yves André, every finite covering of a regular scheme is an fpqc covering, equivalently every finite extension of a regular ring is dominated by a faithfully flat algebra. Key computation: for $R=\\mathbb{Z}_p[[x_2,\\ldots,x_n]]$ and finite reduced $R\\subset S$, adjoining all $p$-power roots gives the perfectoid algebra $R_\\infty=\\mathbb{Z}_p[p^{1/p^\\infty}][[x_2^{1/p^\\infty},\\ldots,x_n^{1/p^\\infty}]]^{\\wedge}$; after choosing $g$ with $S[1/pg]$ étale over $R[1/pg]$, perfectoid Abhyankar makes $S_\\infty^\\infty$ $(pg)^{1/p^\\infty}$-almost finite étale over $R_\\infty^\\infty$, and $T=\\Sigma^{-1}(\\prod_{\\mathbb N}S_\\infty^\\infty/\\bigoplus_{\\mathbb N}S_\\infty^\\infty)$ removes almostness to produce a perfectoid $S$-algebra faithfully flat over $R$."
  2112,Jessica Fintzen,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/45/41/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.NT,math.NT math.RT mathematics p-adic-groups p-adic-numbers automorphic-forms algebraic-combinatorics,null,null,null,"Jessica Fintzen, explicitly constructed omni-supercuspidal types force arbitrary algebraic automorphic forms to be congruent modulo $p^m$ to forms supercuspidal at $p$. Key computation: when $p$ exceeds the Coxeter number of $G$, there are nested compact opens $U_{p,1}\\supset U_{p,2}\\supset\\cdots$ and maps $\\lambda_m:U_{p,m}\\twoheadrightarrow\\mathbb{Z}/p^m\\mathbb{Z}$ such that $(U_{p,m},\\psi\\circ\\lambda_m)$ is a supercuspidal type for every nontrivial $\\psi$; consequently $M(U_{p,m}U^p,\\mathbb{Z}_p)/p^m\\simeq M(U_{p,m}U^p,A_m)$ Hecke-equivariantly, and every automorphic representation contributing to $M(U_{p,m}U^p,A_m)\\otimes_{\\mathbb{Z}_p}\\mathbb{Q}_p$ has supercuspidal local factor at $p$."
  2113,David Vogan,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/2/65/23/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.AP,math.AP math.CT math.LO math.FA mathematics category-theory nonstandard-analysis pde,null,null,null,"David Vogan, real parabolic induction, cohomological induction, and weakly unipotent representations reduce the construction of large portions of the unitary dual to unitary data on Levi subgroups. Key computation: for a $\\theta$-stable $P=LU$ and an $(\\mathfrak l,L\\cap K)$-module $\\pi_L$ of infinitesimal character $\\gamma=\\gamma_z+\\gamma_d$, Zuckerman's functors satisfy $R^i(\\pi_L)=0$ for $i<s$ and $R^s(\\pi_L)$ is unitary when $\\pi_L$ is weakly unipotent unitary and $\\gamma_z$ is weakly antidominant on coroots of $U$; for $G=\\mathrm{GL}(2n)$, $L=\\mathrm{GL}(n)\\times\\mathrm{GL}(n)$, and $\\pi_L=(1\\otimes|\\det|^a)\\otimes(1\\otimes|\\det|^b)$, one has $\\gamma_z=[(a,\\ldots,a)(b,\\ldots,b)]$ and the required central condition is exactly $a-b\\ge0$."
  2114,Mark Goresky (with Robert MacPherson,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/2/46/44/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.NT,math.NT math.GN math.RT mathematics automorphic-forms l-functions point-set-topology,null,null,null,"Mark Goresky (with Robert MacPherson, Günter Harder, and Robert Kottwitz), weighted cohomology turns Arthur's topological trace formula into a Lefschetz fixed-point formula for Hecke correspondences on arithmetic locally symmetric spaces. Key computation: the middle-weight truncation on the reductive Borel--Serre compactification satisfies $R\\pi_*W^\\mu H^*(X^{\\mathrm{RBS}})\\simeq IH^*(X^{\\mathrm{BB}})\\simeq H_{(2)}^*(X)$; for a hyperbolic fixed component $F$, its local term is $\\operatorname{lc}(F)=\\sum_i(-1)^i\\operatorname{Tr}(C^*\\mid H^i_{\\{\\mathrm{expanding}\\}}(U,A^\\bullet))$, and summing these boundary-stratum contributions recovers Arthur's $L^2$ Lefschetz number $L(C(h))=\\sum_i(-1)^i\\operatorname{Tr}(q_*p^*\\mid H^i_{(2)}(X;E_\\rho))$ and the discrete-series character factor."
  2115,Guozhen Wang (joint with Ruochuan Liu),"https://pouiyter.github.io/prismslice-4.pdf",github.io,math.NT,math.NT math.AG math.AT math.CT mathematics p-adic-numbers descent-theory topological-hochschild-homology algebraic-topology talk github,null,null,null,"Guozhen Wang (joint with Ruochuan Liu), prismatic descent computes the mod-$p$ topological cyclic homology of $\\mathbb{Z}_p$ from a perfectoid resolution. Key computation: for $R=\\mathbb{Z}_p[p^{1/p^\\infty}]^{\\wedge}$, the descent Hopf algebroid gives $E_2^{*,*,*}=\\mathbb{F}_p[f,g,\\sigma^{\\pm1}]/(g^2)$, and with $\\psi(k)=(p^k-1)/(p-1)$ its Tate differentials are $d_{p\\psi(k)}(f^{p^{k-1}})=g f^{p^{k-1}+\\psi(k)-1}$; the survivors recover $(\\mathrm{TC}/p)_*(\\mathbb{Z}_p)$ as a free $\\mathbb{F}_p[v_1]$-module on $\\{1,\\gamma,a_0\\gamma,a_m\\mid0\\le m\\le p-1\\}$, in stems $0,-1,2p-2$, and $2p-2m-1$ respectively."
  2116,Neil Strickland,"https://strickland1.org/talks/chromatic_talk.pdf",strickland1.org,math.AT,math.AT math.CT mathematics chromatic-homotopy-theory category-theory stable-homotopy-theory algebraic-topology,null,null,null,"Neil Strickland, Morava $K$-theories classify the thick subcategories of finite spectra and organize stable homotopy into chromatic layers. Key computation: a finite $p$-local spectrum $X$ has type $n$ when $K(p,n)_*(X)\\ne0$ but $K(p,m)_*(X)=0$ for $m<n$, defining $\\mathcal{F}(p,n)$; every thick subcategory is one of the $\\mathcal{F}(p,n)$, while periodicity supplies $v:\\Sigma^dX\\to X$ inducing an isomorphism on $K(p,n)_*(X)$, and chromatic convergence decomposes $\\pi_*X$ into layers controlled by $K(p,n)$ with eventual periods $2(p^n-1)p^k$."
  2117,Gautier Ponsinet,"https://maine-quebec.mat.ulaval.ca/20/slides/Gautier_Ponsinet.pdf",ulaval.ca,math.AG,math.AG math.NT math.DG mathematics p-adic-hodge-theory differential-geometry perfectoid-spaces abelian-varieties,null,null,null,"Gautier Ponsinet, universal norms of de Rham representations over perfectoid extensions are controlled by the positive Hodge--Tate part via the Fargues--Fontaine curve. Key computation: if $V$ is de Rham with Hodge--Tate weights at most $1$ and $V_0\\subset V$ is minimal such that $V/V_0$ has weights at most $0$, then for $T_0=T\\cap V_0$ and perfectoid $\\widehat L$, $H_e^1(L,V/T)=\\operatorname{Im}(H^1(L,V_0/T_0)\\to H^1(L,V/T))$; for an abelian variety, $\\widetilde{\\widehat A}=\\varprojlim_{[p]}\\widehat A\\simeq\\Gamma(X_{FF},\\mathcal{E}(\\widetilde{\\widehat A}))$ with all Harder--Narasimhan slopes positive, so the bundle classification gives $H^1(L,\\widetilde{\\widehat A})=0$ and recovers the Coates--Greenberg description of the Kummer image."
  2118,Christian Sattler and David Wärn,"https://dwarn.se/slides/7wftop.pdf",dwarn.se,math.LO,math.LO cs.LO math.AT math.CA mathematics computer-science mathematical-logic type-theory algebraic-topology category-theory,null,null,null,"Christian Sattler and David Wärn, a short proposition-valued axiom system inside homotopy type theory reconstructs the basic calculus of univalent $\\infty$-categories. Key computation: defining $[0]=\\mathbf{1}$ and $[n+1]=\\operatorname{Fun}([n],I)$, the Segal pushout $[2]\\simeq I\\amalg_{\\mathbf{1}}I$ makes $\\operatorname{Map}([2],C)\\simeq\\operatorname{Map}(I,C)\\times_{\\operatorname{Ob}(C)}\\operatorname{Map}(I,C)$ and hence defines composition; inductively $[n]$ is the colimit of its spine, giving $\\operatorname{Map}([n],C)\\simeq\\sum_{a:\\operatorname{Fin}(n+1)\\to\\operatorname{Ob}(C)}\\prod_{i:\\operatorname{Fin}(n)}C(a_i,a_{i+1})$, while the Rezk axiom identifies $(x\\simeq y)\\simeq(x=y)$."
  2119,Gabriele Vezzosi,"https://indico.math.cnrs.fr/event/2518/attachments/754/844/09102013a.pdf",cnrs.fr,math.AG,math.AG mathematics algebraic-geometry deformation-theory derived-algebraic-geometry enumerative-geometry,null,null,null,"Gabriele Vezzosi, derived algebraic geometry realizes the reduced obstruction theory for stable maps to a K3 surface as the cotangent complex of a quasi-smooth derived Deligne--Mumford stack. Key computation: for a stable map $f:C\\to S$, the standard obstruction space fits into $H^1(C,T_C)\\to H^1(C,f^*T_S)\\to\\operatorname{Obs}_f\\to0$, and the symplectic identification $T_S\\simeq\\Omega_S^1$ followed by $df$ gives a surjection $a:\\operatorname{Obs}_f\\to H^1(C,\\omega_C)\\simeq\\mathbb{C}$, forcing $[\\overline{M}_g(S;\\beta)]_{\\mathrm{std}}^{\\mathrm{vir}}=0$; removing this factor produces a quasi-smooth $R\\overline{M}_g^{\\mathrm{red}}(S;\\beta)$ with obstruction space $\\ker a$ and the nontrivial reduced virtual class."
  2120,Jared Weinstein,"https://swc-math.github.io/aws/2017/2017WeinsteinLecture4Slides.pdf",github.io,math.NT,math.NT math.AG mathematics p-adic-numbers perfectoid-spaces p-adic-hodge-theory etale-cohomology github,null,null,null,"Jared Weinstein, diamonds turn analytic adic spaces over $\\mathbb{Z}_p$ into pro-étale sheaves and identify perfectoid spaces over $\\mathbb{Q}_p$ with characteristic-$p$ perfectoid spaces over the diamond $\\operatorname{Spd}\\mathbb{Q}_p$. Key computation: $\\operatorname{Spd}\\mathbb{Q}_p=(\\operatorname{Spd}\\mathbb{Q}_p^{\\mathrm{cycl}})/\\mathbb{Z}_p^*$ and $(\\operatorname{Spd}\\mathbb{Q}_p)(S)=\\{S^\\sharp\\to\\operatorname{Spa}\\mathbb{Q}_p:(S^\\sharp)^\\flat\\simeq S\\}$; for $R$ a $\\mathbb{Q}_p$-vector space the associated diamond $\\widetilde H_C^\\diamond(R)=H^0(X_{R^\\flat},\\mathcal O(1))$ fits into $0\\to\\mathbb{Q}_p(1)\\to\\widetilde H_C^\\diamond\\to\\mathbb A_C^{1,\\diamond}\\to0$, and the construction yields $\\{\\text{perfectoid spaces}/\\mathbb{Q}_p\\}\\simeq\\{X/\\mathbb F_p\\text{ perfectoid}:X\\to\\operatorname{Spd}\\mathbb{Q}_p\\}$."
  2121,Charles Rezk,"https://rezk.web.illinois.edu/baltimore-2010-power-ops-handout.pdf",illinois.edu,math.AT,math.AT math.NT math.RT math.AG mathematics arithmetic-geometry chromatic-homotopy-theory formal-groups lie-theory,null,null,null,"Charles Rezk, height-$2$ Morava $E$-theory at $p=2$ gives an explicit twisted bialgebra of power operations, making $\\pi_0$ of every $K(2)$-local commutative $E$-algebra an amplified $\\Gamma$-ring. Key computation: for $E_0=\\mathbb Z_2[[a]]$, additive operations $Q_0,Q_1,Q_2$ satisfy $Q_1Q_0=2Q_2Q_1-2Q_0Q_2$, $Q_2Q_0=Q_0Q_1+aQ_0Q_2-2Q_1Q_2$, and $Q_0(x)=x^2+2\\theta(x)$; their Cartan formulas are encoded by $\\Delta(Q_0)=Q_0\\otimes Q_0+2Q_1\\otimes Q_2+2Q_2\\otimes Q_1$ and analogous displayed formulas for $\\Delta(Q_1),\\Delta(Q_2)$, while $\\Gamma$ has left $\\mathbb Z_2[[a]]$-basis $Q_0^iQ_{j_1}\\cdots Q_{j_r}$ with $j_k\\in\\{1,2\\}$, yielding $\\pi_0L_{K(2)}\\mathbb P_E(E)\\simeq \\widehat F_{(2,a)}$ for the free amplified $\\Gamma$-ring $F$."
  2122,Agnès Beaudry,"https://cpb-us-e1.wpmucdn.com/s.wayne.edu/dist/0/60/files/2019/11/Beaudry-PartIV.pdf",wpmucdn.com,math.AT,math.AT mathematics chromatic-homotopy-theory formal-groups deformation-theory algebraic-topology,null,null,null,"Agnès Beaudry, the extended Morava stabilizer group realizes the $K(n)$-local sphere as homotopy fixed points of Morava $E$-theory and turns the local Adams--Novikov spectral sequence into continuous group cohomology. Key computation: Lubin--Tate deformation theory gives $(E_n)_0=\\mathbb Z_{p^n}[[u_1,\\ldots,u_{n-1}]]$ representing deformations of the height-$n$ Honda formal group and $\\mathbb G_n=\\{(f,i):i\\in\\operatorname{Gal}(\\mathbb F_{p^n}/\\mathbb F_p),\\ f:i^*\\Gamma_n\\xrightarrow\\sim\\Gamma_n\\}$; Goerss--Hopkins--Miller lifts its action to $E_n$, and $(E_n)_*E_n\\simeq\\operatorname{Hom}_c(\\mathbb G_n,(E_n)_*)$ promotes to $L_{K(n)}E_n^{\\bullet+1}\\simeq F_c((\\mathbb G_n)^{\\bullet+},E_n)$, hence $L_{K(n)}S^0\\simeq E_n^{h\\mathbb G_n}$ with $H_c^*(\\mathbb G_n,(E_n)_*)\\Longrightarrow\\pi_*L_{K(n)}S^0$."
  2123,Dominic Joyce,"https://people.maths.ox.ac.uk/joyce/DAG2022/DAG1+2.pdf",ac.uk,math.AG,math.AG math.GT math.DG mathematics curves differential-topology schemes,null,null,null,"Dominic Joyce, Koszul cdgas retain relations among defining equations and make derived intersections quasi-smooth with the expected virtual dimension, recovering Bézout counts without transversality. Key computation: for $p_1,\\ldots,p_n\\in\\mathbb C[x_1,\\ldots,x_m]$, the cdga $A^*=\\mathbb C[x_1,\\ldots,x_m;y_1,\\ldots,y_n]$ with $|y_i|=-1$ and $dy_i=p_i$ has $H^0(A^*)=\\mathbb C[x_1,\\ldots,x_m]/(p_1,\\ldots,p_n)$ but its $H^{-1}$ distinguishes dependent presentations such as $(x^2,y^2)$ and $(x^2,y^2,x^2+y^2)$; thus $\\operatorname{Spec}A^*$ has $\\operatorname{vdim}=m-n$, and for plane curves $C,D\\subset\\mathbb{CP}^2$ of degrees $m,n$, the derived intersection $C\\times^R_{\\mathbb{CP}^2}D$ is proper quasi-smooth of virtual dimension $0$ with $[C\\times^R_{\\mathbb{CP}^2}D]^{\\mathrm{virt}}=mn$ even when the classical intersection has dimension $1$."
  2124,Giovanni Landi,"https://gdenittis.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/08/chile-i-2016-2.pdf",wordpress.com,math.OA,math.OA math-ph math.DG math.QA mathematics physics noncommutative-geometry index-theory commutative-algebra linear-algebra blog,null,null,null,"Giovanni Landi, Connes's reconstruction theorem characterizes compact oriented spin$^c$ manifolds spectrally, with the Dirac operator recovering dimension, distance, and measure from the commutative algebra. Key computation: an $n$-dimensional spectral triple has resolvent singular values $\\mu_k(|D|)\\sim2\\pi\\bigl(k/(\\Omega_n\\operatorname{vol}M)\\bigr)^{1/n}$ and Connes's distance formula $d(p,q)=\\sup\\{|f(p)-f(q)|:f\\in C^\\infty(M),\\ \\|[D,f]\\|\\le1\\}$, while integration is the Dixmier trace $\\int_M f\\,d\\mu_g\\sim\\mathop{\\rlap{-}\\!\\int}f|D|^{-n}$; imposing the first-order and orientability relations together with the spectral conditions and multiplicity $2^{n/2}$ reconstructs $A=C^\\infty(M)$ and $D$ as a Dirac operator, whereas the noncommutative example $(A(SU_q(2)),H,D)$ is regular, $3^+$-summable, and has dimension spectrum $\\{1,2,3\\}$ and $KO$-dimension $3$."
  2125,Hal Schenck (joint with R,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/2/40/69/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.RA,math.RA math.AG math.NA mathematics linear-algebra curves talk,null,null,null,"Hal Schenck (joint with R. Di Gennaro, G. Ilardi, R. M. Miró-Roig, and J. Vallès), finite eigenschemes classify free plane curves annihilated by a fixed derivation and control singular members of pencils. Key computation: for $\\delta=P_1\\partial_x+P_2\\partial_y+P_3\\partial_z$ of degree $a$ with finite eigenscheme $\\Gamma_\\delta$ cut out by the $2\\times2$ minors of $\\begin{psmallmatrix}x&P_1\\\\y&P_2\\\\z&P_3\\end{psmallmatrix}$, the resolution $0\\to\\mathcal O_{\\mathbb P^2}(-1)\\oplus\\mathcal O_{\\mathbb P^2}(-a)\\to\\mathcal O_{\\mathbb P^2}^3\\to\\mathcal J_{\\Gamma_\\delta}(a+1)\\to0$ gives $\\operatorname{length}(\\Gamma_\\delta)=1+a+a^2$; if $f\\in R_d$, $d\\ge a+1$, and $\\delta(f)=0$, then $V(f)$ is free with exponents $(a,d-a-1)$ exactly when $f\\in I_{\\Gamma_\\delta}$, while for a pencil generated by reduced $f\\in R_n,g\\in R_m$, the canonical derivation $\\delta_{f,g}=\\det(\\nabla f,\\nabla g,\\nabla)$ bounds singular members away from the base locus by $(n-1)^2+(n-1)(m-1)+(m-1)^2$."
  2126,Nathan Pagliaroli (joint with Hamed Hessam,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/73/68/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,cs.LO,cs.LO math-ph math.AG math.OA computer-science physics mathematics birational-geometry fuzzy-logic linear-algebra noncommutative-geometry talk,null,null,null,"Nathan Pagliaroli (joint with Hamed Hessam, Masoud Khalkhali, and Luuk Verhoeven), quartic random fuzzy spectral triples reduce to explicit bi-tracial matrix models whose equilibrium measure computes a one-cut/two-cut spectral phase transition and identifies a double-scaling gravity class. Key computation: for the type $(1,0)$ triple $(M_N(\\mathbb C),M_N(\\mathbb C),D=\\{H,\\cdot\\})$, $\\int e^{-g\\operatorname{Tr}D^2-\\operatorname{Tr}D^4}\\,dD$ becomes $\\int e^{-[2Ng\\operatorname{Tr}H^2+2g(\\operatorname{Tr}H)^2+2N\\operatorname{Tr}H^4+8\\operatorname{Tr}H\\operatorname{Tr}H^3+6(\\operatorname{Tr}H^2)^2]}\\,dH$; the one-cut density $\\Psi(x)=\\pi^{-1}(-4a^2+\\tfrac1{2a^2}+4x^2)\\sqrt{4a^2-x^2}_+$ reaches zero at $a_c=1/\\sqrt[4]{8}$, giving $g_c=-4\\sqrt2$, after which $\\Psi(x)=\\tfrac2\\pi|x|\\sqrt{(x^2-a^2)(b^2-x^2)}_+$ has two-cut support; the double-scaling limit is the $(3,2)$ pure-gravity minimal model."
  2127,Matthew Kennedy (joint results with Eli Shamovich and with Laurent Marcoux and Mikael…,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/73/21/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.RA,math.RA math.OA math.NA math.OC mathematics linear-algebra operator-algebras convex-analysis probability,null,null,null,"Matthew Kennedy (joint results with Eli Shamovich and with Laurent Marcoux and Mikael Rørdam Skoufranis), noncommutative Choquet theory classifies matrix-convex simplices and turns majorization of operator tuples into existence of a doubly stochastic completely positive map. Key computation: a compact noncommutative convex set $K$ is an nc simplex precisely when each $x\\in K$ has a unique maximal representing nc state, equivalently $A(K)^{**}$ is a von Neumann algebra; the nc Choquet order is the dilation order $\\mu\\prec_{\\mathrm{nc}}\\nu$ iff a Stinespring representation satisfies $\\pi_\\nu|_{A(K)}=\\begin{psmallmatrix}\\pi_\\mu|_{A(K)}&*\\\\ *&*\\end{psmallmatrix}$. Consequently, for self-adjoint tuples $a=(a_i),b=(b_i)$ in a finite von Neumann algebra $(M,\\tau)$, the inequalities $f(a)\\le f(b)$ for every continuous convex nc function $f$ are equivalent to $a_i=\\varphi(b_i)$ for all $i$ under one normal unital completely positive trace-preserving map $\\varphi:M\\to M$."
  2128,Markus J,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/44/87/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.AT,math.AT math.AG math.OA math.CT mathematics sheaf-theory algebraic-topology differential-geometry groupoids talk,null,null,null,"Markus J. Pflaum (joint with Hessel Posthuma and Xiang Tang), Hochschild homology of a proper Lie-groupoid convolution algebra localizes to its orbit space and, for compact actions, is computed from differential forms on fixed-point loci and the inertia space. Key computation: for the convolution sheaf $\\mathcal A$ on $X=M/G$, diagonal cutoff chain maps $\\Psi_{k,\\varepsilon}(x_0,\\ldots,x_k)=\\prod_{j=0}^k\\rho_\\varepsilon(d^2(x_j,x_{j+1}))$ admit homotopies $bH_{k,\\varepsilon}+H_{k-1,\\varepsilon}b=1-\\Psi_\\varepsilon$, proving $HH_*(\\mathcal A(X))\\simeq\\Gamma(X;\\mathcal{HH}_*(\\mathcal A))$; each stalk reduces to $C^\\infty(G_x\\ltimes N_x\\mathcal O)$, and the twisted Connes--HKR complex $(\\Omega^*(V),\\iota_{Y_h})$ for $Y_h(v)=v-hv$ is quasi-isomorphic via restriction and projection to $(\\Omega^*(V^h),0)$, hence $H_k(C^\\infty(V),{}_hC^\\infty(V))\\simeq\\Omega^k(V^h)$ and verifies Brylinski's inertia-form model for circle actions."
  2129,Jamie Mingo,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/57/18/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.PR,math.PR math.OA mathematics free-probability probability,null,null,null,"Jamie Mingo, noncrossing-partition cumulants characterize free independence and convert the moment sequence of a noncommutative random variable into Voiculescu's compositional-transform equation. Key computation: Möbius inversion on $NC(n)$ gives $\\kappa_n(a_1,\\ldots,a_n)=\\sum_{\\pi\\in NC(n)}\\mu_{NC(n)}(\\pi,1_n)\\varphi_\\pi(a_1,\\ldots,a_n)$ and $\\varphi(a_1\\cdots a_n)=\\sum_{\\pi\\in NC(n)}\\kappa_\\pi(a_1,\\ldots,a_n)$; unital subalgebras $A_1,\\ldots,A_s$ are freely independent iff every mixed cumulant vanishes, namely $\\kappa_n(a_1,\\ldots,a_n)=0$ whenever $a_i\\in A_{j_i}$ and $\\ker(j)\\ne1_n$. Decomposing a noncrossing partition by the block containing $1$ yields $m_n=\\sum_{s=1}^n\\kappa_s\\sum_{i_1+\\cdots+i_s+s=n}m_{i_1}\\cdots m_{i_s}$, hence $C(zM(z))=M(z)$ and $R(z)=G^{\\langle-1\\rangle}(z)-z^{-1}$."
  2130,Andrew J,"https://web.ma.utexas.edu/users/blumberg/stanford-slides-latest.pdf",utexas.edu,math.AT,math.AT mathematics topological-hochschild-homology algebraic-topology cobordism category-theory slides,null,null,null,"Andrew J. Blumberg, trace methods identify topological Hochschild homology by the Hill--Hopkins--Ravenel norm and recover $TR$ and $TC$ from curves in algebraic $K$-theory. Key computation: for a ring spectrum $R$, the slides model $\\mathrm{THH}(R)$ as $N_e^{S^1}R$ and state that the equivariant homotopy type of $\\mathrm{THH}$ of a Thom spectrum is again an equivariant Thom spectrum; the cyclotomic restriction and Frobenius maps assemble $\\widetilde K(R[x]/x^n)$ over the indexing category $I$, and the completed limits $\\varprojlim_I\\Omega\\widetilde K(R[x]/x^n)$ recover $\\mathrm{TR}(R)$ and $\\mathrm{TC}(R)$, while $K\\operatorname{End}(\\mathcal C)\\simeq R\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathrm{Mot}}(S[t],\\mathcal C)$ identifies the trace motivically."
  2131,Maria Yakerson,"https://math.mit.edu/topology/notes/yakerson-slides.pdf",mit.edu,math.AG,math.AG mathematics algebraic-geometry motives schemes commutative-algebra slides,null,null,null,"Maria Yakerson, finite-flat transfers give a universal framed model for connective motivic algebraic $K$-theory and a Hilbert-scheme presentation over a perfect field. Key computation: the slides identify the motivic spectrum $\\mathrm{kgl}$ with the framed suspension spectrum generated by the presheaf $\\mathrm{Vect}$ of vector bundles, using the $\\mathbb A^1$-equivalence obtained from the Rees algebra of the canonical filtration on a finite flat algebra; group completion then turns the forgetful map from finite-flat Hilbert schemes to $\\mathrm{Vect}$ into an $\\mathbb A^1$-equivalence, producing $\\Omega_T^\\infty\\mathrm{kgl}\\simeq L_{\\mathrm{mot}}\\operatorname{Hilb}^{\\mathrm{fl}}(\\mathbb A^\\infty)$."
  2132,Maxence Mayrand (joint with Peter Crooks),"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/2/49/40/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.SG,math.SG math.RA hep-th math.AG mathematics physics symplectic-geometry noncommutative-algebra derived-algebraic-geometry group-theory talk,null,null,null,"Maxence Mayrand (joint with Peter Crooks), shifted symplectic geometry proves the regular Moore--Tachikawa construction of a two-dimensional TQFT from a complex semisimple group. Key computation: composition of $1$-shifted Lagrangian correspondences is the derived fibre product $L_1\\times_{G_2}L_2$, and Hamiltonian composition is symplectic reduction $(M\\times N)//H$; Kostant's slice $S\\subset\\mathfrak g^*_{\\mathrm{reg}}$ and abelian regular stabilizers give a Morita equivalence $T^*G|_{\\mathfrak g^*_{\\mathrm{reg}}}\\simeq T^*G|_S$, so the resulting abelian quasi-symplectic groupoid is a commutative Frobenius object and hence defines $\\eta_G:\\operatorname{Cob}_2\\to\\mathrm{Weinstein}^{1\\text{-}\\mathrm{symp}}$."
  2133,Iva Halacheva,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/26/40/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.CT,math.CT math.RT math.AG math.GR mathematics category-theory group-theory lie-theory perverse-sheaves,null,null,null,"Iva Halacheva, Rickard complexes in categorical $\\mathfrak g$-representations yield perverse equivalences whose action on simple objects is the crystal cactus-group action. Key computation: for a categorical $\\mathfrak{sl}_2$-action, $\\Theta_n=(\\cdots\\to E^{(n+2)}F^{(2)}\\to E^{(n+1)}F^{(1)}\\to E^{(n)})$ induces $D^b(\\mathcal C_{-n})\\simeq D^b(\\mathcal C_n)$ and decategorifies to $s|_{V_{-n}}=\\sum_k(-1)^ke^{(n+k)}f^{(k)}$; composing these complexes along a reduced word for $w_0^J$ gives bijections $\\theta_J$ on $\\operatorname{Irr}(\\mathcal C)$ satisfying $\\theta_J^2=1$, $\\theta_J\\theta_K=\\theta_K\\theta_J$, and $\\theta_J\\theta_K=\\theta_{J^*(K)}\\theta_J$, hence $c_J\\mapsto\\theta_J$ realizes the cactus group and agrees with Schützenberger involutions on crystals."
  2134,Michael Rosbotham (joint with Martin Mathieu),"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/37/25/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.RA,math.RA math.CT math.OA mathematics category-theory homological-algebra linear-algebra noncommutative-algebra talk,null,null,null,"Michael Rosbotham (joint with Martin Mathieu), relative homological algebra classifies unital operator algebras of completely bounded global dimension zero as finite direct sums of full matrix algebras. Key computation: the forgetful functor $F:\\operatorname{OMod}_A^\\infty\\to\\operatorname{Op}^\\infty$ defines the exact structure $\\mathcal E_{\\mathrm{rel}}=\\{(\\mu,\\pi)\\in\\mathcal E_{\\max}:(F\\mu,F\\pi)\\in\\mathcal E_{\\min}\\}$; $E$ is relatively projective exactly when there are completely bounded $A$-module maps $s:E\\to E\\otimes_hA$ and $r:E\\otimes_hA\\to E$ with $rs=\\operatorname{id}_E$, so all operator $A$-modules are relatively projective iff $A$ is classically semisimple, and Artin--Wedderburn plus Gelfand--Mazur yields $d_{gcb}(A)=0\\Longleftrightarrow A\\cong\\bigoplus_iM_{n_i}(\\mathbb C)$."
  2135,Emily Riehl,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/63/18/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,cs.LO,cs.LO math.LO math.CT computer-science mathematics type-theory category-theory constructive-mathematics algebraic-topology,null,null,null,"Emily Riehl, univalent foundations encode higher-categorical composition by contractibility and support a machine computation of $\\pi_4(S^3)$. Key computation: univalence makes $\\operatorname{id\\text{-}to\\text{-}equiv}:(A=_{\\mathcal U}B)\\to(A\\simeq B)$ an equivalence, while the composite space $\\operatorname{Comp}(f,g)$ of two paths is a pullback of $\\operatorname{Map}(\\Delta,X)\\to\\operatorname{Map}(\\Lambda,X)$ and is contractible because $D^n\\times\\Delta$ deformation-retracts onto $S^{n-1}\\times\\Delta\\cup_{S^{n-1}\\times\\Lambda}D^n\\times\\Lambda$; this contractible-choice principle supplies composition in synthetic $\\infty$-categories, and the constructive Hopf-fibration/Freudenthal/Blakers--Massey calculation normalizes in Cubical Agda to the Brunerie number $n=-2$, hence $\\pi_4(S^3)\\cong\\mathbb Z/2\\mathbb Z$."
  2136,Pádraig Daly,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/73/83/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.OA,math.OA mathematics operator-algebras,null,null,null,"Pádraig Daly, operator-system methods classify quantum superchannels, prove every channel-space supermap extends to a superchannel, and characterize extreme constrained completely positive extensions. Key computation: the Choi map identifies $S_{\\mathrm{CPTP}}(d,r)$ completely order isomorphically with $S(d,r)=\\{(P_{ij}):\\operatorname{Tr}P_{ii}=\\operatorname{Tr}P_{jj},\\ \\operatorname{Tr}P_{ij}=0\\ (i\\ne j)\\}$; a CPTP-preserving $\\Gamma$ is a quantum superchannel exactly when the induced $\\widetilde\\Gamma:S(d_1,r_1)\\to S(d_2,r_2)$ is completely positive, so Arveson's extension theorem and Choi's theorem produce $S(\\varphi)=\\psi_{\\mathrm{post}}\\circ(\\operatorname{id}_e\\otimes\\varphi)\\circ\\psi_{\\mathrm{pre}}$, with minimal $e=\\operatorname{rank}(\\operatorname{Tr}_{r_1}\\operatorname{Tr}_{r_2}C_{\\widetilde S})$; two extensions induce the same action precisely subject to $\\operatorname{Tr}_{d_1}(C_{\\widetilde S_1}-C_{\\widetilde S_2})=0$."
  2137,Reyer Sjamaar (joint work with Florian Dorsch,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/2/49/30/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.CT,math.CT math.SG math.DG math.AG mathematics symplectic-geometry groupoids derived-algebraic-geometry diffeology-and-generalized-smooth-spaces talk,null,null,null,"Reyer Sjamaar (joint work with Florian Dorsch, Miguel Cueca, and Chenchang Zhu), derived higher Lie groupoids encode singular symplectic reduction as a shifted symplectic groupoid Morita equivalent to a derived Lagrangian intersection. Key computation: a derived manifold $\\mathcal M=(M,E_\\bullet,X_\\bullet)$ has tangent complex $T_mM\\to E_{1,m}\\to\\cdots$, and weak equivalences are bijective on classical loci and quasi-isomorphisms on these complexes; for a Hamiltonian $G$-manifold $(M,\\omega,\\mu)$, the derived action groupoid $G\\times(\\mathfrak g^*\\times M)\\rightrightarrows(\\mathfrak g^*\\times M)$ has tangent total complex $0\\to\\mathfrak g\\to T_mM\\to\\mathfrak g^*\\to0$, while $(\\omega,\\mu)$ is closed in the de Rham triple complex because $d\\omega=0$ and $\\delta\\omega=d\\mu$, and its induced pairing is a quasi-isomorphism $T\\simeq T^\\vee$, giving the $0$-shifted symplectic derived quotient $M//G$."
  2138,Adam Humeniuk,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/73/38/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.OA,math.OA mathematics operator-algebras,null,null,null,"Adam Humeniuk, the $C^*$-covers of a nonselfadjoint operator algebra form a complete lattice, and explicit constructions separate five natural notions of equality of cover data. Key computation: ordering covers by $(C,\\iota)\\geq(D,\\eta)$ when a surjective $*$-homomorphism $C\\twoheadrightarrow D$ intertwines $\\iota$ and $\\eta$ makes $C^*\\!\\operatorname{-Lat}(A)$ a complete lattice with extrema $C^*_{\\max}(A)$ and $C^*_{\\mathrm{env}}(A)$; the formulas $C^*\\!\\operatorname{-Lat}(A\\oplus B)\\cong C^*\\!\\operatorname{-Lat}(A)\\times C^*\\!\\operatorname{-Lat}(B)$ and, for nuclear simple $B$, $C^*\\!\\operatorname{-Lat}(A\\otimes B)\\cong C^*\\!\\operatorname{-Lat}(A)\\otimes B$ yield examples such as $T_2$ and $T_2\\otimes M_2\\cong M_2(T_2)$ that are lattice-isomorphic but not lattice-intertwined, proving that isomorphism, intertwining, lattice $*$-isomorphism, lattice isomorphism, and cover equivalence are genuinely distinct."
  2139,Michael Brannan (joint with Kari Eifler,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/25/18/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.CO,math.CO math.QA hep-th mathematics physics graph-theory vertex-algebras talk,null,null,null,"Michael Brannan (joint with Kari Eifler, Christian Voigt, and Moritz Weber), quantum graphs admit universal quantum Cuntz--Krieger algebras that recover the classical construction for commutative vertex algebras and exhibit new nonnuclear examples. Key computation: for $\\mathcal G=(B,\\psi,A)$ with $B=\\bigoplus_{k=1}^mM_{n(k)}(\\mathbb C)$, writing $S^{(k)}=(\\dim B/n(k))[S(e_{ij}^{(k)})]$ turns the defining relation $\\mu(1\\otimes\\mu)(S\\otimes S^*\\otimes S)(1\\otimes m^*)m^*=S$ into $S^{(k)}S^{(k)*}S^{(k)}=S^{(k)}$, while $\\mu(S^*\\otimes S)m^*=\\mu(S\\otimes S^*)m^*A$ becomes $S^{(k)*}S^{(k)}=(S^{(1)}S^{(1)*},\\ldots,S^{(m)}S^{(m)*})\\widetilde A$; these relations define $\\mathcal O_{\\mathcal G}$, give $\\mathcal O_{\\mathcal G}$ equal to the usual Cuntz--Krieger algebra when $B$ is abelian, and for the complete quantum graph yield the explicit quotient $\\mathcal O_{K(M_n)}\\twoheadrightarrow\\mathcal O_{n^2}$ by $S_{ij}\\mapsto n^{-1/2}\\widehat S_{ij}$."
  2140,Dominic Joyce,"https://people.maths.ox.ac.uk/joyce/DAG2022/DAG3+4.pdf",ac.uk,math.AG,math.AG math.CT math.AT math.DG mathematics category-theory stacks algebraic-topology differential-geometry,null,null,null,"Dominic Joyce, global quotient stacks form a $(2,1)$-category in which classifying stacks retain automorphisms of principal bundles and $2$-fibre products differ essentially from fibre products in the homotopy category. Key computation: morphisms $U\\to[*/\\Gamma]$ classify principal $\\Gamma$-bundles $P\\to U$, and the fibre product of $[U/\\Gamma]\\to[W/K]\\leftarrow[V/\\Delta]$ is $[\\{(u,v,k):f(u)=kg(v)\\}/(\\Gamma\\times\\Delta)]$ with $(\\gamma,\\delta)(u,v,k)=(\\gamma u,\\delta v,\\rho(\\gamma)k\\sigma(\\delta)^{-1})$; in particular $*\\times_{[*/\\Gamma]}*\\simeq\\Gamma$, so pulling back the tautological $*\\to[*/\\Gamma]$ along the classifying map recovers $P\\to U$."
  2141,Dominic Joyce,"https://people.maths.ox.ac.uk/joyce/DAG2022/DAG5+6.pdf",ac.uk,math.AG,math.AG math.AT mathematics sheaf-theory moduli-spaces,null,null,null,"Dominic Joyce, coherent sheaves on $\\mathbb{CP}^1$ are classified by line bundles and finite-length skyscraper sheaves, and torsion-free sheaves compactify moduli of vector bundles by admitting singular limits. Key computation: the exact sequence $0\\to\\mathcal O_{\\mathbb{CP}^1}\\xrightarrow{s^{\\otimes k}}\\mathcal O_{\\mathbb{CP}^1}(k)\\to\\mathcal O_{[x,y]}^{(k)}\\to0$ constructs the length-$k$ torsion summands, and every $E\\in\\operatorname{coh}(\\mathbb{CP}^1)$ decomposes uniquely as a finite direct sum of $\\mathcal O_{\\mathbb{CP}^1}(n)$ and $\\mathcal O_{[x,y]}^{(k)}$; on $\\mathbb{CP}^2$, the cokernel of $(y,z,\\delta\\operatorname{id}):\\mathcal O\\to\\mathcal O(1)^{\\oplus2}\\oplus\\mathcal O$ is $\\mathcal O(1)^{\\oplus2}$ for $\\delta\\ne0$ but specializes at $\\delta=0$ to $(I_{[1,0,0]}\\otimes\\mathcal O(2))\\oplus\\mathcal O$, an explicit non-locally-free torsion-free limit."
  2142,Dominic Joyce,"https://people.maths.ox.ac.uk/joyce/DAG2022/DAG9+10.pdf",ac.uk,math.CT,math.CT math.AT mathematics category-theory homotopy-theory groupoids higher-category-theory,null,null,null,"Dominic Joyce, Dwyer--Kan hammock localization upgrades inversion of weak equivalences from an ordinary homotopy category to a simplicial model for the associated $\\infty$-category, while horn filling distinguishes categories, groupoids, and quasicategories. Key computation: for a model category $\\mathcal M$, the mapping simplicial set $\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathrm{DK}}(X,Y)$ is built from rows of zigzags $X\\leftarrow^{\\sim}K_1\\leftarrow^{\\sim}L_1\\to K_2\\leftarrow^{\\sim}\\cdots\\to Y$, and $\\pi_0\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathrm{DK}}(X,Y)=\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal M[W^{-1}]}(X,Y)$; moreover a simplicial set is $N(\\mathcal C)$ exactly when every inner horn has a unique filler, and $N(\\mathcal C)$ is Kan exactly when $\\mathcal C$ is a groupoid, identifying Kan complexes as models for $\\infty$-groupoids and weak Kan complexes as quasicategories."
  2143,Dominic Joyce,"https://people.maths.ox.ac.uk/joyce/DAG2022/DAG13+14.pdf",ac.uk,math.AG,math.AG math.CT math.SG math.AT mathematics derived-algebraic-geometry homological-algebra moduli-spaces symplectic-geometry,null,null,null,"Dominic Joyce, PTVV shifted symplectic structures turn Calabi--Yau moduli into explicit local critical-locus geometry and lower the shift under derived Lagrangian intersection. Key computation: for the derived moduli stack $\\mathcal M$ of coherent complexes on a Calabi--Yau $m$-fold, $h^i(T_{\\mathcal M}|_E)\\cong\\operatorname{Ext}^{i+1}(E,E)$ and Serre duality $\\operatorname{Ext}^i(E,E)\\cong\\operatorname{Ext}^{m-i}(E,E)^*$ induce the nondegenerate quasi-isomorphism $T_{\\mathcal M}\\xrightarrow{\\sim}L_{\\mathcal M}[2-m]$, hence a $(2-m)$-shifted symplectic form; if $L,M\\to(X,\\omega_k)$ are Lagrangian then $L\\times_XM$ is $(k-1)$-shifted symplectic, and for $k=-1$ the shifted Darboux theorem gives $(X,\\omega)\\simeq\\operatorname{Crit}(H:U\\to\\mathbb A^1)$ Zariski locally, so Calabi--Yau $3$-fold sheaf moduli are locally classical critical loci."
  2144,Jared Weinstein,"https://math.bu.edu/people/jsweinst/Tate_conference_slides.pdf",bu.edu,math.AG,math.AG math.NT math.AT math.GR mathematics arithmetic-geometry perfectoid-spaces chromatic-homotopy-theory formal-groups,null,null,null,"Jared Weinstein, Faltings's two-towers theorem identifies the Lubin--Tate and Drinfeld towers as quotient $v$-stacks and converts Morava-stabilizer cohomology into $p$-adic Lie-group cohomology. Key computation: the isomorphism $[\\mathrm{LT}_{n,\\eta}^{\\diamond}/\\mathbb G_n]\\cong[\\mathcal H_{n-1}^{\\diamond}/\\mathrm{GL}_n(\\mathbb Z_p)]$ arises because a height-$n$, dimension-$1$ $p$-divisible group $G/\\mathcal O_C$ has Hodge--Tate sequence $0\\to\\operatorname{Lie}G\\otimes C(1)\\to T_pG\\otimes C\\to(\\operatorname{Lie}G^*)^*\\otimes C\\to0$, determining a line in $T_pG\\otimes C$, with the formal-group locus exactly Drinfeld's $\\mathcal H_{n-1}$; taking $\\mathcal O^+$-cohomology yields $H^*(\\mathbb G_n,W(\\mathbb F_p))\\to H^*(\\mathbb G_n,W(\\mathbb F_p)[[u_1,\\ldots,u_{n-1}]])$ as an isomorphism after inverting $p$ and $H^*(\\mathbb G_n,E_n^0)[1/p]\\cong\\mathbb Q_p[x_1,x_3,\\ldots,x_{2n-1}]$ with $|x_i|=i$."
  2145,Dihua Jiang,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/2/65/34/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.RT,math.RT math.AG math.CT math.GR mathematics descent-theory geometric-representation-theory group-theory langlands-program,null,null,null,"Dihua Jiang, consecutive local descents of enhanced Langlands parameters classify the arithmetic wavefront set of generic representations of classical groups by rational nilpotent orbits. Key computation: local Gan--Gross--Prasad gives $\\dim_{\\mathbb C}\\operatorname{Hom}_{M_{\\ell,X}}(J_X(V_\\pi)\\otimes\\widehat\\sigma^\\vee,1)=1$ exactly when the component-group characters satisfy $(\\chi,\\chi')=(\\chi^z_{\\phi,\\varphi},\\chi^z_{\\varphi,\\phi})$; defining $D_\\ell(\\phi,\\chi)=\\bigcup_{z\\in F^\\times/F^{\\times2}}D_\\ell^z(\\phi,\\chi)$ and $\\ell_0=\\max\\{\\ell:D_\\ell(\\phi,\\chi)\\ne\\varnothing\\}$, first descent is discrete and $\\ell_0(\\pi)=\\ell_0(\\phi,\\chi)$, so iteration produces signed Young-tableau data $(\\ell_j,q(\\ell_j))$ and a canonical orbit set $\\mathrm{WF}_{\\mathrm{ari}}(\\pi)$; over $\\mathbb R$, if $S_\\phi\\ne1$ then $\\mathrm{WF}_{\\mathrm{ari}}(\\pi)_{\\max}=\\{\\mathcal O_a(\\phi,\\chi)\\}$, a unique rational nilpotent orbit determining a stable orbit."
  2146,Wee Teck Gan,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/2/65/19/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.NT,math.NT cs.NI math.SG math.RT mathematics computer-science automorphic-forms networking symplectic-geometry,null,null,null,"Wee Teck Gan, the endoscopic classification of the metaplectic group $\\operatorname{Mp}_{2n}$ decomposes its discrete automorphic spectrum into packets indexed by elliptic Arthur parameters, with multiplicities controlled by component-group characters and central epsilon factors. Key computation: for $\\Psi=\\boxplus_i\\Pi_i\\boxtimes S_{d_i}$ the local packet is $\\widetilde\\Pi_{\\Psi_v,\\psi_v}=\\bigoplus_{\\eta_v\\in\\operatorname{Irr}(S_{\\Psi_v})}\\eta_v\\otimes\\widetilde\\pi_{\\eta_v}$ and $\\widetilde A_{n,\\mathrm{disc}}=\\bigoplus_\\Psi\\widetilde A_{\\Psi,\\psi}$ with $\\widetilde A_{\\Psi,\\psi}=\\langle\\widetilde\\epsilon_\\Psi,\\Delta^*(\\widetilde\\Pi_{\\Psi,\\psi})\\rangle_{S_\\Psi}$, where $\\widetilde\\epsilon_\\Psi=\\epsilon_\\Psi\\widetilde\\eta_\\Psi$ and $\\widetilde\\eta_\\Psi(e_i)=\\epsilon(1/2,\\Pi_i)$ for symplectic $\\Pi_i$ and $1$ for orthogonal $\\Pi_i$; locally, endoscopic transfer satisfies $t_{a,b}^*(\\Theta_\\Psi)=\\epsilon(1/2,\\Psi_b)\\sum_\\eta\\eta(s_{a,b})\\Theta_{\\widetilde\\pi_\\eta}$ for $s_{a,b}=(1_{2a},-1_{2b})$."
  2147,Yiannis Sakellaridis (joint with Chen Wan),"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/2/65/31/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.RT,math.RT math.NT mathematics relative-langlands automorphic-forms complex-analysis l-functions talk,null,null,null,"Yiannis Sakellaridis (joint with Chen Wan), comparison of the Jacquet--Zagier and Kuznetsov trace formulas separates the residual spectrum of $\\mathrm{GL}_n$ into boundary terms indexed by generic length. Key computation: after partially compactifying Whittaker test functions there is an isomorphism $U:\\mathcal S(G/G)\\xrightarrow{\\sim}\\mathcal S^+((N,\\psi)\\backslash G/(N,\\psi))$ pulling stable characters back to relative characters and satisfying $\\mathrm{STF}(f)=\\mathrm{KTF}(Uf)+\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}B_j(f)$; the Kuznetsov main term has spectral expansion over generic automorphic representations, whereas $B_j$ has expansion over representations of generic length $n-j$, and meromorphic comparison in a parameter $s$ pairs the divergence of the naive trace formula with the pole of $L(\\pi,\\operatorname{Ad},s)$, so taking $\\operatorname{Res}_{s=1}$ yields the noninvariant trace-formula identity with all residual boundary contributions separated."
  2148,Bill Casselman,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/2/65/37/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.RT,math.RT math.NT mathematics langlands-program representation-theory,null,null,null,"Bill Casselman, the inverse Satake transform gives an explicit basic Schwartz function for every irreducible finite-dimensional representation of $\\widehat{\\mathrm{GL}}_2$, reducing the construction to symmetric-power multiplicities and vector partition functions. Key computation: for $F_{m,n}=\\mathbf1_{K\\varpi^{(m,n)}K}$, $\\mathcal S^{-1}(\\det^nq^{(m-n)/2}\\chi_{m-n})=\\Phi_{m,n}=\\sum_{i:m-i\\ge n+i}F_{m-i,n+i}$, while $\\det(I-At)^{-1}=\\sum_{m\\ge0}\\operatorname{tr}S^m(A)t^m$ and $S^m(\\sigma_k)=\\sum_{i\\le km/2}\\delta_{km}(i)\\det^i\\sigma_{km-2i}$ imply that the $L$-function's basic function is $\\sum_{i\\le km/2}q^i\\delta_{km}(i)\\Phi_{km-i,i}$; writing the weight multiplicity piecewise as $\\omega_{km}(n)=\\sum_{j=0}^i(-1)^jD_{\\alpha_j}(n-m_j)$ on $m_i\\le n\\le m_{i+1}$ proves it is quasi-polynomial on each Sturmfels cone and yields the displayed Sylvester asymptotics for $\\omega_{km}$ and $\\delta_{km}$."
  2149,Malors Espinosa (joint with Melissa Emory,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/2/65/26/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.NT,math.NT math.SG math.CA math.RT mathematics symplectic-geometry algebraic-number-theory automorphic-forms harmonic-analysis talk,null,null,null,"Malors Espinosa (joint with Melissa Emory, Debanjana Kundu, and Tian An Wong), interpolation and Poisson summation isolate the trivial and special-representation traces inside the regular elliptic trace formula for $\\mathrm{GL}_2$ over a totally real number field. Key computation: contributing elliptic classes are exactly $(\\tau,u)\\in\\mathcal O_K\\times\\mathcal O_K^\\times$ with $\\tau^2-4u\\epsilon$ nonsquare, and $(\\tau^2-4u\\epsilon)=\\mathfrak S_\\gamma^2\\Delta_\\gamma$ defines $O(z,\\gamma)=N(\\mathfrak S_\\gamma)^z\\sum_{\\mathfrak d\\mid\\mathfrak S_\\gamma}N(\\mathfrak d)^{1-2z}\\prod_{\\mathfrak q\\mid\\mathfrak d'}(1-\\chi_\\gamma(\\mathfrak q)N(\\mathfrak q)^{-z})$; then $O(1,\\gamma)=\\prod_{\\mathfrak q}O(\\gamma,f_\\mathfrak q)$ and $\\Lambda(z,\\chi_\\gamma)O(z,\\gamma)=\\Lambda(1-z,\\chi_\\gamma)O(1-z,\\gamma)$, while Poisson summation gives $\\operatorname{Tr}(1(f))=p^{-k/2}(1-p^{-(k+1)})(1-p^{-1})^{-1}\\sum_\\pm\\int_{\\mathbb R^{2n-1}}\\theta_\\pm(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy$ and identifies the second residue as $-2$ times the special-representation trace."
  2150,Andrew Dean (joint with Luis Santiago),"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/37/07/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.FA,math.FA mathematics functional-analysis talk,null,null,null,"Andrew Dean (joint with Luis Santiago), triples of real, complexified, and quaternionified Cuntz semigroups classify unital real AI algebras and their homomorphisms up to approximate unitary equivalence. Key computation: $\\operatorname{Inv}(A)$ is the diagram $(\\operatorname{Cu}(A),[1])\\to(\\operatorname{Cu}(A\\otimes_{\\mathbb R}\\mathbb C),[1])\\to(\\operatorname{Cu}(A\\otimes_{\\mathbb R}\\mathbb H),[1])$; every morphism $\\eta=(\\eta_r,\\eta_c,\\eta_h):\\operatorname{Inv}(A)\\to\\operatorname{Inv}(B)$ is realized by a unital $*$-homomorphism $\\varphi:A\\to B$ with $(\\operatorname{Cu}(\\varphi),\\operatorname{Cu}(\\varphi\\otimes_{\\mathbb R}\\operatorname{id}_{\\mathbb C}),\\operatorname{Cu}(\\varphi\\otimes_{\\mathbb R}\\operatorname{id}_{\\mathbb H}))=(\\eta_r,\\eta_c,\\eta_h)$, and equality $\\operatorname{Inv}(\\varphi)=\\operatorname{Inv}(\\psi)$ implies $\\varphi\\sim_{\\mathrm{a.u.}}\\psi$."
  2151,Joachim Zacharias (joint with Joan Bosa,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/73/75/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.OA,math.OA mathematics operator-algebras talk,null,null,null,"Joachim Zacharias (joint with Joan Bosa, Francesc Perera, and Jianchao Wu), almost elementary actions have $\\mathcal Z$-stable crossed products and unify almost finite dynamics with $\\mathcal Z$-stability of simple nuclear algebras. Key computation: a noncommutative tower is a c.p.c. order-zero map $\\lambda:M_n\\otimes C(S)\\to A$ satisfying $\\lambda(\\,\\cdot\\,\\otimes\\delta_g)=\\alpha_{gh^{-1}}\\lambda(\\,\\cdot\\,\\otimes\\delta_h)$; castles approximating finite sets with $1-\\lambda(h)\\precsim_G b$ and $\\|h-g\\cdot h\\|<\\varepsilon$ define almost elementariness, for which $\\alpha:G\\curvearrowright A\\Rightarrow A\\rtimes_\\alpha G$ is almost elementary and hence $\\mathcal Z$-stable, while for $A=C(X)$ and amenable $G$ the condition is equivalent to almost finiteness of $G\\curvearrowright X$."
  2152,Marcelo Laca (joint with Camila F,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/73/31/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.FA,math.FA math.OA math.PR mathematics free-probability functional-analysis talk,null,null,null,"Marcelo Laca (joint with Camila F. Sehnem), constructible right ideals and foundation-set relations give the full boundary quotient of a semigroup Toeplitz algebra and a criterion for its reduced quotient to be purely infinite simple. Key computation: for $P\\subseteq G$, neutral words $\\alpha$ produce projections $L_{\\dot\\alpha}=1_{K(\\alpha)}$, and adjoining $\\prod_{\\beta\\in F}(w_{\\dot\\alpha}-w_{\\dot\\beta})=0$ for every foundation cover $\\bigcup_{\\beta\\in F}K(\\beta)\\subseteq K(\\alpha)$ yields canonical isomorphisms $\\mathcal C\\rtimes_{\\mathcal C P}P\\cong C^*(w_p\\mid\\mathrm{(T1)-(T5)})\\cong C(\\partial\\Omega_P)\\rtimes_u G$; boundary topological freeness, equivalently condition $\\mathrm{(BTF)}$, then makes $C(\\partial\\Omega_P)\\rtimes_rG$ purely infinite simple, recovering this for $P=R\\rtimes R^\\times$ by explicitly choosing $s=(0,ac)$ or $s=(0,ac\\bar x r)$ so that $psP\\cap qsP=\\varnothing$."
  2153,Gelu Popescu,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/39/66/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.KT,math.KT math.AT mathematics k-theory algebraic-geometry operator-algebras representation-theory,null,null,null,"Gelu Popescu, noncommutative Berezin kernels give a unique Wold decomposition and classify pure representations of the $C^*$-algebra of a noncommutative variety by multiplicity. Key computation: for $V_i=\\pi(B_i)$ and $\\Phi_{f,V}(Y)=\\sum_{|\\alpha|\\ge1}a_\\alpha V_\\alpha YV_\\alpha^*$, the projections $P_{\\mathcal K_1}=\\operatorname*{SOT-lim}_{s\\to\\infty}\\Phi_{f,V}^s(I)$ and $P_{\\mathcal K_0}=I-P_{\\mathcal K_1}$ split $\\mathcal K=\\mathcal K_0\\oplus\\mathcal K_1$ into pure and Cuntz tuples; on $\\mathcal K_0$, the constrained Berezin kernel is unitary and $V_i=K_{f,V,Q}^*(B_i\\otimes I_{\\mathcal D})K_{f,V,Q}$ with $\\mathcal D=\\operatorname{ran}(I-\\Phi_{f,V})^m(I)^{1/2}$, so pure representations are unitarily equivalent exactly when $\\dim\\mathcal D$ agrees."
  2154,Leonel Robert,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/25/20/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.OA,math.OA mathematics operator-algebras,null,null,null,"Leonel Robert, commutator subgroups of connected invertibles and unitaries in a unital $C^*$-algebra admit explicit exponential generators and strong simplicity properties. Key computation: the local Campbell--Baker--Hausdorff/Kashiwara--Vergne identities $e^xe^y=e^{x+y+[x,a]+[y,b]}$ and $e^{x+y}=(e^Re^xe^{-R})(e^Se^ye^{-S})$ show $e^{x+y}$ is commutator-equivalent to $e^xe^y$; consequently $D\\mathrm{GL}_0(A)=\\langle e^{[c,d]}:c,d\\in A\\rangle$ and $D\\mathrm U_0(A)=\\langle e^{i[x^*,x]}:x\\in A\\rangle$, and if $A$ has no one-dimensional representations then these groups are perfect, with $D\\mathrm{GL}_0(A)$ generated by $1+x$ for $x^2=0$."
  2155,Camila F,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/73/61/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.OA,math.OA math.PR mathematics operator-algebras probability talk,null,null,null,"Camila F. Sehnem (joint with Zahra Afsar, Marcelo Laca, and Jacqui Ramagge), the KMS$_\\beta$ simplex of a Toeplitz noncommutative torus is classified by probability measures on a torus determined by the degeneracy of its zero-energy block. Key computation: for $r=(r_1,\\ldots,r_k,0_d)$, every KMS state satisfies $\\varphi(L_pL_xL_y^*L_q^*)=\\delta_{p,q}e^{-\\beta\\langle p,r\\rangle}\\varphi(L_xL_y^*)$ and is obtained from a trace $\\omega$ on $Q\\mathcal T_r(\\mathbb N^n,\\sigma_\\Theta)Q$ by $T_\\beta(\\omega)(X)=Z(\\beta)^{-1}\\sum_{l\\in\\mathbb N^k\\times0_d}e^{-\\beta\\langle l,r\\rangle}\\omega(QL_l^*XL_lQ)$, where $Q=\\prod_{j=1}^k(1-L_{e_j}L_{e_j}^*)$ and $Z(\\beta)=\\sum_pe^{-\\beta\\langle p,r\\rangle}$; since traces on $A_{\\Theta_d}$ are states on $Z(A_{\\Theta_d})\\cong C(\\mathbb T^m)$, this gives $\\operatorname{KMS}_\\beta\\cong\\mathcal M_1(\\mathbb T^m)$."
  2156,Sarah Reznikoff (joint with Jonathan Brown,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/73/49/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.CO,math.CO math.OA mathematics graph-theory operator-algebras talk,null,null,null,"Sarah Reznikoff (joint with Jonathan Brown, Adam Fuller, and David Pitts), regular ideals of a $C^*$-algebra are classified by invariant regular ideals of a regular subalgebra, and regular graph-algebra quotients preserve Condition (L). Key computation: for a regular inclusion $B\\subseteq A$ with faithful invariant expectation $E:A\\to B$ and regular ideal intersection property, $J\\mapsto J\\cap B$ and $K\\mapsto J_K=\\{a\\in A:E(a^*a)\\in K^{\\perp\\perp}\\}$ are inverse Boolean-algebra isomorphisms between regular ideals of $A$ and invariant regular ideals of $B$; for graph algebras, $J=J^{\\perp\\perp}$ is equivalent to $p_w\\in J\\Longleftrightarrow(\\forall v\\in T(w))(\\exists u\\in T(v))\\,p_u\\in H(J)$, which forces every cycle of $E/J$ to retain an entrance and hence preserves Condition (L)."
  2157,Paul Skoufranis,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/73/36/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.OA,math.OA math.PR math.RA math.NA mathematics linear-algebra operator-algebras probability,null,null,null,"Paul Skoufranis, tracial inequalities completely characterize convex hulls of unitary orbits of self-adjoint elements in arbitrary unital $C^*$-algebras and extend to joint majorization of commuting tuples. Key computation: for self-adjoint $S,T\\in A$, one has $S\\in\\overline{\\operatorname{conv}}(\\mathcal U(T))$ exactly when $\\tau((S-\\alpha)_+)\\le\\tau((T-\\alpha)_+)$ and $\\tau((-S-\\alpha)_+)\\le\\tau((-T-\\alpha)_+)$ for every lower-semicontinuous trace $\\tau$ and $\\alpha\\in\\mathbb R$; in $M_n$, this is equivalent to $\\sum_{k=1}^ma_k\\le\\sum_{k=1}^mb_k$ for ordered eigenvalues (equality at $m=n$), to $a=Xb$ for a doubly stochastic $X$, and to a unital trace-preserving completely positive map $\\Psi$ with $\\Psi(T)=S$."
  2158,Dan Ursu (joint with Shirly Geffen),"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/73/80/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.OA,math.OA mathematics operator-algebras talk,null,null,null,"Dan Ursu (joint with Shirly Geffen), simplicity of reduced crossed products by FC-hypercentral groups is characterized by the absence of a central corner on which a finite-conjugacy element acts inner with centralizer-invariant implementer. Key computation: for a minimal action $\\alpha:G\\curvearrowright A$ with $G$ FC-hypercentral, $A\\rtimes_rG$ is not simple exactly when there exist $t\\in\\mathrm{FC}(G)\\setminus\\{e\\}$, a nonzero $t$-invariant $p\\in Z(I(A))$, and $u\\in\\mathcal U(I(A)p)$ such that $\\alpha_t|_{I(A)p}=\\operatorname{Ad}(u)$ and $\\alpha_s(p)=p,\\ \\alpha_s(u)=u$ for every $s\\in C_G(t)$; this refines proper outerness on the injective envelope and is equivalent, for separable $A$, to an intrinsic obstruction on an essential $t$-invariant ideal $J\\triangleleft A$ with an implementing unitary in $M(J)$."
  2159,Matthew Kennedy,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/39/77/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.OA,math.OA math.RA mathematics operator-algebras linear-algebra convex-analysis,null,null,null,"Matthew Kennedy, noncommutative Choquet theory classifies operator systems by compact matrix-convex state spaces and characterizes those arising from $C^*$-algebras as noncommutative Bauer simplices. Key computation: matrix convexity closes $K=\\coprod_nK_n$ under $\\sum_i\\alpha_i^*x_i\\alpha_i$ whenever $\\sum_i\\alpha_i^*\\alpha_i=I$, and evaluation gives the dual equivalence $S\\cong A(K)$; every $x\\in K_n$ has a maximal representing nc state $\\mu:C(K)\\to M_n$ with $a(x)=\\int_Ka\\,d\\lambda$ supported on $\\partial K$, while uniqueness of this maximal state is equivalent to $A(K)^{**}$ being a von Neumann algebra and to $A(K)\\otimes_cT=A(K)\\otimes_{\\max}T$ for every operator system $T$; adding closed $\\partial K$ characterizes precisely nc state spaces of unital $C^*$-algebras."
  2160,Kenneth R,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/42/07/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.FA,math.FA math.CA math.OA math.PR mathematics functional-analysis operator-algebras harmonic-analysis random-matrix-theory,null,null,null,"Kenneth R. Davidson, free semigroup algebras admit a Lebesgue--von Neumann--Wold decomposition and are uniformly hyperreflexive. Key computation: on Fock space $\\mathcal F_n^2=\\ell^2(\\mathbb F_n^+)$ the left shifts satisfy $L_i^*L_j=\\delta_{ij}I$ and $\\sum_iL_iL_i^*=I-\\xi_\\varnothing\\xi_\\varnothing^*$, while $\\mathcal L_n'=\\mathcal R_n$ and every $A\\in\\mathcal L_n$ has Ces\\`aro-summed Fourier expansion $A\\sim\\sum_wa_wL_w$; the structure projection $P$ determined by $\\bigcap_{k\\ge1}\\mathcal S_0^k=\\mathcal WP$ splits an arbitrary free semigroup algebra into von Neumann and type-$L$ corners, and Kennedy's wandering-vector theorem for absolutely continuous representations yields $\\mathcal S\\simeq\\mathcal L^{(\\alpha)}\\oplus\\mathcal S_a\\oplus\\mathcal S_{vN}\\oplus\\mathcal S_d$, with every free semigroup algebra hyperreflexive with constant at most $6$."
  2161,Michael Brannan,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/36/99/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.AT,math.AT math.CO mathematics chromatic-homotopy-theory graph-theory,null,null,null,"Michael Brannan, complete quantum graphs have no finite classical coloring but have quantum chromatic number exactly $n^2$, and their coloring algebras possess strongly solid noninjective factor representations. Key computation: the algebra $\\mathcal A(\\operatorname{Hom}(QK_n,K_{n^2}))$ is generated by $p_{ij}^{(a)}$ with $p_{ij}^{(a)}p_{kl}^{(a)}=\\delta_{jk}n^{-1}p_{il}^{(a)}$, $\\sum_jp_{ij}^{(a)}p_{jk}^{(b)}=\\delta_{ab}p_{ik}^{(a)}$, $\\sum_ip_{ii}^{(a)}=n^{-1}1$, and $\\sum_ap_{ij}^{(a)}=\\delta_{ij}1$; any unitary error basis $\\{u_a\\}_{a=1}^{n^2}\\subset U(M_n)$ gives the representation $p_{ij}^{(a)}\\mapsto n^{-1}u_a^*e_{ij}u_a$, while the quantum Hoffman bound supplies the reverse inequality, proving $\\chi_q(QK_n)=n^2$ whereas $\\chi_{\\mathrm{loc}}(QK_n)=\\infty$; for $n\\ge3$ the same universal algebra has a faithful trace whose GNS algebra is a strongly solid noninjective $\\mathrm{II}_1$ factor."
  2162,"Ra\\'ul E","https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/39/29/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.OA,math.OA math.RA math.NA mathematics linear-algebra operator-algebras talk,null,null,null,"Ra\\'ul E. Curto (joint with In Sung Hwang and Woo Young Lee), Potapov's factorization theorem extends from matrices to rational two-sided inner operator-valued functions. Key computation: rationality is defined by the Hankel-kernel condition $\\theta H^2(\\mathbb T,E)\\subseteq\\ker H_{\\Phi^*}$ for a finite Blaschke product $\\theta$; if an inner $\\Delta$ divides $\\Theta=\\theta I_E$, then $\\Delta$ is a finite product of Blaschke--Potapov factors $b_\\alpha P_M+(I_E-P_M)$, and the Beurling--Lax--Halmos identity $\\ker H_{\\Phi^*}=\\Delta H^2$ yields the coprime factorization $\\Phi=\\Delta A^*$; consequently a two-sided inner $\\Phi\\in H^\\infty(\\mathbb T,B(E))$ is rational exactly when it is a finite Blaschke--Potapov product."
  2163,Ryan O'Loughlin,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/45/66/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.FA,math.FA mathematics functional-analysis,null,null,null,"Ryan O'Loughlin, compact truncated Toeplitz operators on a model space have continuous inner-multiple symbols exactly under the same conditions that give bounded symbols for all bounded truncated Toeplitz operators. Key computation: for $K_I^2=H^2\\ominus IH^2$ and $A_g^I f=P_I(gf)$, the projective product space $X=\\{\\sum_kx_ky_k:\\sum_k\\|x_k\\|_2\\|y_k\\|_2<\\infty\\}$ satisfies $X^*=\\mathcal T(I)$ and ${}^*X=\\mathcal T_c(I)$; the maps $S:X\\to zH^1\\cap K_{I^2}^1$, $f\\mapsto If$, ${}^*S:C(\\mathbb T)/(F_{I^2}\\cap C(\\mathbb T))\\to\\mathcal T_c(I)$, $[f]\\mapsto A_{If}^I$, and $S^*:L^\\infty/Q\\to\\mathcal T(I)$ identify their images with compact operators having symbols in $I C(\\mathbb T)$ and bounded-symbol operators, so duality gives $\\mathcal T_c(I)=\\{A_{Ih}^I:h\\in C(\\mathbb T)\\}$ if and only if every bounded $A_g^I$ has a bounded symbol."
  2164,Alfons Van Daele,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/80/15/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.OA,math.OA math.QA math.RT mathematics operator-algebras quantum-groups,null,null,null,"Alfons Van Daele, the polar decomposition of the antipode determines the modular structure of a locally compact quantum group and proves uniqueness of Haar weights. Key computation: regular representations satisfy $\\Delta(x)=W^*(1\\otimes x)W$ and $(\\Delta\\otimes\\iota)W=W_{13}W_{23}$, while the closed antipode has $S((\\iota\\otimes\\omega)W)=(\\iota\\otimes\\omega)W^*$ and polar decomposition $S=R\\tau_{-i/2}$; for two left Haar weights, $(K\\otimes T_r)W_1=W_2^*(K\\otimes T_r)$ implies $(1\\otimes u_t)W_1(1\\otimes u_t^*)=W_1$, hence $\\Delta(u_t)=1\\otimes u_t$ and $u_t$ is scalar, proving uniqueness up to scale; relative modular theory then produces the affiliated modular element $\\delta$ with $\\psi=\\varphi(\\delta^{1/2}\\,\\cdot\\,\\delta^{1/2})$, $\\Delta(\\delta)=\\delta\\otimes\\delta$, and $\\sigma_t'(x)=\\delta^{it}\\sigma_t(x)\\delta^{-it}$."
  2165,Jingbo Xia,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/39/73/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.CA,math.CA math.NT math.OA mathematics harmonic-analysis cyclotomic-fields operator-algebras,null,null,null,"Jingbo Xia, the Drury--Arveson space is the universal dilation space for commuting row contractions and its multiplier norm gives the correct multivariable von Neumann inequality. Key computation: for a strict commuting row contraction $A=(A_1,\\ldots,A_n)$, the identity $\\sum_{\\alpha\\in\\mathbb Z_+^n}\\frac{|\\alpha|!}{\\alpha!}A^\\alpha(I-\\sum_iA_iA_i^*)A^{*\\alpha}=I$ makes $Zx=\\sum_\\alpha\\frac{|\\alpha|!}{\\alpha!}(I-\\sum_iA_iA_i^*)^{1/2}A^{*\\alpha}x\\,\\zeta^\\alpha$ an isometry into $H_n^2\\otimes H$ satisfying $Zp(A^*)=(p(M_\\zeta^*)\\otimes I)Z$; hence $\\|p(A)\\|\\leq\\|p\\|_{\\operatorname{Mult}(H_n^2)}$, while for $\\theta(\\zeta)=\\zeta_1\\cdots\\zeta_n$ one has $\\|\\theta^k\\|_\\infty=n^{-kn/2}$ but $\\|\\theta^k\\|_{H_n^2}=((k!)^n/(nk)!)^{1/2}\\asymp k^{(n-1)/4}n^{-kn/2}$, explicitly showing why the supremum norm cannot replace the multiplier norm for $n\\ge2$."
  2166,Ilijas Farah,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/33/03/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.LO,math.LO math.OA mathematics descriptive-set-theory operator-algebras,null,null,null,"Ilijas Farah, set-theoretic saturation sharply classifies ultrapowers of separable $C^*$-algebras: CH makes all nonprincipal ultrapowers isomorphic, while failure of CH yields the maximal number of isomorphism types. Key computation: countably saturated elementarily equivalent structures $C,D$ of density $\\aleph_1$ are written as continuous chains $C=\\bigcup_{\\alpha<\\omega_1}C_\\alpha$ and $D=\\bigcup_{\\alpha<\\omega_1}D_\\alpha$; a transfinite back-and-forth constructs compatible elementary embeddings whose unions are inverse isomorphisms, so under CH, $A^{\\mathcal U}\\cong A^{\\mathcal V}$ commuting with the diagonal copy of every separable $A$ and $A'\\cap A^{\\mathcal U}\\cong A'\\cap A^{\\mathcal V}$; conversely, if CH fails, every separable infinite-dimensional $A$ has $2^{2^{\\aleph_0}}$ nonisomorphic ultrapowers and as many nonisomorphic relative commutants."
  2167,Guoliang Yu,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/44/64/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.KT,math.KT math.CA math.CO math.DG mathematics combinatorics harmonic-analysis index-theory k-theory,null,null,null,"Guoliang Yu, localization-algebra representatives refine higher indices to secondary rho invariants whose delocalized cyclic pairings recover Lott eta invariants. Key computation: partitions of unity on covers with mesh $<1/n$ localize $F=D(1+D^2)^{-1/2}$ to $F_n=\\sum_k\\sqrt{\\phi_k^{(n)}}F\\sqrt{\\phi_k^{(n)}}$, and interpolation gives $\\operatorname{propagation}(F_t)\\to0$, defining $\\operatorname{Index}_L(D)=[\\exp(2\\pi i(F_\\bullet+1)/2)]\\in K_1(C_L^*(\\widetilde X)^G)$ with $e_*\\operatorname{Index}_L(D)=\\operatorname{Index}(D)$; when $D$ is invertible, splicing a trivialization to this path gives $\\rho(D)\\in K_*(C_{L,0}^*(\\widetilde X)^G)$, and for a polynomial-growth conjugacy class $\\langle h\\rangle$, the explicit pairing $\\tau_h(u)=(2\\pi i)^{-1}\\int_0^\\infty\\operatorname{tr}_h(\\dot u(t)u(t)^{-1})dt$ satisfies $\\tau_h(\\rho(D))=\\tfrac12\\eta_{\\langle h\\rangle}(D)$."
  2168,Paolo Piazza (joint work with Hessel Posthuma,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/44/79/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.AG,math.AG math.OA math.RA math-ph mathematics physics algebraic-groups noncommutative-algebra noncommutative-geometry talk,null,null,null,"Paolo Piazza (joint work with Hessel Posthuma, Yanli Song, and Xiang Tang), cyclic cocycles on Harish--Chandra Schwartz algebras give delocalized Atiyah--Patodi--Singer formulas for cocompact proper actions of real reductive groups. Key computation: the Connes--Moscovici heat projector $V(D)$ has entries in $\\mathcal A_G^\\infty(Y)$ and defines $\\operatorname{Ind}_\\infty(D)$; extending the semisimple orbital integral $\\tau_g(f)=\\int_{G/Z_G(g)}f(xgx^{-1})d(xZ_G(g))$ and applying Melrose's defect formula ${}^b\\!\\operatorname{Tr}[R,T]=(i/2\\pi)\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\operatorname{Tr}(\\partial_\\lambda I(R,\\lambda)I(T,\\lambda))d\\lambda$ produces a relative cyclic cocycle, whose pairing yields $\\langle\\operatorname{Ind}_\\infty(D),\\tau_g^Y\\rangle=\\int_{Y_0^g}c^g\\operatorname{AS}_g(Y_0)-\\tfrac12\\eta_g(D_{\\partial Y})$ with $\\eta_g(D)=(1/\\sqrt\\pi)\\int_0^\\infty\\tau_g(D e^{-tD^2})dt/\\sqrt t$."
  2169,Masoud Khalkhali,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/44/85/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.OA,math.OA math-ph math.QA math.NT mathematics physics noncommutative-geometry function-fields,null,null,null,"Masoud Khalkhali, stable anti-Yetter--Drinfeld modules supply the coefficient objects that turn Hopf symmetries into cyclic modules and recover the Connes--Moscovici theory. Key computation: for a left $H$-module coalgebra $C$ and a right-left SAYD module $M$, the complex $C^n(C,M)=M\\otimes_HC^{\\otimes(n+1)}$ has cyclic operator $\\tau_n(m\\otimes c_0\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes c_n)=m_{(0)}\\otimes c_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes c_n\\otimes m_{(-1)}c_0$; the anti-Yetter--Drinfeld condition makes this operator descend through $\\otimes_H$, and stability gives $\\tau_n^{n+1}=\\operatorname{id}$, hence a cocyclic module; modular pairs in involution $(\\delta,\\sigma)$ are exactly one-dimensional SAYD modules ${}^\\sigma\\mathbb C_\\delta$, so taking $C=H$ recovers Connes--Moscovici Hopf cyclic cohomology and its characteristic map $\\chi_\\tau(h_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes h_n)(a_0,\\ldots,a_n)=\\tau(a_0h_1(a_1)\\cdots h_n(a_n))$."
  2170,Cong Xue,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/45/37/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.NT,math.NT math.RT math.AG mathematics langlands-program geometric-representation-theory stacks,null,null,null,"Cong Xue, the compactly supported relative cohomology of shtuka stacks is ind-smooth and carries an unramified product Weil action. Key computation: geometric Satake defines $\\mathcal H^j_{N,I,W}=R^jp_!\\mathcal F_{N,I,W}$ on $(X\\setminus N)^I$; the partial Frobenius maps $F_{\\{i\\}}:\\operatorname{Frob}_{\\{i\\}}^*\\mathcal H^j_{N,I,W}\\xrightarrow{\\sim}\\mathcal H^j_{N,I,W}$ compose to total Frobenius, so Drinfeld's lemma gives a $\\operatorname{Weil}(\\eta)^I$-action on the generic fiber, while Eichler--Shimura relations, constancy on mixed loci $(\\eta)^{I_1}\\times(v)^{I_2}$, and creation--annihilation (Zorro) operators make every specialization map $(\\mathcal H^j_{N,I,W})_y\\to(\\mathcal H^j_{N,I,W})_x$ an isomorphism; hence $\\mathcal H^j_{N,I,W}$ is ind-smooth and the action factors through $\\operatorname{Weil}(X\\setminus N)^I$."
  2171,Clifton Cunningham (joint with Andrew Fiori and Qing Zhang),"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/27/56/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.RT,math.RT math.AP math.AG math.FA mathematics lie-theory microlocal-analysis functional-analysis moduli-spaces talk,null,null,null,"Clifton Cunningham (joint with Andrew Fiori and Qing Zhang), microlocal geometry classifies the ABV packets of all six irreducible unipotent representations of $G_2(F)$ with subregular infinitesimal parameter and produces stable character combinations. Key computation: the parameter space is $X_{\\mathrm{sub}}=(\\widehat G_2\\times V_{\\mathrm{sub}})/H_{\\mathrm{sub}}$ with $H_{\\mathrm{sub}}\\cong\\mathrm{GL}_2$ and $V_{\\mathrm{sub}}\\cong\\det^{-1}\\otimes\\operatorname{Sym}^3$, so $\\operatorname{Per}_{\\widehat G_2}(X_{\\mathrm{sub}})\\simeq\\operatorname{Per}_{\\mathrm{GL}_2}(\\det^{-1}\\otimes\\operatorname{Sym}^3)$; the four binary-cubic orbits $C_0<C_1<C_2<C_3$ have microlocal groups $S_3,S_2,S_2,S_3$, and normalized microlocal vanishing cycles yield $\\Pi^{\\mathrm{ABV}}_{\\phi_0}=\\{\\pi_0,\\pi_1,\\pi_3^\\varepsilon\\}$, $\\Pi^{\\mathrm{ABV}}_{\\phi_1}=\\{\\pi_1,\\pi_2,\\pi_3^\\varepsilon\\}$, $\\Pi^{\\mathrm{ABV}}_{\\phi_2}=\\{\\pi_2,\\pi_3^\\rho,\\pi_3^\\varepsilon\\}$, and $\\Pi^{\\mathrm{ABV}}_{\\phi_3}=\\{\\pi_3,\\pi_3^\\rho,\\pi_3^\\varepsilon\\}$, with stable distributions including $\\Theta_{\\phi_3}=\\Theta_{\\pi_3}+2\\Theta_{\\pi_3^\\rho}+\\Theta_{\\pi_3^\\varepsilon}$."
  2172,"Joachim Cuntz (joint with Guillermo Corti\\~nas","https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/44/83/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.AC,math.AC math.AT math.AG math.DG mathematics commutative-algebra algebraic-topology differential-geometry noncommutative-geometry slides talk,null,null,null,"Joachim Cuntz (joint with Guillermo Corti\\~nas, Ralf Meyer, and Georg Tamme), the Feigin--Tsygan theorem identifies periodic cyclic homology of any finite-type commutative algebra in characteristic zero with the de Rham cohomology of a smooth formal presentation. Key computation: for a smooth surjection $P\\twoheadrightarrow A=P/J$ and the $J$-adic completion $P_J=\\varprojlim_nP/J^n$, flatness of $P_J$ over the Noetherian ring $P$ and two completed bar resolutions give a quasi-isomorphism $P_J\\otimes_P(C(P),b)\\to(C(P_J),b)$ and therefore $HH_*(P_J)\\cong P_J\\otimes_PHH_*(P)\\cong P_J\\otimes_P\\Omega_P^*$ by HKR; under this identification Connes' $B$ becomes $d$, while nilpotent invariance gives $HP_*(A)\\cong HP_*(P_J)$, hence $HP_*(A)\\cong HP_*(P_J)\\cong H^*_{\\mathrm{dR}}(P_J)=H^*_{\\mathrm{inf}}(A)$."
  2173,Victor Nistor,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/44/84/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.AT,math.AT math.GR math.RT math.AG mathematics topological-hochschild-homology algebraic-groups algebraic-topology group-theory,null,null,null,"Victor Nistor, localization at conjugacy classes computes cyclic invariants of crossed products and reduces the periodic cyclic homology of a real algebraic group action to its maximal compact subgroup. Key computation: for a discrete action, the condition $\\gamma_0\\cdots\\gamma_k\\in\\langle\\gamma\\rangle$ is preserved by Hochschild faces and cyclic permutation, giving $HH_k(A\\rtimes\\Gamma)\\cong\\bigoplus_{\\langle\\gamma\\rangle}HH_k(A\\rtimes\\Gamma)_\\gamma$ and $HH_*(A\\rtimes\\Gamma)_\\gamma\\cong HH_*(A\\rtimes\\Gamma)_{\\mathfrak m_\\gamma}$ for $\\mathfrak m_\\gamma=\\{f\\in C^\\infty(\\Gamma)^\\Gamma:f(\\gamma)=0\\}$; for a connected real algebraic $G$ with maximal compact $K$ and $q=\\dim(G/K)$, localization of the smooth convolution algebra then gives $HP_*(A\\rtimes G)_{\\mathfrak m}\\cong HP_{*+q}(A\\rtimes K)_{\\mathfrak m}$, so every non-elliptic localization vanishes, while $HH_*(C^\\infty(K))=C^\\infty(K)^K$ and $HP_*(C^\\infty(K))\\cong C^\\infty(K)^K\\otimes\\mathbb C[\\sigma]$ with $|\\sigma|=2$."
  2174,Katia Consani (joint with Alain Connes),"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/44/61/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.OA,math.OA math-ph math.QA mathematics physics noncommutative-geometry harmonic-analysis linear-algebra talk,null,null,null,"Katia Consani (joint with Alain Connes), Witt theory realizes the integral Bost--Connes algebra uniformly at finite and absolute arithmetic places. Key computation: $\\mathcal A_{\\mathbb Z}=\\mathbb Z[\\mathbb Q/\\mathbb Z]\\rtimes_\\rho\\mathbb N$ is generated by $e(\\gamma),\\widetilde\\mu_n,\\mu_n^*$ with $\\rho_n(e(\\gamma))=\\sum_{n\\gamma'=\\gamma}e(\\gamma')$ and $\\mu_n^*\\widetilde\\mu_n=n$; for an embedding $\\sigma:\\overline{\\mathbb F}_p^\\times\\hookrightarrow\\mathbb C^\\times$, the eigenvalue-divisor isomorphism $W_0(\\overline{\\mathbb F}_p)\\xrightarrow{\\sim}\\mathbb Z[\\overline{\\mathbb F}_p^\\times]$ intertwines $(F_n,V_n)$ with $(\\sigma_n,\\rho_n)$, yielding $\\pi_\\sigma(\\mu_n^*)=F_n$ and $\\pi_\\sigma(\\widetilde\\mu_n)=V_n$ and extending continuously to $W(\\overline{\\mathbb F}_p)$; globally the Fourier-trace invariant gives $W_0(\\mathbb S)\\cong\\mathbb Z[\\mathbb Q/\\mathbb Z]^{\\widehat{\\mathbb Z}^{\\times}}$ via $\\widehat\\tau(E,T)(n)=\\operatorname{tr}(T^n|_{T^\\infty(E(1_+))})$."
  2175,Deepak Kumar Pradhan (joint with K,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/46/29/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.CV,math.CV math.FA mathematics complex-analysis functional-analysis talk,null,null,null,"Deepak Kumar Pradhan (joint with K. D. Deepak and Jaydeb Sarkar), partially isometric Toeplitz operators on the Hardy space of a polydisc are classified up to unitary equivalence as shifts, co-shifts, or direct sums of truncated shifts. Key computation: the Brown--Halmos relations $T_{z_i}^*TT_{z_i}=T$ force a partial-isometry symbol to depend on at most one of $z_i,\\bar z_i$ for every $i$, and one obtains $T_\\varphi=T_{\\varphi_1}^*T_{\\varphi_2}$ for inner $\\varphi_1,\\varphi_2\\in H^\\infty(\\mathbb T^n)$ depending on disjoint variable sets; thus $D(T_\\varphi)=\\varphi_1H^2(\\mathbb T^n)$, $R(T_\\varphi)=\\varphi_2H^2(\\mathbb T^n)$, every $T_\\varphi^m$ is again a partial isometry, and the Halmos--Wallen decomposition gives precisely the stated classification; moreover $[T_\\varphi^*,T_\\varphi]\\ge0$ holds exactly when $\\varphi$ is inner."
  2176,Orr Shalit,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/39/74/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.AG,math.AG math.CA math.CV mathematics algebraic-geometry complex-geometry harmonic-analysis,null,null,null,"Orr Shalit, quotients of the Drury--Arveson multiplier algebra are classified by the complex geometry of their multiplier varieties. Key computation: for $k(z,w)=(1-\\langle z,w\\rangle)^{-1}$ and $H_V=\\overline{\\operatorname{span}}\\{k_\\lambda:\\lambda\\in V\\}$, one has $\\mathcal M_V=\\operatorname{Mult}(H_V)\\cong\\mathcal M_d/J_V$; an isometric algebra isomorphism induces $\\alpha=(\\varphi(z_1),\\ldots,\\varphi(z_d))\\in\\operatorname{Aut}(\\mathbb B_d)$ through $\\varphi^*(\\rho_\\lambda)=\\rho_{\\alpha(\\lambda)}$, while conversely $Uk_\\lambda=c_\\lambda k_{\\alpha(\\lambda)}$ implements the algebra isomorphism, proving $\\mathcal M_V\\cong_{\\mathrm{c.i.}}\\mathcal M_W$ exactly when an automorphism of $\\mathbb B_d$ maps $W$ onto $V$; for homogeneous varieties, algebraic isomorphism, similarity, biholomorphic equivalence, and equivalence under an invertible linear map of $\\mathbb C^d$ are all equivalent."
  2177,"Branimir \\v{C}a\\'ci\\'c","https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/73/56/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.AT,math.AT math.DG math.SG math.GT mathematics algebraic-topology differential-geometry floer-homology,null,null,null,"Branimir \\v{C}a\\'ci\\'c, quantum principal $U(1)$-bundles with connection are reconstructed from Hermitian line bimodules with connection, explicitly recovering the quantum Hopf fibration and its $q$-monopole. Key computation: the grading $\\mathcal O_q(S^3)=\\bigoplus_{m\\in\\mathbb Z}L_m$ has $L_m=\\mathcal O_q(S^3)_m$ and $L_1$ a Hermitian line $\\mathcal O_q(\\mathbb{CP}^1)$-bimodule; with $\\Omega_q^1(\\mathbb{CP}^1)=L_{-2}\\oplus L_2$, $\\partial_+=\\partial_{q^{1/2}EK}$, and $\\partial_-=\\partial_{q^{-1/2}FK}$, multiplication isomorphisms $\\mu_{j,k}:L_j\\otimes L_k\\xrightarrow{\\sim}L_{j+k}$ define $\\nabla_m^{(0)}=-i(\\mu_{m,-2}^{-1}\\partial_+,\\mu_{m,2}^{-1}\\partial_-)^T$, the unique left $\\mathcal O_q(SU(2))$-covariant connection; its curvature and vertical deformation are $F_{[L_m,\\nabla_m]}=[m]_{q^{-2}}q^2\\operatorname{vol}$ and $\\kappa_{[L_m,\\nabla_m]}=q^{2m}$, and the differential Pimsner construction lifts $(L_1,\\nabla_1)$ uniquely to $(\\mathcal O_q(S^3),\\Omega_q(S^3),d_q,\\Pi_q)$."
  2178,Robert T,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/39/65/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.CV,math.CV mathematics complex-analysis,null,null,null,"Robert T. W. Martin, noncommutative rational functions in the free Hardy space are exactly rational Szeg\\H{o} kernels with stable minimal realizations, leading to finite free Blaschke products and finitely correlated Clark measures. Key computation: a minimal descriptor realization $r(Z)=(I_n\\otimes b^*)(I_n\\otimes I_N-\\sum_jZ_j\\otimes A_j)^{-1}(I_n\\otimes c)$ belongs to $H_d^2$ exactly when $\\operatorname{spr}(A)<1$, equivalently when $A$ is jointly similar to a strict row contraction $W$ and $r=K\\{W,y,v\\}$, equivalently $\\mathbb B_d^{\\mathrm{nc}}\\subseteq\\operatorname{Dom}r$; rational inner--outer factorization stays rational, every rational inner is a finite product of irreducible free Blaschke factors, and a contractive $b$ is rational exactly when its Clark functional $\\mu_b$ is finitely correlated, with $b$ inner exactly when the compressed tuple $T_{\\mu_b}=(\\Pi_{\\mu_b}^*|_{H_{\\mu_b}})^*$ is a finite row co-isometry."
  2179,"Rapha\\'el Ponge","https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/37/34/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.OA,math.OA math-ph math.DG math.QA mathematics physics noncommutative-geometry spectral-geometry,null,null,null,"Rapha\\'el Ponge, measurable weak-trace-class operators are exactly Tauberian operators, giving a purely spectral formula for Connes' noncommutative integral and a semiclassical Weyl law for $L\\log L$ potentials. Key computation: for $T\\in\\mathcal L_{1,\\infty}$, $\\tau(T)=[\\{(\\log N)^{-1}\\sum_{k<N}\\lambda_k(T)\\}_N]\\in\\ell_\\infty/c_0$ and every Dixmier trace is $\\operatorname{Tr}_\\omega=\\omega\\circ\\tau$; independence of $\\omega$ is equivalent to existence of $\\lim_{N\\to\\infty}(\\log N)^{-1}\\sum_{k<N}\\lambda_k(T)$, which equals $\\mathop{\\rlap{-}\\!\\int}T$; if $\\int_M|V|\\log(1+|V|)\\,d\\operatorname{vol}_g<\\infty$, then $\\Delta_g^{-n/4}V\\Delta_g^{-n/4}\\in\\mathcal L_{1,\\infty}$ and $\\lim_{k\\to\\infty}k\\lambda_k^\\pm=c_n\\int_MV_\\pm\\,d\\operatorname{vol}_g$, hence $\\mathop{\\rlap{-}\\!\\int}\\Delta_g^{-n/4}V\\Delta_g^{-n/4}=c_n\\int_MV\\,d\\operatorname{vol}_g$ and $\\lim_{h\\to0^+}h^nN^-(h^n\\Delta_g+V)=c_n\\int_MV_-\\,d\\operatorname{vol}_g$."
  2180,Steven Amelotte (joint with Ben Briggs),"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/2/49/53/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.CT,math.CT math.AT math.RA math.CO mathematics homological-algebra toric-topology talk,null,null,null,"Steven Amelotte (joint with Ben Briggs), componentwise almost-linear Stanley--Reisner resolutions classify a family of moment-angle manifolds as connected sums of sphere products. Key computation: for $S=\\Bbbk[v_1,\\ldots,v_m]$, the torus sweep operations assemble to $\\delta=\\sum_{U\\subseteq[m]}v_U\\otimes\\delta_U$ on $S\\otimes H^*(\\mathcal Z_K)$, giving the minimal free resolution of $\\Bbbk[K]$, while $H^*(\\mathcal Z_K)\\cong\\operatorname{Tor}_S^*(\\Bbbk[K],\\Bbbk)$ and $H_i(\\operatorname{lin}F_\\bullet)=0$ for $1<i<\\operatorname{projdim}\\Bbbk[K]$ define the CAL condition; if $K$ is Gorenstein and CAL, then $\\mathcal Z_K\\simeq\\mathbin{\\#}$ sphere products and $\\Omega\\mathcal Z_K\\simeq$ a product of loop spaces of spheres."
  2181,Reyer Sjamaar (joint with Yi Lin,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/26/34/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.AT,math.AT math.SG mathematics algebraic-topology symplectic-geometry talk,null,null,null,"Reyer Sjamaar (joint with Yi Lin, Yiannis Loizides, and Yanli Song), Euler-like Lie-algebroid retractions extend Darboux--Moser--Weinstein normal forms and Marsden--Weinstein reduction to symplectic Lie algebroids. Key computation: an Euler-like section along a clean intersection $j:N\\hookrightarrow M$ produces a deformation retraction $\\varrho:T[0,1]\\times A\\to A$ onto $j^!A$ and hence a homotopy equivalence $j_\\sharp^*:\\Omega_A^\\bullet(U)\\simeq\\Omega_{j^!A}^\\bullet(N)$; this supplies the Moser primitive giving $\\varphi^*\\omega_1=\\omega_0$ near $N$, while for $N=\\mu^{-1}(\\mathcal O)$ the reduced form on $C\\to Q=N/G$ is determined by $q_\\sharp^*\\omega_C=j_\\sharp^*\\omega-\\operatorname{an}^*\\mu_N^*\\sigma_{\\mathcal O}$."
  2182,Valentin Deaconu,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/73/26/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.CT,math.CT math.OA mathematics groupoids,null,null,null,"Valentin Deaconu, pseudo-free self-similar groupoid actions produce ample groupoids whose Higman--Thompson groups are their topological full groups and satisfy Matui's AH exact sequence under pure infiniteness and minimality. Key computation: germs $[\\alpha,g,\\beta;\\xi]$ form $\\mathcal G(G,E)$ with cocycle $\\rho([\\alpha,g,\\beta;\\xi])=|\\alpha|-|\\beta|$ and kernel $\\mathcal N(G,E)=\\bigcup_k\\mathcal N_k(G,E)$, where $\\mathcal N_k(G,E)\\cong(E^\\infty\\rtimes G)\\times R_k$; the resulting long exact sequence with map $1-\\rho_*$ computes $H_*(\\mathcal G(G,E))$, and $V_E(G)\\cong[[\\mathcal G(G,E)]]$ fits into $H_0(\\mathcal G)\\otimes\\mathbb Z_2\\to[[\\mathcal G]]_{\\mathrm{ab}}\\xrightarrow{I}H_1(\\mathcal G)\\to0$."
  2183,"Asuman G\\'uven Aksoy (joint with Yunied Puig)","https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/39/50/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.FA,math.FA mathematics functional-analysis talk,null,null,null,"Asuman G\\'uven Aksoy (joint with Yunied Puig), backward weighted shifts characterize a nuclear operator ideal by an exact factorization theorem. Key computation: $T\\in BN_{(w,p,q)}(X,Y)$ precisely when $T=RB_wS$ for $S:X\\to\\ell^{q'}$ and $R:\\ell^p\\to Y$, with $Sx=(a_i(x))_i$, $R((\\nu_i))=\\sum_i\\nu_i y_i$, and $B_w=\\sum_{i\\ge2}w_i e_i^*\\otimes e_{i-1}$; the representation $T=\\sum_{i\\ge2}w_i a_i\\otimes y_{i-1}$ gives $\\|T\\|_{(w,p,q)}=\\inf\\|R\\|\\,\\|B_w\\|\\,\\|S\\|$, and $1/s=1/p'+1/q'$ makes $(BN_{(w,p,q)},\\|\\cdot\\|_{(w,p,q)})$ an $s$-Banach operator ideal."
  2184,Nathan Wagner (joint with Cody Stockdale),"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/45/65/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.CV,math.CV math.FA mathematics complex-analysis functional-analysis talk,null,null,null,"Nathan Wagner (joint with Cody Stockdale), Berezin-transform boundary decay classifies compact Toeplitz operators on broad weighted Bergman spaces. Key computation: for $T_u f=P(uf)$ and normalized kernels $k_z(w)=(1-|z|^2)^{(n+1)/2}/(1-z\\overline w)^{n+1}$, the Berezin transform $\\widetilde T_u(z)=\\langle T_uk_z,k_z\\rangle$ satisfies $\\widetilde T_u(z)\\to0$ as $|z|\\to1^-$ iff $T_u$ is compact on $A_\\sigma^p$ when $\\sigma\\in B_p\\cap RH_r$ and $\\sigma^{1-p'}\\in RH_r$; the underlying weighted Riesz--Kolmogorov criterion is $\\lim_{r\\to1^-}\\sup_{f\\in\\mathcal F}\\int_{r\\mathbb B_n^c}|f|^p\\sigma\\,dV=0$, and sparse domination $|T_uf(z)|\\lesssim\\sum_{K\\in\\mathcal D}\\langle|uf|\\rangle_{\\widehat K}\\chi_{\\widehat K}(z)$ yields boundedness for the larger $uB_p$ classes."
  2185,"Pascal Lef\\`evre (joint with Tonie Far\\`es)","https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/39/45/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.OA,math.OA mathematics operator-algebras talk,null,null,null,"Pascal Lef\\`evre (joint with Tonie Far\\`es), an explicit integral condition completely classifies absolutely summing composition operators between Bloch-type spaces and produces nuclear inner-symbol examples. Key computation: for $\\varphi:\\mathbb D\\to\\mathbb D$, $C_\\varphi:\\mathcal B^\\mu\\to\\mathcal B^\\beta$ is $p$-summing exactly when $\\int_{\\mathbb D}\\sup_{z\\in\\mathbb D}\\left(\\frac{(1-|w|^2)^2}{|1-\\overline w\\varphi(z)|^2}\\right)^p\\left(\\frac{(1-|z|^2)^\\beta|\\varphi'(z)|}{|1-\\overline w\\varphi(z)|^\\mu}\\right)^p\\frac{dA(w)}{(1-|w|^2)^2}<\\infty$; for lens maps $\\varphi_\\theta$, this gives $C_{\\varphi_\\theta}$ $p$-summing when $p>\\theta/((\\beta-1)(1-\\theta))$ and nuclear when $\\theta<1-1/\\beta$, while suitable Blaschke products yield nuclear $C_\\varphi$ on $\\mathcal B^\\beta$ for $\\beta\\ge1$."
  2186,Hugo J,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/36/51/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.AC,math.AC math.RA math.CA math.NA mathematics commutative-algebra linear-algebra harmonic-analysis,null,null,null,"Hugo J. Woerdeman, positive structured completions of multilevel Toeplitz matrices classify stable multivariable autoregressive filters. Key computation: in two variables, Fourier data $c_k=\\overline{c_{-k}}$ on $\\mathbf n=\\{0,\\ldots,n_1\\}\\times\\{0,\\ldots,n_2\\}$ come from a stable spectral density $|p(z_1,z_2)|^{-2}$ exactly when the missing coefficients can be chosen so that $T_{\\mathbf n}=(c_{k-\\ell})_{k,\\ell\\in\\mathbf n}>0$ and $\\operatorname{rank}(c_{k-\\ell})_{k\\in\\{1,\\ldots,n_1\\}\\times\\{0,\\ldots,n_2\\},\\,\\ell\\in\\{0,\\ldots,n_1\\}\\times\\{1,\\ldots,n_2\\}}=n_1n_2$; for $c_{00}=1$ and $c_{01}=c_{10}=c_{11}=0.9$, the completion is explicitly $c_{1,-1}=0.81$, making the Toeplitz matrix positive definite and producing the stable filter."
  2187,"Herv\\'e Queff\\'elec (joint with Pascal Lef\\`evre","https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/38/65/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.CA,math.CA math.CV mathematics complex-analysis harmonic-analysis talk,null,null,null,"Herv\\'e Queff\\'elec (joint with Pascal Lef\\`evre, Daniel Li, and Luis Rodr\\'iguez-Piazza), conditional multipliers of admissible weighted Hardy spaces classify symbols by strict range contraction and finite Blaschke products. Key computation: for $H=H^2(\\beta)$ and $M(H,\\varphi)=\\{w:w(f\\circ\\varphi)\\in H\\ \\forall f\\in H\\}$, one has $M(H^2,\\varphi)\\subseteq M(H,\\varphi)$ and the sharp equivalences $M(H,\\varphi)=H\\Longleftrightarrow\\|\\varphi\\|_\\infty<1$ and $M(H,\\varphi)=H^\\infty\\Longleftrightarrow\\varphi$ is a finite Blaschke product; for $H^2$, the kernel estimate $\\|K_a\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\\le\\sqrt{(1+|\\varphi(a)|)/(1-|\\varphi(a)|)}(1-|a|^2)^{-1/2}$ turns $M(H^2,\\varphi)=H^\\infty$ into $\\sup_a(1-|\\varphi(a)|)/(1-|a|)<\\infty$, which is precisely the finite-Blaschke condition."
  2188,P,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/45/12/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.OA,math.OA mathematics operator-algebras,null,null,null,"P. Muthukumar, a Schur-class divisibility condition classifies exactly which Beurling subspaces are invariant under a composition operator. Key computation: for inner $\\theta$ and analytic $\\varphi:\\mathbb D\\to\\mathbb D$, the conditions $C_\\varphi(\\theta H^2)\\subseteq\\theta H^2$ and $(\\theta\\circ\\varphi)/\\theta\\in\\mathcal S(\\mathbb D)$ are equivalent; Douglas range inclusion rewrites this as $C_\\varphi M_\\theta=M_\\theta X$, and evaluation of $X^*M_\\theta^*=M_\\theta^*C_\\varphi^*$ on $K_w(z)=(1-\\overline wz)^{-1}$ yields $X^*(\\overline{\\theta(w)}K_w)=\\overline{\\theta(\\varphi(w))}K_{\\varphi(w)}$, giving an equivalent bounded-kernel criterion and, for Blaschke $B$, the zero-multiplicity test $\\operatorname{mult}_B(w)\\le\\operatorname{mult}_{B\\circ\\varphi}(w)$."
  2189,"Dragan Vukoti\\'c (joint work with Alexandru Aleman and Aristomenis Siskakis)","https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/39/32/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.FA,math.FA math.RA math.NA mathematics functional-analysis linear-algebra talk,null,null,null,"Dragan Vukoti\\'c (joint work with Alexandru Aleman and Aristomenis Siskakis), the conformal-invariance index of an analytic Banach space determines the complete spectrum of its Hilbert matrix operator. Key computation: the weighted automorphisms $W_a^{1/2}f=(\\varphi_a')^{1/2}(f\\circ\\varphi_a)$ have generator $Df(z)=(1-z^2)f'(z)-zf(z)$ with $\\sigma(D)=S_\\alpha=\\{\\lambda:|\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda|\\le|1-2\\alpha|\\}$, and $Kf(z)=\\int_{-1}^1f(r)/(1-rz)\\,dr=\\int_{-1}^1W_a^{1/2}f(z)\\,da/\\sqrt{1-a^2}$; spectral mapping gives $\\sigma(H)=\\sigma(K)=\\{0\\}\\cup\\{\\pi/\\cos(\\pi\\lambda/2):\\lambda\\in S_\\alpha\\}$ and $r(H)=\\pi/\\cos(\\pi|\\alpha-1/2|)$, recovering $\\sigma(H)=[0,\\pi]$ when $\\alpha=1/2$."
  2190,Francesco Fournier-Facio (joint with Bharatram Rangarajan),"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/57/71/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.DS,math.DS math.CT math.CA math.RA mathematics ergodic-theory homological-algebra ode-and-dynamical-systems talk,null,null,null,"Francesco Fournier-Facio (joint with Bharatram Rangarajan), every lamplighter $\\Gamma\\wr\\mathbb Z$ is Ulam stable with a linear estimate, which implies the same for Thompson's groups $F',F,T$ and embeds every countable group in a three-generated linearly Ulam-stable group. Key computation: an asymptotic homomorphism $\\varphi_n:\\Gamma\\to U(k_n)$ defines $W=\\prod_{n\\to\\omega}\\mathfrak u(k_n)$ and an Ulam class $\\alpha_\\varphi\\in H_a^2(\\Gamma,W)$, with $\\alpha_\\varphi=0$ equivalent to a defect-diminishing correction $\\operatorname{dist}(\\varphi_n,\\psi_n)=O_\\omega(\\operatorname{def}(\\varphi_n))$ and $\\operatorname{def}(\\psi_n)=o_\\omega(\\operatorname{def}(\\varphi_n))$; the highly ergodic Zimmer-amenable model for $\\Gamma\\wr\\mathbb Z$ makes the relevant invariant cochain complex alternate $\\operatorname{id}$ and $0$, killing every Ulam class, while the coamenable inclusion $F[a,b]\\cong F< F'$ injects restriction on $H_a^2$ and transfers the vanishing to $F',F,T$."
  2191,Stephen S,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/26/61/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.NT,math.NT math.AG math.CA math.CO mathematics algebraic-geometry graph-theory harmonic-analysis modular-forms,null,null,null,"Stephen S. Kudla, the numerical ring of special cycles on an orthogonal Shimura variety is determined by Fourier coefficients of pullbacks of Hilbert--Siegel Eisenstein series and depends only on the finite quadratic space. Key computation: the theta-form identity $[\\theta(g_\\tau';\\varphi)]=\\phi_n(\\tau;\\varphi)=\\sum_{T\\ge0}Z(T,\\varphi)q^T$ and wedge compatibility $\\varphi_\\infty^{(n_1+n_2)}=\\varphi_\\infty^{(n_1)}\\wedge\\varphi_\\infty^{(n_2)}$, followed by Siegel--Weil, give $\\langle z(T_1,\\varphi_1),z(T_2,\\varphi_2)\\rangle=A(T_1,T_2;\\lambda_{V_f}(\\varphi_1\\otimes\\varphi_2))$ for $n_1+n_2=m$ and $\\langle z(T_1,\\varphi_1)z(T_2,\\varphi_2),z(T_3,\\varphi_3)\\rangle=A(T_1,T_2,T_3;\\lambda_{V_f}(\\varphi_1\\otimes\\varphi_2\\otimes\\varphi_3))$ for $n_1+n_2+n_3=m$; nondegeneracy determines multiplication, and matching Schwartz functions for $V(\\mathbb A_f)\\simeq V'(\\mathbb A_f)$ yield an isometric graded-ring isomorphism $SC^\\bullet(V)\\simeq SC^\\bullet(V')$."
  2192,Iskander A,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/23/12/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.AT,math.AT math.RT math.RA mathematics algebraic-topology lie-theory,null,null,null,"Iskander A. Taimanov, the generalized Kummer resolution gives an explicit intersection-ring classification for a simply connected Joyce $G_2$-manifold and shows that it has no nontrivial Massey products. Key computation: for the commuting involutions $\\alpha,\\beta,\\gamma$ on $T^7$ with $\\Gamma\\cong(\\mathbb Z/2)^3$, one computes $b_1(T^7/\\Gamma)=b_2(T^7/\\Gamma)=0$ and $b_3(T^7/\\Gamma)=b_4(T^7/\\Gamma)=7$; resolving the twelve singular $T^3\\times(D/\\{\\pm1\\})$ strata adds classes giving $b_2(M)=12$ and $b_3(M)=43$, and the nonzero products in the homology intersection ring are $c_{\\delta i}\\cap c'_{\\delta i}=-2$, $c_{\\delta ij}\\cap c'_{\\delta ij}=-2$, $t_\\delta\\cap t'_\\delta=4$, $t_i\\cap t'_i=8$, $c'_{\\delta i}\\cap c'_{\\delta i}=-2t_\\delta$, and $t'_\\delta\\cap c'_{\\delta i}=4c_{\\delta i}$, which by Poincar\\'e duality determines $H^*(M;\\mathbb Q)$."
  2193,Herbert Gangl,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/41/26/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.AG,math.AG math.NT math.CA mathematics motives l-functions special-functions,null,null,null,"Herbert Gangl, explicit polylogarithmic functional equations realize Zagier's polylogarithm conjecture through weight four and construct higher-weight analogues of the decisive motivic relation. Key computation: Bloch's map $\\delta_2^F:\\mathbb Z[F]\\to\\bigwedge^2F^\\times$, $[x]\\mapsto x\\wedge(1-x)$, together with the exact sequence $0\\to T(F)\\to K_3(F)/K_3^M(F)\\to B_2(F)\\to0$, turns the single-valued dilogarithm into the regulator formula $\\pi^{r_2}\\zeta_F^*(-1)=q\\det(\\mathcal L_2(\\xi_i^\\sigma))_{i,\\sigma}$; at weight four an explicit 122-term reduction $I_{3,1}(z,V(x,y))\\equiv\\sum_{i=1}^{122}c_i\\operatorname{Li}_4(f_i(x,y,z))$ modulo lower-weight products supplies the $Q_4$ relation used by Goncharov--Rudenko to prove the conjecture for $m=4$, while the explicit quadruple ratio is a 368-orbit combination on eight points in $\\mathbb P^3$ representing a nonzero class in $H^7(GL_4(\\mathbb C),\\mathbb R)$."
  2194,Jongbaek Song (joint with Xin Fu and Tseleung So),"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/2/51/02/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.AT,math.AT math.RA math.NA mathematics algebraic-topology linear-algebra talk,null,null,null,"Jongbaek Song (joint with Xin Fu and Tseleung So), the fan vectors of a compact toric surface determine its integral cohomology ring through an explicit cup-product matrix. Key computation: for rays $(-1,0),(0,-1),(a_1,b_1),\\ldots,(a_n,b_n)$, a cellular cofibration $S^3\\to\\bigvee_{i=1}^nS_i^2\\to X_\\Sigma$ yields bases $H^2(X_\\Sigma;\\mathbb Z)=\\mathbb Z\\langle u_1,\\ldots,u_n\\rangle$ and $H^4(X_\\Sigma;\\mathbb Z)=\\mathbb Z\\langle v\\rangle$; the weighted Stanley--Reisner model and the morphisms $X_\\Sigma\\to\\mathbb{CP}_{1,a_i,b_i}^2$ send $[a_ib_ix_1]$ to $\\sum_{k<i}a_kb_i y_k+a_ib_i y_i+\\sum_{k>i}a_ib_k y_k$ and $[x_2x_3]$ to $[y_{n+1}y_{n+2}]$, so $u_i\\smile u_j=a_{\\min(i,j)}b_{\\max(i,j)}v$ and $M(X_\\Sigma)_{ij}=a_{\\min(i,j)}b_{\\max(i,j)}$."
  2195,Lisa Jeffrey (joint with Nan-Kuo Ho,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/26/31/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.AG,math.AG mathematics algebraic-geometry singularities talk,null,null,null,"Lisa Jeffrey (joint with Nan-Kuo Ho, Paul Selick, and Eugene Xia), Mayer--Vietoris decompositions compute the integral cohomology ring of the genus-two $SU(2)$ character variety with puncture monodromy $-I$ and identify its connected-sum type. Key computation: with $M=\\mu^{-1}(-I)$ and $A=M/SU(2)$, the commutator trace stratification gives $A\\simeq T\\cup_{\\mathbb{RP}^3\\times S^2}T$, where $T=\\{(g,h):[g,h]=I\\}\\simeq S^3\\vee S^3\\vee S^2\\vee\\Sigma^2\\mathbb{RP}^2$; the prequantum bundle Mayer--Vietoris sequence $0\\to\\mathbb Z/2\\to H^4(L;\\mathbb Z)\\to\\mathbb Z/2\\to0$ and $H^4(L;\\mathbb Z/2)=\\mathbb Z/2$ force $H^4(L;\\mathbb Z)=\\mathbb Z/4$, and yield $H^*(A)=\\langle1,x,s_1,s_2,s_3,s_4,y,z\\rangle$ with $|x|=2$, $|s_i|=3$, $|y|=4$, $|z|=6$, $x^2=4y$, and $xy=s_1s_3=s_2s_4=z$; Wall's theorem then gives $A\\cong A^0\\#(S^3\\times S^3)\\#(S^3\\times S^3)$."
  2196,Ron Donagi,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/2/42/29/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.AG,math.AG mathematics moduli-spaces,null,null,null,"Ron Donagi, supermoduli is non-split and non-projected in genus at least five, while the super Mumford isomorphism constructs natural measures that extend across the vanishing-theta-null locus and, with Ramond punctures, across codimension at least two. Key computation: a supermanifold $S=(M,\\mathcal O_S)$ has splitting obstruction $\\omega\\in H^1(M,T_M\\otimes\\bigwedge^2V^*)$, appearing as the third component of its super Atiyah class alongside the Atiyah classes of $T_+S$ and $T_-S$; for $\\mathfrak M_g$, $\\dim\\mathfrak M_g=(3g-3\\mid2g-2)$ with $T_+|_{[S]}=H^1(T_M)$ and $T_-|_{[S]}=H^1(T_M^{1/2})$, and restricting $\\omega$ to a lifted family of odd-branched covers gives $\\omega\\ne0$ for $g\\ge5$; the identity $\\mathcal L_{3/2}\\simeq\\mathcal L_{1/2}^{5}$ defines the superstring measure, and a codimension-$\\ge2$ Hartogs extension gives a natural measure on $\\mathfrak M_{g,0,r}$ for $r\\ge2$ Ramond punctures."
  2197,Meng Fai Lim,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/33/73/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.NT,math.NT math.AG math.AT mathematics arithmetic-statistics etale-cohomology iwasawa-theory topological-hochschild-homology,null,null,null,"Meng Fai Lim, the growth of \\'etale wild kernels in multi-false-Tate towers is controlled by an Iwasawa-module rank, and its smaller-order growth is equivalent to the noncommutative Greenberg conjecture. Key computation: Poitou--Tate duality identifies $R_i(F)=WK_{2i}^{\\mathrm{\\acute et}}(F)^\\vee$ with the kernel of $H^1(G_S(F),\\mathbb Q_p/\\mathbb Z_p(-i))\\to\\bigoplus_{v\\in S}H^1(F_v,\\mathbb Q_p/\\mathbb Z_p(-i))$; Hochschild--Serre gives $\\ker h_n=H^1(G_n,\\mathbb Q_p/\\mathbb Z_p(-i)(F_\\infty))$ and $\\operatorname{coker}h_n\\subseteq H^2(G_n,\\mathbb Q_p/\\mathbb Z_p(-i)(F_\\infty))$, yielding $e(R_i(F_\\infty)^{G_n})-e(R_i(F_n))=O(np^{(d-2)n})$; if the cyclotomic $\\mu$-conjecture holds, then $e(WK_{2i}^{\\mathrm{\\acute et}}(F_n))=\\operatorname{rank}_{\\mathbb Z_p[[H]]}Y_i(F_\\infty)\\,np^{(d-1)n}+O(p^{(d-1)n})$, and vanishing of this rank is equivalent to $\\operatorname{Gal}(K(F_\\infty)/F_\\infty)$ being pseudo-null."
  2198,Soumen Sarkar,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/2/50/96/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.AT,math.AT math.CO mathematics equivariant-topology graph-theory,null,null,null,"Soumen Sarkar, weighted Grassmann orbifolds have torsion-free integral cohomology concentrated in even degrees, and their integral equivariant cohomology is completely encoded by an orbifold GKM graph. Key computation: for $W=(w_1,\\ldots,w_n)$ and $a\\ge1$, $WGr(d,n)=M_d(n,d;\\mathbb C)/{\\sim_W}$ with $(a_1,\\ldots,a_n)^t\\sim_W(t^{w_1}a_1,\\ldots,t^{w_n}a_n)^tT$ whenever $t^a=\\det T$; its finite-quotient strata satisfy that $H_j(U(L)/G;\\mathbb Z)$ has no $p$-torsion for $p\\nmid|G|$, producing a torsion-free even-dimensional cellular filtration; each $WGr(d,n)$ is consequently a GKM orbifold and $H_{T^n}^*(WGr(d,n);\\mathbb Z)\\cong\\{f:V(\\Gamma)\\to H^*(BT^n;\\mathbb Z)\\mid r_e\\alpha(e)\\text{ divides }f(s(e))-f(t(e))\\}=H_{T^n}^*(\\Gamma,\\alpha,\\theta)$."
  2199,Payman Eskandari (joint with Kumar Murty),"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/27/43/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.AT,math.AT math.CO math.CT math.LO mathematics fixed-point-theorems graph-theory homological-algebra mathematical-logic talk,null,null,null,"Payman Eskandari (joint with Kumar Murty), the Ceresa cycle of every Fermat curve $F(p)$ of prime degree $p>7$ has infinite order modulo rational equivalence, for every base point. Key computation: quadratic iterated integrals give $E_e^\\infty\\in\\operatorname{Ext}((H^1)^{\\otimes3},\\mathbb Q(-1))$, and for a correspondence $Z$ the Darmon--Rotger--Sols formula is $\\xi_Z^{-1}(E_e^\\infty)=(\\int_\\Delta\\xi_Z)(\\infty-e)-P_Z$; taking $Z$ as the graph of $(x,y,z)\\mapsto(-y,z,x)$ on $F(p)$ gives fixed points $Q=(\\eta,\\eta^{-1},1)$ and $\\bar Q=(\\eta^{-1},\\eta,1)$ and hence $P_Z=Q+\\bar Q-2e$, which Gross--Rohrlich proves has infinite order while $\\infty-e$ is torsion; thus $E_e^\\infty\\ne0$, its $H^1\\otimes\\xi_\\Delta$ component $-2g\\infty+2e+K$ is torsion, and Harris--Pulte identifies the remaining component with $\\tfrac12AJ(F(p)_e-F(p)_e^-)$, forcing the Abel--Jacobi image, and therefore the Ceresa cycle, to be nonzero."
  2200,Avi Zeff,"https://www.math.columbia.edu/~avizeff/seminars/other/prismatic.pdf",columbia.edu,math.AT,math.AT math.AG math.NT math.AC mathematics topological-hochschild-homology algebraic-topology commutative-algebra prismatic-cohomology,null,null,null,"Avi Zeff, prismatic cohomology of quasiregular semiperfectoid rings is computed in degree zero and its Nygaard completion recovers the even homotopy of $\\mathrm{THH}$, $\\mathrm{TC}^-$, and $\\mathrm{TP}$. Key computation: the initial prism $(\\Delta_R^{\\mathrm{init}},(d))$ gives $\\Delta_{R/A}=\\Delta_R=\\Delta_R^{\\mathrm{init}}$ and $N^{\\ge i}\\Delta_R=\\varphi^{-1}(d^i\\Delta_R)$; after Nygaard completion, $\\pi_{2*}\\mathrm{THH}(R)=N^*\\widehat\\Delta_R\\{*\\}$, $\\pi_{2*}\\mathrm{TC}^-(R)=N^{\\ge *}\\widehat\\Delta_R\\{*\\}$, and $\\pi_{2*}\\mathrm{TP}(R)=\\widehat\\Delta_R\\{*\\}$, with all odd homotopy groups vanishing."
  2201,"David Mart\\'inez-Carpena (with Carles Casacuberta and Javier J","http://xabier.garcia.martinez.webs.uvigo.gal/CT2024/files/Slides/Mart%C3%ADnez-Carpena_Slides.pdf",uvigo.gal,math.CT,math.CT math.AG cs.LO math.NT mathematics computer-science category-theory field-with-one-element type-theory,null,null,null,"David Mart\\'inez-Carpena (with Carles Casacuberta and Javier J. Guti\\'errez), presentable $\\infty$-categories are exactly the categories of models of limit $\\infty$-sketches. Key computation: for a limit $\\infty$-sketch $\\Sigma=(A,L)$, $\\operatorname{Mod}(\\Sigma)=\\{F:A\\to\\mathcal S\\mid F(\\alpha)\\text{ is limiting for every }\\alpha\\in L\\}$ is presentable, and conversely every presentable $\\infty$-category $\\mathcal C$ admits a normal $\\Sigma$ with $\\mathcal C\\simeq\\operatorname{Mod}(\\Sigma)$; concretely, the cones $\\alpha_n$ on $\\Delta^{\\mathrm{op}}$ impose $F_n\\simeq F_1\\times_{F_0}\\cdots\\times_{F_0}F_1$, so their models are Segal spaces, while adjoining the univalence cone produces complete Segal spaces."
  2202,"Andr\\'e Joyal","http://xabier.garcia.martinez.webs.uvigo.gal/CT2024/files/Slides/Joyal_Slides.pdf",uvigo.gal,math.AC,math.AC math.CT mathematics commutative-algebra category-theory,null,null,null,"Andr\\'e Joyal, the free bicompletion of a locally small category is characterized intrinsically by softness together with atomic and co-atomic generators. Key computation: softness requires the canonical square from $\\operatorname*{colim}_I\\operatorname*{lim}_J\\mathcal E(A_i,B_j)$ to $\\mathcal E(\\operatorname*{lim}_I A_i,\\operatorname*{colim}_J B_j)$ to be a pushout; hence every $f:\\prod_{i\\in I}A_i\\to\\coprod_{j\\in J}B_j$ factors through some projection $\\prod_iA_i\\to A_i$, some inclusion $B_j\\to\\coprod_jB_j$, or both, and $\\eta:K\\to\\Lambda(K)$ is the free bicompletion precisely when $\\Lambda(K)$ is bicomplete and soft, $\\eta$ is fully faithful and generating, and each $\\eta(A)$ is atomic and co-atomic."
  2203,Nathanael Arkor and Dylan McDermott,"http://xabier.garcia.martinez.webs.uvigo.gal/CT2024/files/Slides/Arkor_Slides.pdf",uvigo.gal,math.CT,math.CT mathematics category-theory,null,null,null,"Nathanael Arkor and Dylan McDermott, the algebras of any relative monad rooted at a dense functor are reconstructed as a pullback along the corresponding nerve, subsuming standard nerve theorems. Key computation: for $j:A\\to E$ dense and a $j$-relative monad $T$ with $\\operatorname{Kl}(T)(x,y)=E(jx,ty)$, the square $\\operatorname{Alg}(T)\\to\\widehat{\\operatorname{Kl}(T)}$ over $E\\xrightarrow{n_j}\\widehat A$, where $n_j(e)=E(j-,e)$, is a pullback in $\\operatorname{Cat}$; thus $\\operatorname{Kl}(T)\\hookrightarrow\\operatorname{Alg}(T)$ is dense, and for an ordinary monad the analogous square is a pullback exactly when $T$ is $j$-ary, recovering algebraic theories and the Segal-nerve classification of categories."
  2204,Joanna Ko,"http://xabier.garcia.martinez.webs.uvigo.gal/CT2024/files/Slides/Ko_Slides.pdf",uvigo.gal,math.CT,math.CT math.AT mathematics category-theory algebraic-topology higher-category-theory,null,null,null,"Joanna Ko, enhanced simplicial categories of $(\\infty,1)$-categories with specified limits and lax morphisms admit the higher limit constructions needed for Eilenberg--Moore objects. Key computation: $J\\text{-}\\operatorname{Lim}$ admits rigged $n$-inserters, so in particular comma objects whose projections preserve and jointly reflect $J$-shaped limits; since a loose homotopy-coherent monad's Eilenberg--Moore object is assembled from simplicial powers, rigged $n$-inserters, and limits of countable chains of tight isofibrations, $\\operatorname{Lali}(\\mathcal K)$ admits such Eilenberg--Moore objects and its forgetful functor is exact and detects exactness, recovering that $U:T\\text{-}\\operatorname{Alg}\\to A$ lifts $J$-limits even when $T$ does not preserve them."
  2205,Seerp Roald Koudenburg,"http://xabier.garcia.martinez.webs.uvigo.gal/CT2024/files/Slides/Koudenburg_Slides.pdf",uvigo.gal,math.CT,math.CT math.QA mathematics category-theory monoidal-categories algebraic-topology,null,null,null,"Seerp Roald Koudenburg, formal Day convolution lifts Yoneda morphisms and yields a monoidal double Grothendieck correspondence. Key computation: a lax $T$-algebra $(A,\\alpha)$ and Yoneda morphism $y:A\\to\\widehat A$ lift, when the pointwise weak composite $\\alpha_*\\odot Ty_*$ exists, to a lax $T$-algebra structure on $\\widehat A$ for which $y$ is pseudo-$T$, giving $\\widehat A(y,-):(\\operatorname{Lax}\\text{-}T\\text{-}\\operatorname{Mor}(B,\\widehat A))\\simeq(T\\text{-}\\operatorname{Mor}(A,B))$; for $y:A\\to\\operatorname{Set}^{A^{\\mathrm{op}}}$ this constructs Day convolution, while for $Y:1\\to\\operatorname{Span}(\\operatorname{Cat})$ it identifies lax monoidal lax double functors $G:(B,\\otimes)\\to(\\operatorname{Span}(\\operatorname{Cat}),\\times)$ with lax monoidal double split opfibrations $\\int G\\to B$."
  2206,Fosco Loregian,"http://xabier.garcia.martinez.webs.uvigo.gal/CT2024/files/Slides/Loregian_Slides.pdf",uvigo.gal,math.CT,math.CT mathematics category-theory higher-category-theory enriched-and-formal-category-theory monoidal-categories,null,null,null,"Fosco Loregian, bicategorical Moore machines are models of a finite weighted-limit sketch and their universal machine is computed by a right Kan extension. Key computation: classical machines arise from pullbacks $\\operatorname{Mly}(F,B)=\\operatorname{Alg}(F)\\times_K(F/B)$ and $\\operatorname{Mre}(F,B)=\\operatorname{Alg}(F)\\times_K(K/B)$; in a bicategory, a fixed-input/output biMoore machine fills the $2$-cells $\\delta:e\\circ i\\Rightarrow e$ and $\\sigma:e\\Rightarrow o$, and its terminal object is $\\operatorname{Ran}_{i^\\sharp}(o)$ along the free monad $i^\\sharp:A\\to A$, recovering $[A^*,B]$ in the one-object closed-monoidal case and extending the construction to $(T,V)$-profunctors."
  2207,Arne Mertens (joint with Wendy Lowen),"http://xabier.garcia.martinez.webs.uvigo.gal/CT2024/files/Slides/Mertens_Slides.pdf",uvigo.gal,math.CT,math.CT mathematics category-theory enriched-and-formal-category-theory higher-category-theory monoidal-categories talk,null,null,null,"Arne Mertens (joint with Wendy Lowen), necklace-parametrized templicial objects define quasi-categories enriched in a non-cartesian monoidal category and explicitly compute left adjoints to enriched nerve functors. Key computation: the nerve has $N_V(C)_n=C_1\\otimes_{C_0}\\cdots\\otimes_{C_0}C_1$ and comultiplications $\\mu_{k,l}:X_{k+l}\\to X_k\\otimes_{X_0}X_l$ indexed by inclusions $\\Delta^k\\vee\\Delta^l\\hookrightarrow\\Delta^{k+l}$; if $\\pi D\\simeq\\operatorname{Lan}_\\iota D'$, then $\\pi(L_DX(a,b))\\simeq\\coprod_{T\\in\\operatorname{Nec}}F(X_T^{\\mathrm{nd}}(a,b))\\otimes D'(T)$, yielding for Lurie's dg nerve $L_{\\mathrm{dg}}(X)_n(a,b)\\simeq\\bigoplus_{\\dim T=n}k\\!\\cdot\\!X_T^{\\mathrm{nd}}(a,b)$."
  2208,Samarpita Ray (joint with Abhishek Banerjee and Mamta Balodi),"http://xabier.garcia.martinez.webs.uvigo.gal/CT2024/files/Slides/Ray_Slides.pdf",uvigo.gal,cs.DC,cs.DC math.CT math.AG math.RT computer-science mathematics distributed-systems category-theory representation-theory sheaf-theory talk,null,null,null,"Samarpita Ray (joint with Abhishek Banerjee and Mamta Balodi), representations of coalgebras have Grothendieck categories of cis- and trans-comodules, with cartesian subcategories serving as coalgebraic analogues of quasi-coherent sheaves. Key computation: for $C:X\\to\\operatorname{Coalg}$ with right-semiperfect values, a map $V\\to M_x$ from a finite-dimensional projective generates $N_y=\\operatorname{Im}(\\bigoplus_{\\beta:x\\to y}\\beta_*V\\to M_y)$ with $|N|\\le\\kappa=\\sup\\{\\aleph_0,|\\operatorname{Mor}X|,|K|\\}$, and every $M$ is a sum of such $N$, proving $\\operatorname{Com}^{\\mathrm{cs}}\\!\\text{-}C$ is Grothendieck; if $C$ is quasi-finite then $\\alpha_!=H_{C_y}(C_x,-)\\dashv\\alpha^*$ gives the corresponding Grothendieck classification for $\\operatorname{Com}^{\\mathrm{tr}}\\!\\text{-}C$, while semiperfect $C$ makes $\\operatorname{Com}^{\\mathrm{cs}}\\!\\text{-}C$ a hereditary torsion class in $C^*\\text{-}\\operatorname{Mod}^{\\mathrm{tr}}$."
  2209,"Brais Ramos P\\'erez","http://xabier.garcia.martinez.webs.uvigo.gal/CT2024/files/Slides/RamosP%C3%A9rez_Slides.pdf",uvigo.gal,math.RA,math.RA math.CT math.GR mathematics abstract-algebra category-theory,null,null,null,"Brais Ramos P\\'erez, modules over invertible Hopf-algebra $1$-cocycles are equivalent to modules over the associated Hopf braces. Key computation: an invertible $1$-cocycle is a coalgebra isomorphism $\\pi:A\\to H$ satisfying $\\pi\\circ\\mu_A=\\mu_H\\circ(\\pi\\otimes\\varphi_H)\\circ(\\delta_A\\otimes\\pi)$; it produces the brace $H_\\pi=Q(\\pi,\\varphi_H)$, and the functor $H_\\pi:(\\pi,\\varphi_H)\\text{-}\\operatorname{Mod}\\to H_\\pi\\text{-}\\operatorname{Mod}$ sends $(M,N,\\varphi_M,\\phi_M,\\varphi_N,\\gamma)$ to $(M,\\phi_M,\\gamma\\circ\\varphi_N\\circ(\\pi^{-1}\\otimes\\gamma^{-1}))$, with inverse $M_{(\\pi,\\operatorname{id}_H)}\\circ G_{H_\\pi}$, so the composites are $\\operatorname{id}_{H_\\pi\\text{-}\\operatorname{Mod}}$ and naturally isomorphic to $\\operatorname{id}_{(\\pi,\\varphi_H)\\text{-}\\operatorname{Mod}}$."
  2210,"Ivan Limonchenko (joint with Rade \\v{Z}ivaljevi\\'c and Matvey Sergeev)","https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/2/49/55/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.CO,math.CO math.AT math.GT math.DG mathematics differential-topology discrete-geometry toric-topology talk,null,null,null,"Ivan Limonchenko (joint with Rade \\v{Z}ivaljevi\\'c and Matvey Sergeev), flag Bier spheres are classified by four families of simple polytopes, and every Bier sphere canonically determines smooth moment-angle and toric manifolds. Key computation: every flag $\\operatorname{Bier}(K)$ is the nerve of $I^n$, $I^n\\times Q_5$, $I^n\\times Q_6$, or $I^n\\times P_4$; moreover $s_{\\mathbb R}(\\operatorname{Bier}(K))=s(\\operatorname{Bier}(K))=m+1$, and the cones $C(\\sigma,\\tau)=\\mathbb R_{\\ge0}\\langle-e_p,e_q\\mid p\\in\\sigma,\\ q'\\in\\tau\\rangle$ form a complete regular fan $\\Sigma(K)\\subset\\mathbb R^{m-1}$, producing the smooth manifolds $Z_{\\operatorname{Bier}(K)}^{3m-1}$ and $X_{\\Sigma(K)}^{2(m-1)}$."
  2211,Daisuke Kishimoto (joint with Kouyemon Iriye),"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/2/49/64/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.AG,math.AG math.AT math.CT math.NT mathematics algebraic-topology homological-algebra prismatic-cohomology talk,null,null,null,"Daisuke Kishimoto (joint with Kouyemon Iriye), a triangulation of an $\\mathbb F$-orientable manifold is $\\mathbb F$-Golod if and only if it is $\\mathbb F$-tight. Key computation: the dga $C^*(K)$ with $C^p(K)=\\bigoplus_{\\varnothing\\ne I\\subset[m]}\\widetilde C^{p-|I|-1}(K_I)$ is homotopy equivalent to the Koszul complex of $\\mathbb F[K]$; tightness lifts cocycles from $\\widetilde C^*(K_I)$ to $\\widetilde C^*(K)$, and higher prism operators $P_H$ obey $\\partial P_H+(-1)^{q-1}P_H\\partial=\\sum_{i=0}^q(-1)^iP_{H\\circ(1\\otimes d^i)}$, furnishing the higher homotopies that kill every product and Massey product in $H^*(C^*(K))\\cong\\operatorname{Tor}^S(\\mathbb F[K],\\mathbb F)$."
  2212,Alexandru Suciu (joint work with Aprodu,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/2/49/66/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.AG,math.AG math.CO math.CT mathematics algebraic-geometry schemes algebraic-combinatorics homological-algebra talk,null,null,null,"Alexandru Suciu (joint work with Aprodu, Farkas, Raicu, and Sama), the support resonance schemes of every simplicial complex are reduced coordinate-subspace arrangements, and their Koszul modules are computed from induced-subcomplex cohomology. Key computation: for $\\Delta$ on $V=[n]$, $\\mathcal R^i(\\Delta)=\\bigcup_{W\\subseteq V\\,\\mathrm{maximal},\\,\\widetilde H^{i-1}(\\Delta_W;k)\\ne0}k^W$ is the irreducible-component decomposition of the reduced support scheme, while for every square-free multi-index $\\mathbf b$ there are natural isomorphisms $[W_i(\\Delta)]_{\\mathbf b}\\cong\\operatorname{Tor}^{S}_{|\\mathbf b|-i}(k,k[\\Delta])_{\\mathbf b}\\cong\\widetilde H^{i-1}(\\Delta_{\\mathbf b};k)^\\vee$, yielding its multigraded Hilbert series by summing these cohomology dimensions."
  2213,Xin Fu (joint with Tseleung So,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/2/49/76/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.AT,math.AT math.NT cs.LO mathematics computer-science number-theory algebraic-topology homotopy-theory toric-topology talk,null,null,null,"Xin Fu (joint with Tseleung So, Jongbaek Song, and Stephen Theriault), the integral cohomology ring determines the away-from-small-primes homotopy type of quasitoric manifolds over products of equal-dimensional simplices. Key computation: if $M,N$ are $2n$-manifolds over $\\prod_{i=1}^{\\ell}\\Delta^d$ and $H^*(M)\\cong H^*(N)$, the fibration $\\prod_{i=1}^{\\ell}S^{2d+1}\\to M\\to BT^{m-n}$ gives $\\operatorname{skel}_{2d}(M)\\simeq\\operatorname{skel}_{2d}(BT^{m-n})\\simeq\\operatorname{skel}_{2d}(N)$; for $k>d$, the attaching map homomorphism $g_M:H_{2k+2}(M)\\to\\pi_{2k+1}(\\operatorname{skel}_{2k}M)$ is an isomorphism after inverting all primes $p\\le n/d+1$, so induction over even skeleta yields $M\\simeq N$ with those primes inverted."
  2214,"Shintar\\^o Kuroki (joint with Yael Karshon)","https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/2/50/97/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.DG,math.DG math.AG math.AT math.CT mathematics category-theory characteristic-classes differential-geometry intersection-theory talk,null,null,null,"Shintar\\^o Kuroki (joint with Yael Karshon), locally standard torus actions are classified up to equivariant diffeomorphism by an orbit manifold, its unimodular facet labeling, and a Chern class. Key computation: to $T^d\\curvearrowright M$ one assigns $Q=M/T$, $\\widehat\\lambda:\\partial_1^\\circ Q\\to(t_{\\mathbb Z})_{\\mathrm{primitive}}/\\{\\pm1\\}$, and $c\\in H^2(Q;t_{\\mathbb Z})$; the exact sequence $0\\to t_{\\mathbb Z}\\to\\mathbb R^d\\to T^d\\to0$, together with $H^q(\\mathring Q;\\mathbb R^d)=0$ and $Q\\simeq\\mathring Q$, identifies $H^1(\\mathring Q;T^d)\\cong H^2(Q;t_{\\mathbb Z})$, so the free principal bundle $M_{\\mathrm{free}}\\to\\mathring Q$ is recovered from $c$, and the functor $\\mathrm{Cl}(M)=(Q,\\widehat\\lambda,c)$ is full and essentially surjective."
  2215,Tao Gong,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/2/51/05/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.AG,math.AG math.CO math.AT mathematics algebraic-geometry algebraic-topology discrete-geometry toric-geometry,null,null,null,"Tao Gong, the quotient of a toric variety attached to a Weyl polytope is homotopy equivalent to the toric variety attached to the quotient polytope, for every Lie type and every parabolic subgroup. Key computation: writing $X_P/W\\cong(S_N\\times(P\\cap C))/\\!\\sim_{\\mathrm{ed}}$ and $X_{P/W}\\cong(S_N\\times(P\\cap C))/\\!\\sim$, the identity on representatives induces $\\Phi:X_P/W\\to X_{P/W}$; if $p\\in\\operatorname{relint}(H_I\\cap F_J\\cap C)$, then $\\Phi^{-1}([t,p])\\cong\\bigl(S_N(\\sigma_{[n],p})/S_N(\\sigma_p)\\bigr)/W_I\\cong\\bigl(\\operatorname{Span}\\{\\alpha_i:i\\in I\\}/S_{P^\\vee_{I,J}}\\bigr)/W_I$, a locally contractible finite-affine quotient, so Smale's theorem gives $\\Phi\\simeq$ a homotopy equivalence; the same argument yields $X_P/W_K\\simeq X_{P/W_K}$."
  2216,Seonghyeon Yu (joint with Suyoung Choi and Younghan Yoon),"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/2/51/20/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.AG,math.AG math.RT math.AT math.RA mathematics lie-theory algebraic-geometry algebraic-groups algebraic-topology talk,null,null,null,"Seonghyeon Yu (joint with Suyoung Choi and Younghan Yoon), the rational homology of the real toric varieties associated with the exceptional Weyl groups $E_7$ and $E_8$ is computed explicitly. Key computation: the Weyl-equivariant decomposition $H^*(X_R^{\\mathbb R})\\cong\\bigoplus_{S\\in\\operatorname{Row}(\\Lambda_R)}\\widetilde H^{*-1}(K_S)$ reduces the problem to orbit representatives of induced subcomplexes; their computed reduced Betti numbers give $(\\beta_0,\\ldots,\\beta_4)(X_{E_7}^{\\mathbb R};\\mathbb Q)=(1,63,8127,131041,122976)$ and $(\\beta_0,\\ldots,\\beta_4)(X_{E_8}^{\\mathbb R};\\mathbb Q)=(1,120,103815,6925200,23932800)$, with all other $\\beta_k$ equal to zero."
  2217,Tyler Schulz (joint with Marcelo Laca),"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/73/42/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.CO,math.CO math.PR mathematics extremal-combinatorics probability talk,null,null,null,"Tyler Schulz (joint with Marcelo Laca), for $0<\\beta\\le1$ the right $ax+b$ system has a simplex of KMS$_\\beta$ states parametrized by probability measures on $\\mathbb N^\\times\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$, whose finite-point extremals generate type $\\mathrm{III}_1$ factors. Key computation: every KMS$_\\beta$ state factors through $C(X)$ with $X\\cong\\varprojlim_{a\\mid b}X_a$, $X_a=\\mathbb T\\times\\Delta_a$, and transition maps $f_{a,b}(z,d)=(z^{d/\\gcd(a,d)},\\gcd(a,d))$; the extremal $\\psi_{n,\\beta}$ further factors through equivariant $*$-homomorphisms $\\mathcal T(\\mathbb N^\\times\\ltimes\\mathbb Z)\\to\\mathcal T(\\mathbb N^\\times\\ltimes(\\mathbb Z/n\\mathbb Z))\\to C_{\\mathbb Q}$, where the intermediate quotient imposes $U^n=1$, producing the Dirac parameter $\\delta_n$ and its type $\\mathrm{III}_1$ GNS factor."
  2218,"Roberto Hern\\'andez Palomares (joint with Brent Nelson)","https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/73/65/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.AG,math.AG math.CT math.NT math.OA mathematics category-theory perfectoid-spaces field-with-one-element operator-algebras talk,null,null,null,"Roberto Hern\\'andez Palomares (joint with Brent Nelson), irreducible $C^*$-discrete inclusions are precisely reduced crossed products by outer unitary tensor-category actions and therefore admit a categorical standard invariant. Key computation: for a faithful expectation $E:A\\subset B$, the canonical correspondence has $\\langle b_1\\mid b_2\\rangle_A=E(b_1^*b_2)$ and the algebraic discrete part decomposes as $B_\\diamond\\cong\\bigoplus_{K\\in\\operatorname{Irr}(\\operatorname{Bim}_{\\mathrm{fgp}}(A))}K\\otimes\\operatorname{Hom}_{A-A}(K,B\\Omega)$; the condition $\\overline{B_\\diamond}^{\\|\\cdot\\|_B}=B$ and $A'\\cap B=\\mathbb C$ is equivalent to data $F:\\mathcal C\\curvearrowright A$, $\\mathbf B\\in C^*\\!\\operatorname{Alg}(\\mathcal C)$ with $A\\subset B\\cong A\\subset A\\rtimes_{F,r}\\mathbf B$, as illustrated by $\\mathcal O_n\\cong\\operatorname{UHF}_{n^\\infty}\\rtimes_{F_1,r}\\mathbb C[\\mathbb Z]$."
  2219,"Marie-France Vign\\'eras (joint with Guy Henniart)","https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/2/65/22/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.RT,math.RT math.AP math.AG mathematics geometric-representation-theory pde representation-theory talk,null,null,null,"Marie-France Vign\\'eras (joint with Guy Henniart), an admissible representation of $\\operatorname{GL}_n(D)$ is, near the identity, an integral combination of parabolically induced trivial representations, making the growth of Moy--Prasad invariants polynomial and identifying its Gelfand--Kirillov dimension. Key computation: for $\\operatorname{char}C\\ne p$, $\\pi\\sim\\sum_{\\lambda\\vdash n}c_\\lambda(\\pi)\\operatorname{ind}_{P_\\lambda}^{G}\\mathbf1$ on a sufficiently small open subgroup; nilpotent orbits correspond to partitions with $\\dim_F\\mathcal O_\\lambda=2d^2\\sum_{i<j}\\lambda_i\\lambda_j$, and hence $\\dim_CV^{G_{x,r+i/d}}=Q_{\\pi,x,r}(p^i)$ where $Q_{\\pi,x,r}(X)=\\sum_{\\lambda\\vdash n}c_\\lambda(\\pi)|P_\\lambda\\backslash G/G_{x,r}|X^{fd\\,d(\\lambda)}$, yielding $\\dim_{\\mathrm{GK}}\\pi=fd\\max\\{d(\\lambda):c_\\lambda(\\pi)\\ne0\\}$."
  2220,Zhizhang Xie (joint work with Jinmin Wang and Guoliang Yu),"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/73/32/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.DG,math.DG math.AC math.AG math.FA mathematics index-theory commutative-algebra functional-analysis schemes talk,null,null,null,"Zhizhang Xie (joint work with Jinmin Wang and Guoliang Yu), a Fredholm index theorem for a twisted Dirac operator on a polyhedron proves Gromov's flat-corner domination theorem and hence Stoker's conjecture in every dimension. Key computation: for the boundary condition induced by $(\\omega\\otimes\\omega)(c(e_n)\\otimes c(e_n))$ and $0<\\theta_{ij}(g)\\le\\theta_{ij}(g_0)<\\pi$, the operator $D_B$ is essentially self-adjoint Fredholm with $\\operatorname{Ind}(D_B)=\\chi(P)=1$; locally the link operator has spectrum $\\{-\\tfrac12+k\\pi/\\alpha:k\\in\\mathbb Z\\}$ and $|P_B|\\ge\\tfrac12$, while the integrated estimate $0=\\int_P|D\\phi|^2\\ge\\int_P|\\nabla\\phi|^2+\\int_P\\tfrac{\\operatorname{Sc}_g}{4}|\\phi|^2+\\sum_i\\int_{F_i}\\tfrac{H_g}{2}|\\phi|^2+\\tfrac12\\sum_{i,j}\\int_{F_i\\cap F_j}(\\theta_{ij}(g_0)-\\theta_{ij}(g))|\\phi|^2$ forces flatness and equality of face and dihedral data."
  2221,Damien Tageddine,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/73/41/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.AG,math.AG math.DG math.CO math.FA mathematics schemes differential-geometry discrete-geometry functional-analysis,null,null,null,"Damien Tageddine, matrix spectral triples attached to finer triangulations converge to the classical Dirac and Laplace operators on a compact Riemannian manifold. Key computation: the inductive-limit algebra satisfies $\\varprojlim\\operatorname{Spec}(A_i)\\simeq\\operatorname{Spec}(\\varinjlim A_i)$ and $Z(A_\\infty)\\simeq C(M)$, while for the lattice Dirac operator $D_n=\\sum_{k=1}^d1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes D_n^{(k)}\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes1$ one has $\\sigma_{A_\\infty}([D_\\infty,a])=\\bigcup_i\\sigma_{A_i}([D_i,a_i])$ and $\\|[D_\\infty,a]\\|=\\|d_ca\\|_\\infty$; with sampled points and $\\hbar_n=n^{-\\alpha}$, the explicit averages $\\widehat S_{n\\hbar_n}(a)=n^{-1}\\sum_{k=1}^n e_k[D_{X_k},a_k]e_k^*$ and $\\Omega_{\\hbar_n}^n(a)$ converge in probability to $[D,a]$ and $\\Delta_Ma$, respectively."
  2222,Judith Packer (joint work with Carla Farsi,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/73/44/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.OA,math.OA math-ph math.QA mathematics physics noncommutative-geometry talk,null,null,null,"Judith Packer (joint work with Carla Farsi, Therese-Marie Landry, Nadia Larsen, and Fr\\'ed\\'eric Latr\\'emoli\\`ere), spectral triples on noncommutative solenoids arise as inductive limits of rotation-algebra triples and converge in spectral propinquity. Key computation: for $\\Gamma=\\mathbb Z[1/p]^2$, the proper bounded-doubling length $L_p(r_1,r_2)=\\max\\{\\|(r_1,r_2)\\|_2,p^{\\operatorname{scale}(r_1,r_2)}\\}$ defines the metric triple $(C^*(\\Gamma,\\sigma_\\theta),\\ell^2(\\Gamma),D_{L_p})$; moreover $A_\\theta\\cong\\varinjlim(A_{\\theta_{2n}},\\varphi_n)$ with $\\varphi_n(U_{2n})=U_{2n+2}^p$ and $\\varphi_n(V_{2n})=V_{2n+2}^p$, and for $D=M_{L_H}\\otimes\\gamma_1+M_F\\otimes\\gamma_2$ the finite-stage triples satisfy $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\Lambda^{\\mathrm{spec}}((C^*(\\Gamma_n,\\sigma),\\ell^2(\\Gamma_n)\\otimes\\mathbb C^2,D_n),(C^*(\\Gamma,\\sigma),\\ell^2(\\Gamma)\\otimes\\mathbb C^2,D))=0$."
  2223,Piotr M,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/73/45/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.CO,math.CO math.CT mathematics graph-theory category-theory combinatorics operator-algebras talk,null,null,null,"Piotr M. Hajac (joint work with Mariusz Tobolski), suitable graph and path homomorphisms make Leavitt path algebras contravariant and graph $C^*$-algebras covariant, yielding pushout-to-pullback theorems for quantum spaces. Key computation: for a proper target-bijective graph map $f:E\\to F$, the induced map $\\bar f^*:L_k(F)\\to L_k(E)$ is explicitly $[\\chi_p]\\mapsto\\sum_{q\\in\\bar f^{-1}(p)}[\\chi_q]$, defining a contravariant functor; dually, for a path homomorphism satisfying the three regular-monomorphism conditions, $f_*:C^*(E)\\to C^*(F)$ is $[\\chi_p]\\mapsto[\\chi_{\\bar f(p)}]$, defining a covariant $*$-functor and specializing to the stated pullback constructions for graph models of the equatorial Podle\\'s quantum sphere and quantum $\\mathbb{CP}^1$."
  2224,Alfons Van Daele,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/80/13/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.OA,math.OA math.QA math.RT mathematics operator-algebras quantum-groups,null,null,null,"Alfons Van Daele, a locally compact quantum group defined by central Haar weights on a $C^*$-algebra canonically yields a faithful von Neumann algebraic locally compact quantum group. Key computation: a lower-semicontinuous semifinite weight extends by $\\widetilde\\varphi(x)=\\sup_{\\omega\\le\\varphi}\\omega(x)$ to a normal weight on $A^{**}$, and Haar invariance $\\varphi((\\omega\\otimes\\iota)\\Delta(a))=\\omega(1)\\varphi(a)$ implies that the support projections $e$ and $f$ of the extended left and right weights satisfy $\\widetilde\\Delta(1-e)(1\\otimes(1-e))=0$, hence $f(1-e)=e(1-f)=0$ and $e=f$; cutting down to $M=A^{**}e$ gives $\\Delta_0(x)=\\widetilde\\Delta(x)(e\\otimes e)$ with faithful invariant restrictions $\\varphi_0,\\psi_0$, so $(M,\\Delta_0)$ is the associated von Neumann algebraic quantum group."
  2225,Stevo Todorcevic,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/69/88/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.CO,math.CO mathematics graph-theory extremal-combinatorics probabilistic-combinatorics,null,null,null,"Stevo Todorcevic, the Ne\\v{s}et\\v{r}il--R\\'odl ordered expansion canonizes finite-arity relations on the random graph and identifies the universal minimal flow of its automorphism group. Key computation: for a finite graph $A$ on $\\{0,\\ldots,k-1\\}$, $C_A=\\operatorname{Emb}(A,R)=\\{\\bar x\\in\\mathbb N^{(k)}:(i,j)\\in E_A\\Longleftrightarrow(x_i,x_j)\\in R\\}$ splits under a homogeneous order $<^*$ into $C_A^\\sigma=\\{\\bar x\\in C_A:x_{\\sigma(0)}<^*\\cdots<^*x_{\\sigma(k-1)}\\}$; every $S\\subseteq\\mathbb N^{(k)}$ is constant on each $C_A^\\sigma$ after restriction to an arbitrarily large finite induced subgraph, all $k!$ classes remain essential, and this Ramsey-plus-ordering computation makes the natural action $\\operatorname{Aut}(R)\\curvearrowright\\operatorname{LO}(\\mathbb N)$ minimal and universal."
  2226,Morris Ang,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/2/41/70/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.DS,math.DS math.PR cond-mat.stat-mech math-ph mathematics physics ode-and-dynamical-systems statistical-mechanics,null,null,null,"Morris Ang, Gaussian multiplicative chaos constructs Liouville quantum gravity surfaces and the DOZZ three-point function computes the universal critical-percolation three-point connectivity constant. Key computation: for $0<\\gamma<2$, $A_h^\\gamma(dz)=\\lim_{\\varepsilon\\to0}\\varepsilon^{\\gamma^2/2}e^{\\gamma h_\\varepsilon(z)}dz$ obeys $A_{h+f}^\\gamma=e^{\\gamma f}A_h^\\gamma$ and $f_*A_h^\\gamma=A_{\\widetilde h}^\\gamma$ under $h=\\widetilde h\\circ f+(2/\\gamma+\\gamma/2)\\log|f'|$; the resulting DOZZ correlation proves the Delfino--Viti formula $P_3(z_1,z_2,z_3)/\\sqrt{P_2(z_1,z_2)P_2(z_2,z_3)P_2(z_3,z_1)}=\\bigl[C_{\\sqrt{8/3}}^{\\mathrm{DOZZ}}(7\\sqrt6/12,7\\sqrt6/12,7\\sqrt6/12)\\bigr]^{-1}\\times(\\text{explicit Gamma factors})\\approx1.022$."
  2227,Liat Kessler (joint work with Pranav Chakravarthy,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/2/49/31/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.CO,math.CO math.SG math.CV math.AG mathematics graph-theory symplectic-geometry complex-geometry talk,null,null,null,"Liat Kessler (joint work with Pranav Chakravarthy, River Chiang, and Martin Pinsonnault), equivariant symplectic blowups of a Hamiltonian circle four-manifold at a fixed component are connected from deformation to isotopy using decorated-graph classification and inflation. Key computation: an embedded $J$-holomorphic surface $Z$ produces $J$-taming forms in the classes $[\\omega_t]=[\\omega]+t\\lambda\\operatorname{PD}[Z]$ for $0\\le\\lambda<T$, where $T=\\infty$ if $Z\\cdot Z\\ge0$ and $T=\\omega(Z)/(-Z\\cdot Z)$ otherwise; applying this family inflation to two $S^1$-equivariant blowups of equal size, whose Karshon decorated graphs coincide, changes the invariant deformation to a cohomologous path, and equivariant Moser then writes $\\omega_t=\\phi_t^*\\omega_0$, giving equivariant isotopy."
  2228,"Zhi L\\'u (joint work with Wei Wang)","https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/23/15/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.AT,math.AT math.GT math.SG mathematics cobordism equivariant-topology symplectic-geometry talk,null,null,null,"Zhi L\\'u (joint work with Wei Wang), equivariant cohomology Chern numbers form a complete system of invariants for unitary $T^k$-bordism, and mixed equivariant characteristic numbers separate equivariant Hamiltonian bordism. Key computation: the universal toric genus $\\Phi:\\Omega_*^{U,T^k}\\to MU^*(BT^k)$ is tested by the Kronecker pairing $\\langle\\Phi([M]_{T^k}),[f:N\\to BT^k]\\rangle=[\\widetilde f^*(ET^k\\times_{T^k}M)]\\in MU_*=\\Omega_*^U$; the stable tangent splitting $T\\widetilde f^*(ET^k\\times_{T^k}M)\\cong\\pi'^*TN\\oplus\\widetilde f^*(ET^k\\times_{T^k}TM)$ expresses every Chern number through equivariant Chern numbers of $M$, so their vanishing kills every pairing, gives $\\Phi([M]_{T^k})=0$, and injectivity of $\\Phi$ yields $[M]_{T^k}=0$."
  2229,Stevo Todorcevic,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/69/90/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.RT,math.RT math.AG math.RA mathematics quiver-representations enumerative-geometry,null,null,null,"Stevo Todorcevic, analytic $n^*$-gaps have a finite basis of minimal equivalence types, obtained by canonizing their tree presentations. Key computation: for gaps $\\Gamma$ on $N$ and $\\Delta$ on $M$, $\\Gamma\\le\\Delta$ is witnessed by an injection $\\phi:N\\to M$ carrying $\\Gamma_i$ into $\\Delta_i$ and $\\Gamma_i^\\perp$ into $\\Delta_i^\\perp$; the tree partition theorem produces, from any non-separated analytic family $\\Gamma=\\{\\Gamma_i:i<n\\}$, a permutation $\\varepsilon:n\\to n$ and injection $u:n^{<\\omega}\\to N$ with $u[X]\\in\\Gamma_{\\varepsilon(i)}$ for every chain $X$ of type $[i]$, reducing every analytic gap to one of finitely many minimal types--exactly five for $n=2$ up to permutation--and yielding the sharp intersection numbers $J(1),\\ldots,J(7)=1,8,61,480,3881,31976,266981$."
  2230,Jeff Achter,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/34/25/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.AG,math.AG mathematics algebraic-geometry moduli-spaces hodge-theory shimura-varieties,null,null,null,"Jeff Achter, cyclic covers turn moduli of genus-$4$ curves, plane quartics, and five marked points on $\\mathbb P^1$ into arithmetic period maps to unitary Shimura varieties. Key computation: for a canonical genus-$4$ curve $C=Q\\cap S\\subset\\mathbb P^3$, the triple cover $v:Z\\to Q$ branched along $C$ is a K3 surface; the inverse images $N_i=v^{-1}(M_i)$ of the two rulings satisfy $(N_1,N_2)=3$, so $U(3)=\\mathbb ZN_1+\\mathbb ZN_2\\hookrightarrow\\operatorname{Pic}(Z)$ and the deck $\\mu_3$-action produces $(Z,\\alpha,\\rho)\\in\\mathcal R_{(L_4,\\chi_4)}$. Over $\\mathbb Z[\\zeta_3,1/6]$, $\\kappa_4:\\mathcal R_{(L_4,\\chi_4)}\\to\\mathcal N_4$ induces an isomorphism on coarse moduli spaces and $\\tau_4$ is an open immersion into $\\operatorname{Sh}(L_4,\\chi_4)$."
  2231,Yousheng Shi,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/41/43/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.AG,math.AG mathematics birational-geometry geometric-invariant-theory singularities,null,null,null,"Yousheng Shi, derived intersections of Kudla--Rapoport divisors on ramified unitary Rapoport--Zink spaces equal a corrected derivative of local representation densities in ranks $n\\le3$. Key computation: blowing up the isolated singularities of $\\mathcal N_n^{\\mathrm{Pap}}$ gives the regular semistable Kr\\'amer model $\\mathcal N_n^{\\mathrm{Kra}}$, and for $L=\\langle x_1,\\ldots,x_n\\rangle$ the intersection is $\\operatorname{Int}(L)=\\chi(\\mathcal N_n,\\mathcal O_{\\mathcal Z(x_1)}\\otimes^{\\mathbf L}\\cdots\\otimes^{\\mathbf L}\\mathcal O_{\\mathcal Z(x_n)})$. Solving the triangular system $2\\alpha'(M_j,M)/\\alpha(M,M)+\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}c_i\\alpha(M_j,M_i)=0$ produces the correction terms and the formula $\\operatorname{Int}(L)=2\\alpha'(L,M)/\\alpha(M,M)+\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}c_i\\alpha(L,M_i)$; for $n=3$, the difference-cycle identity $\\mathbb L D^{\\mathrm{Kra}}(L)\\cdot\\mathcal Z^{\\mathrm{Kra}}(x)=\\beta(L\\oplus\\{x\\},M)(2)$ reduces the proof inductively to $n=2$."
  2232,Somnath Jha,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/30/69/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.NT,math.NT math.CT math.KT math.AG mathematics elliptic-curves homological-algebra k-theory l-functions,null,null,null,"Somnath Jha, Pontryagin dual Selmer groups over noncommutative $p$-adic Lie extensions satisfy an algebraic functional equation, including the local Euler-factor error term. Key computation: twisting by a continuous character $\\theta:\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{cyc}}\\to\\mathbb Z_p^\\times$ makes the control maps $r_{U,A}^{\\theta}$ have finite kernels and cokernels; under the stated $\\mathfrak M_H(G)$ and Ext-vanishing hypotheses this yields $[S(A/K_\\infty)^\\vee]+[E_0^{A^*(1)}]=[S(A^*(1)/K_\\infty)^\\vee]$ in $K_0(\\mathfrak M_H(G))$. For the false-Tate extension and a squarefree-level ordinary newform of weight $k$ at the central twist, the correction is $[E_0^A]=[\\bigoplus_{q\\in P_1\\cup P_2}\\operatorname{Ind}_{G_q}^G T(-1)]$ and evaluates at an Artin representation $\\eta$ as $\\eta(\\xi_{E_0^A})=\\prod_{q\\in P_1\\cup P_2}P_q(f,\\eta,q^{-k/2})/P_q(f,\\eta^*,q^{-k/2})$ modulo $p$-adic units."
  2233,Mahesh Kakde (joint with Samit Dasgupta),"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/26/64/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.NT,math.NT mathematics algebraic-number-theory number-theory talk,null,null,null,"Mahesh Kakde (joint with Samit Dasgupta), the integral Gross--Stark theorem proves the strong Brumer conjecture and gives a $p$-adic class-field construction from Brumer--Stark units. Key computation: the Stickelberger element satisfies $\\chi(\\Theta_q^{H/F})=L_R(\\chi^{-1},0)(1-\\chi^{-1}(q)Nq)$ and $\\Theta_q^{H/F}\\in\\operatorname{Fitt}_{\\mathbb Z[G]}(\\operatorname{Cl}_q(H)^{\\vee,-})$; hence for a split prime $\\mathfrak p$ its class is principalized by the unique unit $u$ with $\\sum_{\\sigma\\in G}[\\sigma^{-1}]\\operatorname{ord}_{\\mathfrak P}(\\sigma u)=\\Theta_q^{H/F}$. For $S=\\{\\mathfrak p_i:\\chi(\\mathfrak p_i)=1\\}$, the proved Gross--Stark formula computes the leading term $L_p^{(r)}(\\chi\\omega,0)/(r!L(\\chi,0)\\prod_{\\chi(\\mathfrak p)\\ne1}(1-\\chi(\\mathfrak p)))=\\det(\\log_pN_{F_{\\mathfrak p_i}/\\mathbb Q_p}u_j)/\\det(\\operatorname{ord}_{\\mathfrak P_i}u_j)$, while in rank one $\\chi(\\Theta_q^{H_{\\mathrm{cyc}}/F})=\\sum_{\\sigma\\in G}\\chi(\\sigma^{-1})(\\operatorname{rec}(\\sigma u)-1)$ in $I/I^2$."
  2234,Tiago J,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/51/38/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.AG,math.AG math.NT math.AT mathematics chromatic-homotopy-theory elliptic-curves motives schemes talk,null,null,null,"Tiago J. Fonseca (joint with Nils Matthes), the universal vector extension gives an algebraic classification of all relatively unipotent integrable connections on a punctured elliptic curve and recovers Brown--Levin elliptic multiple polylogarithms. Key computation: for $0\\to V(R^1f_*\\mathcal O_E)\\to E^\\natural\\xrightarrow{\\pi}E\\to0$ and $D=\\pi^{-1}(O)$, Laumon's vanishing $g_*\\mathcal O_{E^\\natural}\\simeq\\mathcal O_S$, $R^ng_*\\mathcal O_{E^\\natural}=0$ combines with $g_*\\Omega^1_{E^\\natural/S}(\\log D)=g_*\\Omega^1_{E^\\natural/S}\\oplus\\bigoplus_{n\\ge1}K(n)$, where $d\\omega^{(n)}=\\nu\\wedge\\omega^{(n-1)}$ and $\\operatorname{Res}_D\\omega^{(n)}=t^{n-1}/(n-1)!$. Every such connection is therefore canonically $d+A\\otimes\\nu+B_0\\otimes\\omega^{(0)}+\\sum_{n\\ge1}\\operatorname{ad}_A^n(B_0)\\otimes\\omega^{(n)}$, and $\\pi_1^{\\mathrm{dR}}(E\\setminus\\{O\\},x)\\simeq\\operatorname{Spec}(\\bigoplus_{n\\ge0}H^1_{\\mathrm{dR}}(E/k)^{\\otimes n})$."
  2235,Arul Shankar (joint with Manjul Bhargava and Jonathan Hanke),"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/33/67/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.NT,math.NT mathematics arithmetic-statistics algebraic-number-theory quadratic-forms talk,null,null,null,"Arul Shankar (joint with Manjul Bhargava and Jonathan Hanke), local ramification densities determine the average $2$-torsion in class groups of fixed-index monogenic cubic fields. Key computation: pairs of ternary quadratic forms $V=\\mathbb Z^2\\otimes\\operatorname{Sym}^2(\\mathbb Z^3)$ have resolvent $\\operatorname{Res}(A,B)=4\\det(Ax+B)$, and Bhargava's bijection identifies $\\operatorname{SL}_3(\\mathbb Z)$-orbits in $\\operatorname{Res}^{-1}(f)$ with index-$2$ subgroups of $\\operatorname{Cl}(K)$ when $f$ defines $\\mathcal O_K$. Fixing leading coefficient $n$ imposes $\\det A=n/4$; counting the resulting $\\operatorname{SO}_A(\\mathbb Z)$-orbits and evaluating $\\operatorname{Mass}_p(f)=\\sum_{(A,B)}|\\operatorname{Stab}(A,B)|^{-1}=1$ gives, for $n=m^2k$ and $\\rho(\\mathcal F)=\\prod_{p\\mid k}\\rho_p(\\mathcal F)$, the exact averages $\\operatorname{Avg}_{\\mathcal F,\\mathrm{real}}|\\operatorname{Cl}_2(K)|=5/4+\\rho(\\mathcal F)/4$ and $\\operatorname{Avg}_{\\mathcal F,\\mathrm{complex}}|\\operatorname{Cl}_2(K)|=3/2+\\rho(\\mathcal F)/2$."
  2236,Taras Panov,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/26/06/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.CO,math.CO math.GR math.AT math.GT mathematics algebraic-combinatorics algebraic-topology geometric-group-theory graph-theory,null,null,null,"Taras Panov, polyhedral products classify when graph-product classifying spaces are aspherical and when commutator subgroups of right-angled Coxeter groups are free or one-relator. Key computation: the fibration $(EG,G)_K\\to(BG)_K\\to\\prod_{i=1}^mBG_i$ gives $1\\to\\pi_1((EG,G)_K)\\to G_K\\to\\prod_iG_i\\to1$; a missing face $J$ of size $k\\ge3$ has fat-wedge fibre $\\Sigma^{k-1}G_{j_1}\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge G_{j_k}$ and hence nonzero $\\pi_{k-1}$, proving asphericity exactly when $K$ is flag. For $G_i=\\mathbb Z/2$, $\\pi_1((D^1,S^0)_K)=RC_K'$; it is free exactly when $K^1$ is chordal, and it is one-relator exactly when $K=C_p$ or $C_p*\\Delta^q$, equivalently $H_2((D^1,S^0)_K)=\\mathbb Z$; for $K=C_m$ the space is the orientable surface of genus $(m-4)2^{m-3}+1$."
  2237,Nima Hoda,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/26/02/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.CO,math.CO math.AC math.AT mathematics graph-theory algebraic-topology commutative-algebra extremal-combinatorics,null,null,null,"Nima Hoda, bisimplices give a canonical higher-dimensional completion of complete bipartite subgraphs, and filling every $K_{m+1,n+1}$ in a locally finite quadric complex produces a contractible complex with unchanged $2$-skeleton. Key computation: the $(m,n)$-bisimplex $\\nabla\\!\\Delta^{m,n}$ is built inductively by placing one $\\nabla\\!\\Delta^{m',n'}$ on each proper $K_{m'+1,n'+1}$ and then attaching an $(m+n)$-cell to $\\partial\\nabla\\!\\Delta^{m,n}$. An explicit discrete Morse matching on this boundary has exactly two critical cells, so $\\partial\\nabla\\!\\Delta^{m,n}\\simeq S^{m+n-1}$; the link of every cell has the same two-critical-cell matching, hence induction makes every link a sphere, and the generalized Poincar\\'e theorem upgrades the homotopy equivalence to $\\partial\\nabla\\!\\Delta^{m,n}\\cong S^{m+n-1}$, proving $\\nabla\\!\\Delta^{m,n}\\cong B^{m+n}$."
  2238,Victor Buchstaber,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/24/62/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.AT,math.AT math.GT mathematics cobordism chromatic-homotopy-theory,null,null,null,"Victor Buchstaber, partially framed manifolds give explicit generators and characteristic numbers for complex bordism and compute the complex cobordism of $\\Omega SU(2)\\simeq\\Omega S^3$. Key computation: the hypersurface $M_{2n}\\subset(\\mathbb{CP}^{1}_{*})^{n+1}$ Poincar\\'e dual to $\\eta_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes\\eta_{n+1}$ generates $(\\mathrm{fr}(1),U)_{2n}\\cong\\mathbb Z$ and has $\\operatorname{Td}(M_{2n})=(-1)^n$, $c_n(M_{2n})=(-1)^n(n+1)!$, and $\\tau(M_{2n})=4^{n+1}(4^{n+1}-1)B_{2n+2}/(2n+2)$. The James equivalence $J(S^2)\\simeq\\Omega S^3$ then yields $U_*(\\Omega S^3)\\cong\\Omega_U[w]$ and $U^*(\\Omega S^3)\\cong\\Omega_U[[b_2,b_4,\\ldots]]/(b_2^n-n!b_{2n})_{n\\ge2}$, with $b_{2i}b_{2j}=\\binom{i+j}{i}b_{2(i+j)}$ and $\\varphi_U^*u=b_2+\\sum_{n\\ge1}[M_{2n}]b_{2n+2}$."
  2239,Luis Scoccola,"https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/26/87/slides.pdf",utoronto.ca,math.AT,math.AT math.CT math.PR mathematics algebraic-topology category-theory probability slides,null,null,null,"Luis Scoccola, the Gromov--Hausdorff distance is universally characterized as a quotient interleaving distance, making homotopy and density filtrations stable functors of metric data. Key computation: for pseudometric spaces, let $d_I(P,Q)$ be the infimum of $\\delta$ admitting inverse set maps that increase distances by at most $\\delta$, and quotient by zigzags of surjective distance-preserving maps; the slides prove $(d_I)/{\\simeq}=2d_{\\mathrm{GH}}$. Consequently the Vietoris--Rips functor is $2$-Lipschitz into homotopy interleaving distance, while the bifiltration $\\operatorname{KDF}(X)(s,k)=\\{x\\in X:\\int_XK(d_X(x,x')/s)\\,d\\mu_X(x')\\ge k\\}$ is uniformly continuous from Gromov--Hausdorff--Prokhorov distance to Gromov--Hausdorff interleaving distance; for full-support $X$ and iid samples $X_n$, $\\operatorname{KDF}(X_n)\\to\\operatorname{KDF}(X)$ in probability."
