  2932,"Mathematics for Cryptography II: Security Analysis, Polynomials, Number Theory: Part 2","https://github.com/BlockstreamResearch/zk-book/blob/main/presentations/2-math-and-crypto-animated.pdf",github.com,cs.CR,cs.CR math.NT computer-science mathematics security-and-privacy number-theory harmonic-analysis distributed-systems lecture-notes slides github,null,2024,null,"Mathematics for Cryptography II: Security Analysis, Polynomials, Number Theory: Part 2. Beamer slides, 2024; 104 pages. From a repository described as: This is a series of lectures on zero-knowledge conducted internally at Blockstream and Distributed Lab, in which we covered essentials for Research and Development in the field of zero-knowledge proofs and their applications. Sections: Quick Recap; Security Analysis; Polynomials; Basic Number Theory. Subsections include Cipher Definition, Bit Guessing Game, Neglibility, Other Examples, Definition, Roots and Divisibility, Interpolation, and Interpolation Applications: Shamir Secret Sharing. Slides cover What will we learn today?, Introducing the Cipher: a bit of Formalism, Introducing the Cipher, Defining Security, Semantic Security: Bit Guessing Game, Advantage, Neglibility, Neglible Functions, Neglible Functions Examples, and Discrete Logarithm Problem (DL)."
