  3012,Lecture 03 - COMPSCI 203 — Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science (compsci-203/2024-01),"https://gitlab.com/compsci-203/2024-01/-/blob/main/pdf/lecture-03.pdf",gitlab.com,math.LO,math.LO math.PR mathematics mathematical-logic random-matrix-theory lecture-notes slides problem-list gitlab,Xing Shi Cai,2024,null,"Lecture 03 - COMPSCI 203 — Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science (compsci-203/2024-01). Beamer lecture slides by Xing Shi Cai, 2024; 46 pages. Part of COMPSCI203 -- Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science (2024-01): This repository contains materials which I (Xing Shi Cai) prepared for the course COMPSCI203 (Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science) of Duke Kunshan University which started in January 2024. Sections: dm 3.1 Propositional Logic. Subsections include Truth Tables, Xor — Exclusive-or, Logical Equivalence, Deductions — How to argue with logic, and Beyond Propositions. Slides cover Required Reading and Exercises, Truth Tables, A Propositional about Monopoly, The Monopoly Problem, We define one more logic connective exclusive-or, denote by, Solving the Puzzle with a Truth Table, Logical Equivalence, Basic Logic Equivalence, Negation of an Implication, and Valid and invalid arguments."
  3013,Lecture 04 - COMPSCI 203 — Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science (compsci-203/2024-01),"https://gitlab.com/compsci-203/2024-01/-/blob/main/pdf/lecture-04.pdf",gitlab.com,math.CO,math.CO math.NT mathematics combinatorics number-theory lecture-notes slides problem-list gitlab,Xing Shi Cai,2024,null,"Lecture 04 - COMPSCI 203 — Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science (compsci-203/2024-01). Beamer lecture slides by Xing Shi Cai, 2024; 59 pages. Part of COMPSCI203 -- Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science (2024-01): This repository contains materials which I (Xing Shi Cai) prepared for the course COMPSCI203 (Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science) of Duke Kunshan University which started in January 2024. Sections: dm 3.2 Proofs. Subsections include What Is a Mathematical Proof?, Direct Proof, Proof by Contrapositive, Proof by Contradiction, Proof by (Counter) Example, Proof by Cases, and Proof of If and Only if. Slides cover Required Reading and Exercises, What Is a Mathematical Proof?, A classic theorem, Direct Proof, Proof by Contrapositive, Prove by contrapositive that for every prime number p, if p≠2,, A rational number is a real number that can be expressed as, Proof by Contradiction and by Contrapositive, Pigeonholes, and An experiment."
  3014,Lecture 06 — Test of Convergence - COMPSCI 203 — Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science (compsci-203/2024-01),"https://gitlab.com/compsci-203/2024-01/-/blob/main/pdf/lecture-06.pdf",gitlab.com,math.CA,math.CA mathematics real-analysis lecture-notes slides problem-list gitlab,Xing Shi Cai,2024,null,"Lecture 06 — Test of Convergence - COMPSCI 203 — Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science (compsci-203/2024-01). Beamer lecture slides by Xing Shi Cai, 2024; 46 pages. Part of COMPSCI203 -- Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science (2024-01): This repository contains materials which I (Xing Shi Cai) prepared for the course COMPSCI203 (Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science) of Duke Kunshan University which started in January 2024. Sections: ec 9.2 Test of Convergence. Subsections include Sequences Differ in Finitely Many Places, Monotone Bounded Test, Comparison Test, n-th Term Test, and Ratio Test. Slides cover Required Reading and Exercises, Make a Guess, Impact of Finite Changes on Limits, Bounded Sequence, Exercise: Bounded Sequence, Monotone Sequence, Exercise: Monotone Sequence, Monotone Bounded Test (Compact Form), Comparison Test, and Example 9.8."
  3015,Lecture 07 — Alternating Series - COMPSCI 203 — Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science (compsci-203/2024-01),"https://gitlab.com/compsci-203/2024-01/-/blob/main/pdf/lecture-07.pdf",gitlab.com,math.CA,math.CA mathematics real-analysis lecture-notes slides problem-list gitlab,Xing Shi Cai,2024,null,"Lecture 07 — Alternating Series - COMPSCI 203 — Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science (compsci-203/2024-01). Beamer lecture slides by Xing Shi Cai, 2024; 48 pages. Part of COMPSCI203 -- Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science (2024-01): This repository contains materials which I (Xing Shi Cai) prepared for the course COMPSCI203 (Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science) of Duke Kunshan University which started in January 2024. Sections: ec 9.2 Test of Convergence (Continued); ec 9.3 Alternating Series. Subsections include Integral Test, p-series Test, Comparison Test for Series, Limit Comparison Test, Telescope Series Test, Properties of Convergent Series, Alternative Series Test, and Absolute and Conditional Convergence. Slides cover Required Reading and Exercises, Integral Test, Example 9.10, Comparison Test for Series, Limit Comparison Test, Example 9.12, Example 9.13, A numeric experiment, Alternating Series Test, and A Paradox?."
  3016,Lecture 08 — Power Series and Taylor Series - COMPSCI 203 — Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science (compsci-203/2024-01),"https://gitlab.com/compsci-203/2024-01/-/blob/main/pdf/lecture-08.pdf",gitlab.com,math.CA,math.CA mathematics real-analysis lecture-notes slides problem-list gitlab,Xing Shi Cai,2024,null,"Lecture 08 — Power Series and Taylor Series - COMPSCI 203 — Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science (compsci-203/2024-01). Beamer lecture slides by Xing Shi Cai, 2024; 40 pages. Part of COMPSCI203 -- Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science (2024-01): This repository contains materials which I (Xing Shi Cai) prepared for the course COMPSCI203 (Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science) of Duke Kunshan University which started in January 2024. Sections: ec 9.4 Power Series; ec 9.5 Taylor Series. Subsections include Interval of Convergence, Differentiation and Integration, Taylor's Formula, and Approximate a Function. Slides cover Required Reading and Exercises, Power Series, Interval of Convergence, How to Find Interval of Convergence, Example 9.17, Exercise, Differentiation and Integration, Example 9.19, An Experiment, and Taylor’s Formula."
  3017,"Lecture 09— Strings, Sets, and Binomial Coefficients (1) - COMPSCI 203 — Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science (compsci-203/2024-01)","https://gitlab.com/compsci-203/2024-01/-/blob/main/pdf/lecture-09.pdf",gitlab.com,math.CO,math.CO cs.AR math.NT math.OC mathematics computer-science combinatorics computer-architecture graph-theory number-theory lecture-notes survey slides problem-list gitlab,Xing Shi Cai,2024,null,"Lecture 09— Strings, Sets, and Binomial Coefficients (1) - COMPSCI 203 — Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science (compsci-203/2024-01). Beamer lecture slides by Xing Shi Cai, 2024; 59 pages. Part of COMPSCI203 -- Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science (2024-01): This repository contains materials which I (Xing Shi Cai) prepared for the course COMPSCI203 (Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science) of Duke Kunshan University which started in January 2024. Sections: ac 1 An Introduction to Combinatorics; ac 2 Strings, Sets, and Binomial Coefficients. Subsections include 1.1 Enumeration, 1.2 Graph Theory, 1.4 Number Theory, 1.5 Geometry, 1.6 Optimization, 2.1 Strings, 2.2 Permutations, and 2.3 Combinations. Slides cover Required Reading and Exercises, What Is Combinatorics, Strings, Examples of Strings, Strings and CPU, Example: My lunch, The number of passwords — solution, and Factorials."
  3018,"Lecture 10— Strings, Sets, and Binomial Coefficients (2) - COMPSCI 203 — Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science (compsci-203/2024-01)","https://gitlab.com/compsci-203/2024-01/-/blob/main/pdf/lecture-10.pdf",gitlab.com,math.CO,math.CO mathematics combinatorics lecture-notes slides problem-list gitlab,Xing Shi Cai,2024,null,"Lecture 10— Strings, Sets, and Binomial Coefficients (2) - COMPSCI 203 — Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science (compsci-203/2024-01). Beamer lecture slides by Xing Shi Cai, 2024; 41 pages. Part of COMPSCI203 -- Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science (2024-01): This repository contains materials which I (Xing Shi Cai) prepared for the course COMPSCI203 (Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science) of Duke Kunshan University which started in January 2024. Sections: ac 2.4 Combinatorial Proofs; ac 2.5 The Ubiquitous Nature of Binomial Coefficients. Subsections include Summation formulas, Combinatorial Identities, and Integer Composition. Slides cover Required Reading and Exercises, Sum of the First nPositive Integers, Combinatorial Identities, Example 2.18, Example 2.19, Exercise, Exercise: Combinatorial Identities, Challenge: Combinatorial Identities, Integer composition, and The Argument."
  3019,Lecture 12 — Induction - COMPSCI 203 — Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science (compsci-203/2024-01),"https://gitlab.com/compsci-203/2024-01/-/blob/main/pdf/lecture-12.pdf",gitlab.com,math.CO,math.CO mathematics combinatorics lecture-notes slides problem-list gitlab,Xing Shi Cai,2024,null,"Lecture 12 — Induction - COMPSCI 203 — Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science (compsci-203/2024-01). Beamer lecture slides by Xing Shi Cai, 2024; 55 pages. Part of COMPSCI203 -- Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science (2024-01): This repository contains materials which I (Xing Shi Cai) prepared for the course COMPSCI203 (Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science) of Duke Kunshan University which started in January 2024. Sections: ac 3.4 Binomial Coefficients Revisited; ac 3.5 Solving Combinatorial Problems Recursively; ac 3.8 Proof by Induction. Subsections include A Game of, Tiling a Grid, Ternary Strings, What Is Induction, Example Proofs by Induction, More Interesting Example Proofs by Induction, and Strong Induction. Slides cover Required Reading and Exercises, Three Lines, An Experiment, Example 3.3: The Proof, A Recursive Formula, What is f0?, Example 3.6: The Recursive Formula, Proof by Induction, and Example Proofs by Induction."
  3020,Lecture 14 — Inclusion-Exclusion (2) - COMPSCI 203 — Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science (compsci-203/2024-01),"https://gitlab.com/compsci-203/2024-01/-/blob/main/pdf/lecture-14.pdf",gitlab.com,math.RT,math.RT math.CO math.RA mathematics quiver-representations combinatorics lecture-notes survey slides problem-list gitlab,Xing Shi Cai,2024,null,"Lecture 14 — Inclusion-Exclusion (2) - COMPSCI 203 — Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science (compsci-203/2024-01). Beamer lecture slides by Xing Shi Cai, 2024; 54 pages. Part of COMPSCI203 -- Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science (2024-01): This repository contains materials which I (Xing Shi Cai) prepared for the course COMPSCI203 (Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science) of Duke Kunshan University which started in January 2024. Sections: Review of Notations; ac 7.3 Enumerating Surjections; ac 7.4 Derangement. Slides cover Required Reading and Exercises, Example 7.5: Permutations, Review: Surjective (Onto) Functions, 5 Books and 3 Students, The Surjection Numbers, Exercise: 4 Books and 3 Students, Stirling Number of the Second Kind, Example: Stirling Number of the Second Kind, Derangements, and Properties of Permutations."
  3021,Lecture 15— Generating Function (1) - COMPSCI 203 — Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science (compsci-203/2024-01),"https://gitlab.com/compsci-203/2024-01/-/blob/main/pdf/lecture-15.pdf",gitlab.com,math.CO,math.CO mathematics combinatorics lecture-notes slides problem-list gitlab,Xing Shi Cai,2024,null,"Lecture 15— Generating Function (1) - COMPSCI 203 — Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science (compsci-203/2024-01). Beamer lecture slides by Xing Shi Cai, 2024; 43 pages. Part of COMPSCI203 -- Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science (2024-01): This repository contains materials which I (Xing Shi Cai) prepared for the course COMPSCI203 (Discrete Mathematics for Computer Science) of Duke Kunshan University which started in January 2024. Sections: ac 8.1 Basic Notion and Terminology; ac 8.2 Another look at distributing carrots. Subsections include Gfs are not functions, Algebra of gfs, and Example 8.4: Integer Composition. Slides cover Required Reading and Exercises, Flajolet and Analytic Combinatorics, What is a generating function (gf), The Advantage of Generating Functions, Algebra of gfs, Multiply gfs, Multiplicative Inverse of gf, The Transfer Principle/Uniqueness of Power Series, Example 8.2: Derivatives of Power Series, and Treating gfs As Functions."
