  5655,00 Intro,"https://github.com/carlocamilloni/Structural-Bioinformatics/blob/main/Notes/00_Intro.pdf",github.com,q-bio.GN,q-bio.GN q-bio.QM quantitative-biology bioinformatics lecture-notes slides github,null,2026,null,"00 Intro. Slides (PDF), 2026; 9 pages. Part of Structural Bioinformatics: Carlo Camilloni, Department of Biosciences, University of Milano, Italy. Slides cover Structural Bioinformatics, Aims and Background, Program (the topics are reorganized wrt to last year), Course Material, Time table: please note the change of venue, Exam, Practicals and course attendance, and Practicals."
  5656,01 StructuralBiology,"https://github.com/carlocamilloni/Structural-Bioinformatics/blob/main/Notes/01_StructuralBiology.pdf",github.com,q-bio.GN,q-bio.GN cs.DC q-bio.QM quantitative-biology computer-science bioinformatics distributed-systems extremal-combinatorics software-engineering slides github,null,2026,null,"01 StructuralBiology. Slides (PDF), 2026; 49 pages. Part of Structural Bioinformatics: Carlo Camilloni, Department of Biosciences, University of Milano, Italy. Slides cover Light microscope cannot look at molecular spatial scales, Molecular Motions Across Timescales: The Challenge of, As a consequence: observing the microscopic world is hard, X-ray Crystallography, What does X-ray crystallography tell us about molecules motion?, In this Cyclophilin A structure 22 aminoacids have alternative, X-rays in solution: small angle X-ray scattering (SAXS)318 S. Hansen, Example: effect of denaturant on the unfolded state of a protein, SAXS: testing the validity X-rays structures in solution, Cryo-EM can be used to identify molecules in multiple, So how do we “take pictures” of molecules? 3. Nuclear, NMR and X-ray structure are generally the same indicating that the, What does NMR tell us about molecules motion?, and Different graphical representations reveal different properties."
  5657,08 StructurePredictionDocking,"https://github.com/carlocamilloni/Structural-Bioinformatics/blob/main/Notes/08_StructurePredictionDocking.pdf",github.com,q-bio.GN,q-bio.GN q-bio.QM cs.DB quantitative-biology computer-science bioinformatics computational-biology databases algebraic-topology slides github,null,2026,null,"08 StructurePredictionDocking. Slides (PDF), 2026; 51 pages. Part of Structural Bioinformatics: Carlo Camilloni, Department of Biosciences, University of Milano, Italy. Slides cover Proteins are encoded in DNA, produced by ribosomes as linear, Anfinsen: The native structure of a protein is dictated by its sequence, Protein structure and function are intimately linked, The data: public, curated databases, Secondary structure prediction is relatively easy but secondary, Homology modelling, Coevolution also works for protein complexes, From COEVOLUTION to MACHINE LEARNING, Predicting distogram (histogram of distances) from coevolution is the, MIZ1: an example from my own experience, AlphaFold2: some ideas on the architecture, ALPHAFOLD PROTEIN STRUCTURE DATABASE, Interpreting an AF2 prediction: from COLABFOLD, and Molecular Docking: binding pose and affinity."
  5658,"公常路-光侨路行程车速调查: PRESENTATION SLIDE","https://github.com/changyicheng1234/OpenISE/blob/main/%E6%99%BA%E6%85%A7%E4%BA%A4%E9%80%9A%EF%BC%88%E4%BA%A4%E9%80%9A%E5%B7%A5%E7%A8%8B%EF%BC%89/%E5%A4%A7%E4%BA%8C%E4%B8%8A/%E4%BA%A4%E9%80%9A%E8%B0%83%E6%9F%A5%E4%B8%8E%E5%88%86%E6%9E%90/22%E7%BA%A7-%E8%94%A1%E6%80%9D%E8%BF%9C/%E5%A4%A7%E4%BD%9C%E4%B8%9A-%E8%BD%A6%E9%80%9F%E8%B0%83%E6%9F%A5%E5%B0%8F%E7%BB%84/%E4%BA%A4%E9%80%9A%E8%B0%83%E6%9F%A5%E8%A1%8C%E7%A8%8B%E8%BD%A6%E9%80%9F%E5%B0%8F%E7%BB%84%E7%AD%96%E5%88%92%E6%8A%A5%E5%91%8A.pdf",github.com,math.ST,math.ST stat.ME mathematics statistics lecture-notes slides github,null,2026,null,"公常路-光侨路行程车速调查: PRESENTATION SLIDE. Slides (PDF), 2026; 26 pages, in Chinese. From the course-materials archive changyicheng1234/OpenISE. Subject (translated): statistics and statistical inference. Slides cover 调查背景, 人员安排及调, PRESENTATION SLIDE, 调查目标和地点, 调查情况, 调查目的, 现场踩点, 调查地点, 行程车速:车辆行驶在道路某一区间的距离与行程时间的比值。, 确定断面位置(具体标识), 实际调查, and 数据处理."
  5659,"Mathematics of Data Science: Towards probabilities: set theory, Set operations","https://github.com/dodo47/Mathematics-of-Data-Science-2025/blob/main/1%20-%20Foundations%20of%20Probability%20Theory/Slides.pdf",github.com,math.PR,math.PR math.LO mathematics probability mathematical-logic slides github,Dominik Dold,2026,null,"Mathematics of Data Science: Towards probabilities: set theory, Set operations. Slides (PDF) by Dominik Dold, 2026; 42 pages. From a repository described as: This is the material of the Master's lecture Mathematics of Data Science which I taught in 2025/2026 at the University of Vienna. Sections: Towards probabilities: set theory; Set operations; Probability: Kolmogorov axioms; Some intuition behind the axioms; Exercise time; Example: rolling a die; Laplacian probability; Conditional probabilities; Conditional probabilities: visualization; Bayes theorem; Example: the Monty Hall problem (“Ziegenproblem”); Another example. Slides cover The world, and thus data, is inherently probabilistic, Sets, Colloquial interpretation of probability phrases, Sets are very intuitive: collection of elements (with certain property), Outcome space Ω = set of all possible outcomes, Pierre-Simon Laplace (1749-1827), Conditional probability: probability of event A under the condition that, A B C, A random variable X is a mapping from random events to numbers, and In case of discrete random variables."
  5660,"Mathematics of Data Science: Why did we look so much at statistics?, Motivation: making sense of (unlabeled) data","https://github.com/dodo47/Mathematics-of-Data-Science-2025/blob/main/2%20-%20Analysing%20High-Dimensional%20Data/Slides.pdf",github.com,math.ST,math.ST math.RA stat.ME math.NA mathematics statistics linear-algebra functional-analysis slides github,Dominik Dold,2026,null,"Mathematics of Data Science: Why did we look so much at statistics?, Motivation: making sense of (unlabeled) data. Slides (PDF) by Dominik Dold, 2026; 78 pages. From a repository described as: This is the material of the Master's lecture Mathematics of Data Science which I taught in 2025/2026 at the University of Vienna. Sections: Why did we look so much at statistics?; Motivation: making sense of (unlabeled) data; “Fun in high dimensions” quiz: question 1; “Fun in high dimensions” quiz: question 2; “Fun in high dimensions” quiz: last question; The blessing of dimensionality; The curse of dimensionality (actually called like this!); The manifold hypothesis; Examples; Towards dimensionality reduction: a refresher; Exercise time; To get Eigenvalues, we need. the determinant. Slides cover One reason: data can be interpreted as samples from an underlying distribution!, In this lecture block, we will approach the following three challenges, The curse and blessing of high dimensions, Unit sphere, Many operations do not scale well with high dimensions!, Example 1: MNIST handwritten digits, This is especially nice to see using the eigenbasis of matrix A, PC3 PC4, Marčenko-Pastur, and Vastly different data, but the principal components are identical!."
  5661,"Mathematics of Data Science: Motivation: assume the following type of data structure, Motivation","https://github.com/dodo47/Mathematics-of-Data-Science-2025/blob/main/3%20-%20Learning%20from%20Structured%20Data/Slides.pdf",github.com,cs.DS,cs.DS math.RA computer-science mathematics algorithms linear-algebra graph-theory history-of-mathematics slides github,Dominik Dold,2026,null,"Mathematics of Data Science: Motivation: assume the following type of data structure, Motivation. Slides (PDF) by Dominik Dold, 2026; 87 pages. From a repository described as: This is the material of the Master's lecture Mathematics of Data Science which I taught in 2025/2026 at the University of Vienna. Sections: Motivation: assume the following type of data structure; Motivation: recommending based on history; So. how can we do this?; The secret sauce of early Google: PageRank; The internet: a graph of pages linking to each other; PageRank: turning ranking to an eigenvector problem; Recommender systems; Representing our problem as a user-movie matrix; Finding vector embeddings for users and movies; Question!; First idea: spectral decomposition!; Isn’t this just dimensionality reduction?. Slides cover Users Movies, Using PageRank, we can recommend the most famous webpages!, How can we find such embeddings?!, A decomposition that always works is SVD!, This is lossy compression!, In that case, a factorization algorithm exists with a multiplicative update, εkj mj − λuk, Does that sound familiar? Any idea how we could achieve this?, DeepWalk is equivalent to factorizing the matrix, and TRjek."
  5662,"Mathematics of Data Science: Fitting data: the hypothesis set, Empirical risk minimization","https://github.com/dodo47/Mathematics-of-Data-Science-2025/blob/main/4%20-%20Function%20Approximation%20and%20Supervised%20Learning/Slides.pdf",github.com,math.ST,math.ST stat.ME mathematics statistics slides github,Dominik Dold,2026,null,"Mathematics of Data Science: Fitting data: the hypothesis set, Empirical risk minimization. Slides (PDF) by Dominik Dold, 2026; 24 pages. From a repository described as: This is the material of the Master's lecture Mathematics of Data Science which I taught in 2025/2026 at the University of Vienna. Sections: Fitting data: the hypothesis set; Empirical risk minimization; Why the supremum?; Example: linear regression; What about classification?; And. what now?!; Polynomial regression; Some example Legendre polynomials; Fitting data using polynomials; Results for different degrees; Why polynomials? Universal function approximation; Weierstrass approximation theorem. Slides cover So far, we did not discuss “labelled” data!, Hypothesis set and risk, What is our usual setup?, Callback to the random number lectures, R h ≤ sup, Karl Weierstrass, Marshall Harvey Stone, and Using Stone-Weierstrass, explain which of the following hypothesis sets are."
  5663,"Mathematics of Data Science: What is a neural network?, Sketch of an artificial neural network","https://github.com/dodo47/Mathematics-of-Data-Science-2025/blob/main/5%20-%20Deep%20Neural%20Networks/Slides.pdf",github.com,cs.LG,cs.LG stat.ML computer-science statistics machine-learning slides github,Dominik Dold,2026,null,"Mathematics of Data Science: What is a neural network?, Sketch of an artificial neural network. Slides (PDF) by Dominik Dold, 2026; 43 pages. From a repository described as: This is the material of the Master's lecture Mathematics of Data Science which I taught in 2025/2026 at the University of Vienna. Sections: What is a neural network?; Sketch of an artificial neural network; Question!; Warm-up: approximation properties of neural networks; Representing Lipschitz-continuous functions; The same holds for ReLU activation functions!; Final warm-up: going multivariate!; Are neural networks universal function approximators?; If shallow ANNs are universal, what about depth?; Intuitive picture: hierarchy of features; A new tool: affine pieces; Addition and composition of piecewise functions. Slides cover So please do not treat neural networks like a magic silver bullet!, Neural network definition, We define a single layer of a neural network as, Input Output, Why the non-linearity in each layer?, So, what can we represent with neural networks?, ReLU activation function φ and O, Reminder: shallow = 1 hidden layer (what we dealt with so far), Image credit: Yann LeCun, and Hence, ReLU neural networks are piecewise continuous functions!."
  5664,"Mathematics of Data Science: Return of the Hoeffding’s inequality, Learning bound: finite hypothesis set","https://github.com/dodo47/Mathematics-of-Data-Science-2025/blob/main/6%20-%20Generalization%20Bounds/Slides.pdf",github.com,math.PR,math.PR cs.DS mathematics computer-science concentration-of-measure formalization machine-learning slides github,Dominik Dold,2026,null,"Mathematics of Data Science: Return of the Hoeffding’s inequality, Learning bound: finite hypothesis set. Slides (PDF) by Dominik Dold, 2026; 20 pages. From a repository described as: This is the material of the Master's lecture Mathematics of Data Science which I taught in 2025/2026 at the University of Vienna. Sections: Return of the Hoeffding’s inequality; Learning bound: finite hypothesis set; PAC Learning; Problem: what happens in the limit of infinite hypotheses?; Covering number; Example; Learning bound: covering numbers; Covering numbers from Lipschitz constants; Finale: Lipschitz constant of ReLU neural networks; Outro: data science in the overparametrized regime; One explanation: the power of many, small parameters. Slides cover Reminder: Hoeffding’s inequality, Probably Approximately Correct, Let’s formalize this!, Using covering numbers, we get (using a rather similar derivation), Risk, and Expressivity of H."
  5665,"Mathematics of Data Science: Literature","https://github.com/dodo47/Mathematics-of-Data-Science-2025/blob/main/Literature.pdf",github.com,cs.LG,cs.LG math.PR stat.ML computer-science mathematics statistics machine-learning probability slides github,Dominik Dold,2026,null,"Mathematics of Data Science: Literature. Slides (PDF) by Dominik Dold, 2026; 7 pages. From a repository described as: This is the material of the Master's lecture Mathematics of Data Science which I taught in 2025/2026 at the University of Vienna. Sections: 1 – Foundations of Probability Theory; 2 – Analysing High-Dimensional Data; 3 – Learning from Structured Data; 4 – Function Approximation and Supervised Learning; 5 – Deep Neural Networks; 6 – Generalization Bounds."
