  6698,"JHU EN.601.449/EN.601.649: Computational Genomics: Applied Comparative Genomics, 02.sequencing (schatzlab/appliedgenomics2024)","https://github.com/schatzlab/appliedgenomics2024/blob/main/lectures/02.sequencing.pdf",github.com,q-bio.GN,q-bio.GN q-bio.QM quantitative-biology bioinformatics large-language-models lecture-notes slides github,Michael Schatz,2024,null,"JHU EN.601.449/EN.601.649: Computational Genomics: Applied Comparative Genomics, 02.sequencing (schatzlab/appliedgenomics2024). Lecture slides (PDF) by Michael Schatz, 2024; 55 pages. From a repository described as: The primary goal of the course is for students to be grounded in the fundamental theory and applications to leave the course empowered to conduct independent genomic analyses. We will study the leading computational and quantitative approaches for comparing and analyzing genomes starting from raw…. Slides cover Course Webpage, TA: Matthew Nguyen, Assignment 1, What is ChatGPT and Why Does it Work?, Central Dogma of Molecular Biology, Sequencing Capacity, Sensors & Metadata, Cost per Genome, 1x sequencing, 8x sequencing, Pop Quiz!, and K-mers and K-mer counting."
  6699,"JHU EN.601.449/EN.601.649: Computational Genomics: Applied Comparative Genomics, 03.assembly (schatzlab/appliedgenomics2024)","https://github.com/schatzlab/appliedgenomics2024/blob/main/lectures/03.assembly.pdf",github.com,q-bio.GN,q-bio.GN q-bio.QM quantitative-biology bioinformatics lecture-notes book slides github,MIchael Schatz,2024,null,"JHU EN.601.449/EN.601.649: Computational Genomics: Applied Comparative Genomics, 03.assembly (schatzlab/appliedgenomics2024). Lecture slides (PDF) by MIchael Schatz, 2024; 67 pages. From a repository described as: The primary goal of the course is for students to be grounded in the fundamental theory and applications to leave the course empowered to conduct independent genomic analyses. We will study the leading computational and quantitative approaches for comparing and analyzing genomes starting from raw…. Slides cover Assignment 1, TA: Matthew Nguyen, Part 1: Recap and Illumina Sequencing, 2x sequencing, Normal Approximation, K-mers and K-mer counting, Shredded Book Reconstruction, Contig N50, Pop Quiz 2, and Ingredients for a good assembly."
  6700,"JHU EN.601.449/EN.601.649: Computational Genomics: Applied Comparative Genomics, 04.wgaa","https://github.com/schatzlab/appliedgenomics2024/blob/main/lectures/04.wgaa.pdf",github.com,q-bio.GN,q-bio.GN q-bio.QM quantitative-biology bioinformatics functional-analysis graph-theory statistics lecture-notes slides github,Michael Schatz,2024,null,"JHU EN.601.449/EN.601.649: Computational Genomics: Applied Comparative Genomics, 04.wgaa. Lecture slides (PDF) by Michael Schatz, 2024; 52 pages. From a repository described as: The primary goal of the course is for students to be grounded in the fundamental theory and applications to leave the course empowered to conduct independent genomic analyses. We will study the leading computational and quantitative approaches for comparing and analyzing genomes starting from raw…. Slides cover Assignment 1, Assignment 2: Genome Assembly, Part 1: Recap, Poisson Distribution, Assembly Applications, Ingredients for a good assembly, The tally of k-mer counts in real, 2X coverage, Negative Binomial Distribution, Space-efficient and exact de Bruijn graph representation based on a Bloom filter, Repeats and Coverage Statistics, Scaffolding, and Whole Genome Alignment."
  6701,05.humangenome (schatzlab/appliedgenomics2024),"https://github.com/schatzlab/appliedgenomics2024/blob/main/lectures/05.humangenome.pdf",github.com,q-bio.GN,q-bio.GN q-bio.QM quantitative-biology bioinformatics history-of-mathematics slides github,MIchael Schatz,2024,null,"05.humangenome (schatzlab/appliedgenomics2024). Lecture slides (PDF) by MIchael Schatz, 2024; 51 pages. Part of JHU EN.601.449/EN.601.649: Computational Genomics: Applied Comparative Genomics. Slides cover Assignment 2: Genome Assembly, Assembly Summary, SV Types, History of the Human Genome Project, Initial sequencing and analysis of the human genome, Who is the reference human?, Assembly Complexity, “Corrective Lens” for Sequencing, PacBio Single, Oxford Nanopore Technologies (ONT), Nanopore Basecalling, and Two Paradigms for Assembly."
  6702,"JHU EN.601.449/EN.601.649: Computational Genomics: Applied Comparative Genomics, 06.mapping","https://github.com/schatzlab/appliedgenomics2024/blob/main/lectures/06.mapping.pdf",github.com,q-bio.GN,q-bio.GN q-bio.QM quantitative-biology bioinformatics ml-systems lecture-notes book slides github,MIchael Schatz,2024,null,"JHU EN.601.449/EN.601.649: Computational Genomics: Applied Comparative Genomics, 06.mapping. Lecture slides (PDF) by MIchael Schatz, 2024; 40 pages. From a repository described as: The primary goal of the course is for students to be grounded in the fundamental theory and applications to leave the course empowered to conduct independent genomic analyses. We will study the leading computational and quantitative approaches for comparing and analyzing genomes starting from raw…. Slides cover Assignment 2: Genome Assembly, Initial sequencing and analysis of the human genome, “HiFi” Circular Consensus Reads, Two Paradigms for Assembly, BGI CycloneSeq, Multi-pass, single-molecule nanopore reading of long protein strands, Personal Genomics, Brute Force Analysis, Suffix Arrays: Searching the Phone Book, Searching the Index, Binary Search Analysis, and Sapling: Accelerating Suffix Array Queries with."
