  7416,"JHU EN.601.449/EN.601.649: Computational Genomics: Applied Comparative Genomics, 16.rnaseq","https://github.com/schatzlab/appliedgenomics2023/blob/main/lectures/16.rnaseq.pdf",github.com,q-bio.GN,q-bio.GN q-bio.QM quantitative-biology bioinformatics computational-biology machine-learning lecture-notes survey slides github,MIchael Schatz,2023,null,"JHU EN.601.449/EN.601.649: Computational Genomics: Applied Comparative Genomics, 16.rnaseq. Lecture slides (PDF) by MIchael Schatz, 2023; 54 pages. From a repository described as: The primary goal of the course is for students to be grounded in theory and leave the course empowered to conduct independent genomic analyses. We will study the leading computational and quantitative approaches for comparing and analyzing genomes starting from raw sequencing data. Slides cover Project Proposal, Assignment 5, Clustering Refresher, Principle Components Analysis (PCA), Volcano Plot, ImageNet Classification with Deep Convolutional Neural Networks, Balrog: A universal protein model for prokaryotic gene prediction, The *Seq List (in chronological order), RNA-seq, RNA-seq Overview, and RNA-Seq Approaches."
  7417,"JHU EN.601.449/EN.601.649: Computational Genomics: Applied Comparative Genomics, 17.rnaseq.pt2","https://github.com/schatzlab/appliedgenomics2023/blob/main/lectures/17.rnaseq.pt2.pdf",github.com,q-bio.GN,q-bio.GN q-bio.QM quantitative-biology bioinformatics computational-biology lecture-notes survey slides github,MIchael Schatz,2023,null,"JHU EN.601.449/EN.601.649: Computational Genomics: Applied Comparative Genomics, 17.rnaseq.pt2. Lecture slides (PDF) by MIchael Schatz, 2023; 29 pages. From a repository described as: The primary goal of the course is for students to be grounded in theory and leave the course empowered to conduct independent genomic analyses. We will study the leading computational and quantitative approaches for comparing and analyzing genomes starting from raw sequencing data. Slides cover Project Proposal, Assignment 5, RNA-seq Overview, RNA-seq Challenges, RPKM, FPKM, TPM, Models for transcript quantification from RNA-seq, and Salmon: The ultimate."
  7418,"JHU EN.601.449/EN.601.649: Computational Genomics: Applied Comparative Genomics, 18.regulation","https://github.com/schatzlab/appliedgenomics2023/blob/main/lectures/18.regulation.pdf",github.com,q-bio.GN,q-bio.GN q-bio.QM quantitative-biology bioinformatics computational-biology lecture-notes survey slides github,MIchael Schatz,2023,null,"JHU EN.601.449/EN.601.649: Computational Genomics: Applied Comparative Genomics, 18.regulation. Lecture slides (PDF) by MIchael Schatz, 2023; 42 pages. From a repository described as: The primary goal of the course is for students to be grounded in theory and leave the course empowered to conduct independent genomic analyses. We will study the leading computational and quantitative approaches for comparing and analyzing genomes starting from raw sequencing data. Slides cover Upcoming events, Preliminary Report, RNA-seq Overview, Salmon: The ultimate, Human Evolution, The Strategy of the Genes, Epigenetic Modifications to DNA, Detecting DNA cytosine methylation using nanopore sequencing, Transcription Factors, ChIP-seq: TF Binding, ChIP-seq: Histone Modifications, and PeakSeq."
  7419,"JHU EN.601.449/EN.601.649: Computational Genomics: Applied Comparative Genomics, 19.review","https://github.com/schatzlab/appliedgenomics2023/blob/main/lectures/19.review.pdf",github.com,q-bio.GN,q-bio.GN q-bio.QM quantitative-biology bioinformatics lecture-notes slides github,MIchael Schatz,2023,null,"JHU EN.601.449/EN.601.649: Computational Genomics: Applied Comparative Genomics, 19.review. Lecture slides (PDF) by MIchael Schatz, 2023; 43 pages. From a repository described as: The primary goal of the course is for students to be grounded in theory and leave the course empowered to conduct independent genomic analyses. We will study the leading computational and quantitative approaches for comparing and analyzing genomes starting from raw sequencing data. Slides cover Upcoming events, Preliminary Report, Exam Topics, Bisulfite Conversion, Chromatin compaction model, HI-C: Mapping the folding of DNA, “Lamina-Associated Domains are the B compartment”, ENCODE Data Sets, Major Findings, Impact and Evidence of Selection, Many variants in ENCODE-regions, and ENCODE Studies."
